{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Yes", "line": "I ain’t gotta paper-chase, ’cause my paper come to me", "vault_lines": ["I ain't gotta paper-chase, 'cause my paper come to me"], "meaning": "He doesn’t even have to work to get money anymore, since he’s getting so much from investments and sponsors.", "annotation_id": "908890", "url": "https://genius.com/908890", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Yes", "line": "You can catch me ridin’ down on the fuckin’ Verrazano Ass out just like Diallo, Diablo Hi-Ho Silver, call that motherfucker Tonto Leave a smoke trail back in Jersey in my condo", "vault_lines": ["You can catch me ridin' down on the fuckin' Verrazano"], "meaning": "Coming up from New Jersey, you can get to Brooklyn by crossing the Verrazzano Bridge from Staten Island.\n\n\n\nNo idea what “Diallo, Diablo” is about.\n\n“Hi-Yo Silver” and Tonto are references to The Lone Ranger. Silver is the Lone Ranger’s horse, and Tonto is his Indian companion. The show’s intro mentions them both (emphasis added):\n\nA fiery horse with the speed of light, a cloud of dust and a hearty Hi-Yo Silver! The Lone Ranger! … With his faithful Indian companion Tonto, the daring and resourceful masked rider of the plains led the fight for law and order in the early western United States!", "annotation_id": "96331", "url": "https://genius.com/96331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Yes", "line": "Bedsheets so nice, bedbugs don’t bite", "vault_lines": ["Bedsheets so nice, bedbugs don't bite"], "meaning": "Bed bugs are too polite to bother Weezy (plus they love snuggling in the high thread count!)", "annotation_id": "91512", "url": "https://genius.com/91512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Yes", "line": "Red Viper slither through the streets like a red snake Red diamonds in my chain looking like a red snake", "vault_lines": ["Red Viper slither through the streets like a red snake"], "meaning": "Playing on the name of the car a Dodge “VIPER” which he will drive around the town with a.k.a slither the red chain (soo wooo) around his neck must also resemble a snake as well. o.k then voldemort.", "annotation_id": "58514", "url": "https://genius.com/58514", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Yes", "line": "You can run up like you’re Rambo But you better hit me, nigga, if you know what I know ’Cause I got some super-size guys that’ll hit you with a combo Pronto, you won’t see the morning, like Alonzo", "vault_lines": ["You can run up like you're Rambo"], "meaning": "John Rambo is the action hero in the Rambo franchise, portrayed by Sylvester Stallone in the movies:\n\n\n\nA play on words here, Pharrell plans to send men to beat you up and potentially kill you so you will not live to see the morning.\n\nLike Alonzo refers to the basketball player Alonzo Mourning.\n\n\n\nThe line may also refers to Alonzo Harris, portrayed by Denzel Washington from the movie Training Day whose character is killed before the morning.", "annotation_id": "58511", "url": "https://genius.com/58511", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Yes", "line": "Just kickin’ it with a Bruce Lee-esbian", "vault_lines": ["Just kickin' it with a Bruce Lee-esbian"], "meaning": "It sounds like ESPN, which could tie into the ‘kicking’ it reference.\n\nSo drizzy is referring to having game like ESPN, which helps pull Asian lesbo’s.\n\nJust kicking it with a Bruce Lee-esbian..(Asian lesbian)", "annotation_id": "51194", "url": "https://genius.com/51194", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Yes", "line": "Weezy F. Baby, and the F is for Phenomenal", "vault_lines": ["Weezy F. Baby, and the F is for Phenomenal"], "meaning": "Weezy F is one of the various monikers that Lil Wayne has coined during his rap career, and at the bottom of this page, you can read all the words Weezy has claimed it stands for.\n\nWayne’s manager Cortez Bryant said in a 2020 interview that Wayne wanted to remove this line and stop the mixtape from releasing when he realized “phenomenal” didn’t start with an “F”:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/ZXBWweeXZ74?t=1109\n\nThe word starts with ‘ph,’ but he said ‘f,’ right? He was like, ‘Oh shit, I gotta change that.’ We released it. We had an argument about that. He not understanding the concept about digital, and it’s gone now…We had pressed tons of CDs, but he didn’t realize he spelled it wrong, and I didn’t catch it. Wayne is a perfectionist about that, using his words and prepositions in [the] correct way. He might misspell something on purpose for lyrical exercise and wordplay, but he’s really precise about his wordplay. He was really down, pissed, ‘Fuck the whole mixtape’ just because of that one damn line.", "annotation_id": "37071", "url": "https://genius.com/37071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
