{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "Weezy Baby a gangster, no wankster", "vault_lines": ["Weezy Baby a gangster, no wankster"], "meaning": "A potential reference to the 50 Cent song ‘Wanksta’ off his debut album, Get Rich Or Die Tryin'.", "annotation_id": "37345092", "url": "https://genius.com/37345092", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "You do the math, it’s a horror While I be on the ave gettin’ off one nineteen for a soft one, uh", "vault_lines": ["You do the math, it's a horror"], "meaning": "You do the math, it’s a hard one\n\nWayne asks the listener to “do the math” to calculate the value of his stock of drugs.\n\n“It’s a hard one” is a double-entendre because (1) it describes the difficulty of the task Wayne is requesting the listener do: complex arithmetic; and (2) it specifies that the drug is crack cocaine (as opposed to powdered, or “soft,” cocaine) and should be valued accordingly.\n\nWhile I be on the ave, gettin' off one, nineteen for a soft one, uh\n\nWhile the listener is calculating the value of the “hard” cocaine, Wayne will be on the street “getting off one” (i.e., selling a set amount of drugs) and specifies that he will be receiving $19,000 for a kilogram of powdered cocaine, or \"a soft one.”", "annotation_id": "33507232", "url": "https://genius.com/33507232", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "If I catch you without my scratch, I’m stretchin’ you out", "vault_lines": ["If I catch you without my scratch, I'm stretchin' you out"], "meaning": "“Scratch” is a slang term for crack/cocaine. Stretching someone out means to kill or knock someone out, particularly in a manner that makes them they lay flat on the ground.\n\nIf Wayne catches you without his “scratch,” either as a user or a seller, he will stretch you out!", "annotation_id": "33507227", "url": "https://genius.com/33507227", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "I move it from an eight to a slab, from a slab to a quarter From a quarter to a half, from a half to a hard one, uh", "vault_lines": ["I move it from an eight to a slab, from a slab to a quarter"], "meaning": "I move it from an eighth to a slab, from a slab to a quarter\nFrom a quarter to a half, from a half to a whole one, uh\n\nWayne explains that he moves crack cocaine in varying weights, all the way up from eighths to a whole pound or kilo.", "annotation_id": "33507190", "url": "https://genius.com/33507190", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "I know how to cook, so I buy it soft I also sell it soft dependin’ the cost", "vault_lines": ["I know how to cook, so I buy it soft"], "meaning": "“Soft” here refers to cocaine in its powder form, in contrast to “crack,” which is made by dissolving powder cocaine in a mixture of water and either ammonia or baking soda. The mixture is boiled to separate out the solid, which is allowed to cool and dry, then cut up into small nuggets.\n\nTunechi claims to have the requisite “cooking” skills to produce crack cocaine from the powder he buys  for that purpose – though he’s also open to retailing the powder he’s picked up wholesale, if the price is right.", "annotation_id": "13941182", "url": "https://genius.com/13941182", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "And the block still a hell zone Get a helmet for you and your gal’s dome, I suggest (Ayy)", "vault_lines": ["And the block still a hell zone"], "meaning": "Reference to his first single, “Tha Block is Hot” which was also the title of his 1st album.", "annotation_id": "3923704", "url": "https://genius.com/3923704", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "Didn’t know Jam Master well, but I rocks with my shells on", "vault_lines": ["Didn't know Jam Master well, but I rocks with my shells on"], "meaning": "Jam Master Jay was an American musician and rapper. He was the DJ of the influential hip hop group Run–D.M.C; they are credited with breaking hip-hop into mainstream music. Jay died wearing a white pair of Adidas Superstars, or “shell-toes” – the basketball trainers immortalised in Run-DMC’s 1986 hit “My Adidas”; the same shoes Wayne uses to hustle.\n\nWeezy is still a big fan of Adidas shoes until this day, although not the classic ones.", "annotation_id": "3923685", "url": "https://genius.com/3923685", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "I’ll sell a brick out your house, I’ll sell a brick to a house, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I'll sell a brick out your house, I'll sell a brick to a house, nigga"], "meaning": "A play on the two meanings of “brick”, the former being a kilo of cocaine and the latter being the hard rectangles that hold a house up\n\n\n\nA house wouldn’t need any more bricks (if it was still standing) but Wayne’s just that good a hustler", "annotation_id": "2222419", "url": "https://genius.com/2222419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "She give me major face, I sung “Amazing Grace” I was once lost, but now I’m found", "vault_lines": ["She give me major face, I sung \"Amazing Grace\""], "meaning": "A reference to the lyrics of gospel song “Amazing Grace”\n\nI once was lost but now am found,\nWas blind, but now I see.", "annotation_id": "2172409", "url": "https://genius.com/2172409", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "I wasn’t born with no silver spoon But I can heat it in a spoon and pitch it in a balloon, woah", "vault_lines": ["I wasn't born with no silver spoon"], "meaning": "Being born with a “silver spoon” means growing up in a nice home, most likely fairly wealthy as all. Wayne, did not.\n\nWhen Wayne says “I can heat it in a spoon”, that is referring to a drug method which is called freebase.\n\n\n\nAfter Wayne heats up the drugs, he puts it in a little balloon so it’s easier to store and sell.", "annotation_id": "1765716", "url": "https://genius.com/1765716", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "If you catch me without the burner, burn me", "vault_lines": ["If you catch me without the burner, burn me"], "meaning": "The term “burner” is slang for a gun.\n\n\n\n(Example: Ab-Soul mentioning a burner in this interview.)\n\nIn this context, burning Wayne would refer to killing him. So, if he isn’t prepared for any warfare (catch him without the burner), then he should be punished by dying due to someone with a “burner”.\n\nSomewhat like the Dr. Dre line\n\nNever let me slip, cause if I slip then I’m slipping\n\nFrom Nuthin' But A G Thang.", "annotation_id": "1765692", "url": "https://genius.com/1765692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who Wanna", "line": "Lights off, young Teddy Pender’ is out", "vault_lines": ["Lights off, young Teddy Pender' is out"], "meaning": "Teddy Pender an American R&B/soul singer and songwriter had a song called Turn the lights off.\nSo weezy is saying “lights off by teddy pender is out now.”", "annotation_id": "514072", "url": "https://genius.com/514072", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
