{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who", "line": "My bitch ain’t Brazilian But I could bet she look way better than Miss Brazil", "vault_lines": ["But I could bet she look way better than Miss Brazil"], "meaning": "Miss Brazil is a Brazilian national beauty pageant, held annually since 1954. The pageant is held with the aim of selecting the Brazillian representaive for Miss Universe. As of 2025, Miss Brazil is Gabriela Lacerda. As you can see from below, she’s hella bad so if Wayne’s girl really looks ‘way better’ than her, he’s not lying when he says ‘he kicks it with dimes’", "annotation_id": "36499164", "url": "https://genius.com/36499164", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who", "line": "I David Beckham with dimes That mean I kick it with bad ones", "vault_lines": ["I David Beckham with dimes"], "meaning": "David Beckham was an English professional footballer. He played primarily as a right-midfielder and is best known for his time with British club Manchester United.\n\nTo ‘kick it’ means to chill or hang out. Wayne here compares himself to the legendary soccer player Beckham, who was known for being able to kick the ball and curve it extremely well from a free-kick.\n\nBeckham played in the MLS (Major League Soccer, the American soccer league) for LA Galaxy from 2007 to 2012, bringing lots of attention to the league.", "annotation_id": "36499122", "url": "https://genius.com/36499122", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Who", "status": "done", "targets": 3, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-who-lyrics", "genius_title": "Who", "genius_id": 4042833, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Gold chains and a chopper like the A-Team", "vault_lines": ["Gold chains and a chopper like the A-Team"], "meaning": "The A-Team is a popular show from the ‘80s. They were the best at what they did which was completing the mission, whatever the mission may be.\n\n“Gold chains” is a reference to Mr. T who famously wore many gold necklaces and rocked a mohawk.\n\n\n\n“Chopper” is a reference to Murdock in the show who was the pilot. More often than not he was piloting a helicopter. It can also be a reference to guns aka choppers.", "annotation_id": "24271034", "url": "https://genius.com/24271034", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Please have my money on time Please have my money on time, boy ’Cause all of my niggas ex-cons And we’ll go right back like that’s fine, boy", "vault_lines": ["Please have my money on time"], "meaning": "Wayne doesn’t want to commit the crime, but all his boys have been to jail and have no problem going back for a murder charge. His ‘niggas’ are spine, and have strong ideals and strong ways of stating those ideal.\n\nAs a matter of fact one of Lil Wayne’s latest signed artists, Flow, has been back to jail days before the album dropped, a for second-degree murder.", "annotation_id": "18029211", "url": "https://genius.com/18029211", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Please have my money on time Please have my money on time, boy ’Cause all of my niggas ex-cons And we’ll go right back like that’s fine, boy", "vault_lines": ["Please have my money on time"], "meaning": "Wayne doesn’t want to commit the crime, but all his boys have been to jail and have no problem going back for a murder charge. His ‘niggas’ are spine, and have strong ideals and strong ways of stating those ideal.\n\nAs a matter of fact one of Lil Wayne’s latest signed artists, Flow, has been back to jail days before the album dropped, a for second-degree murder.", "annotation_id": "18029209", "url": "https://genius.com/18029209", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Hey, fourty-six for the twenty-eight (Eight) Fourteen for the eighteen (’Teen)", "vault_lines": ["Hey, fourty-six for the twenty-eight (Eight)"], "meaning": "36 ounces is 1 kilogram.  Current market price in Jeezy’s area is $28,000/kilogram of cocaine.\n\n18 ounces is 0.5 kilogram.  Jeezy lets those go for $14,000.\n\nhttp://imgur.com/gallery/4htpk", "annotation_id": "10569985", "url": "https://genius.com/10569985", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "And you know the flow hard, nigga, cooked crack (Woo) You mean the flow hard like cooked crack? (Crack) Damn right, ’cause I used to cook crack (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["And you know the flow hard, nigga, cooked crack (Woo)"], "meaning": "Jeezy has always infused his lyrics with his black market distributions experience.\n\nWhen someone takes cocaine, and ‘cooks’ it up into crack – the end result is a hard crack rock, therefore crack rocks are called ‘hard’, as opposed to ‘soft’ powdery uncooked cocaine.\n\nSince Jeezy has had plenty of experience with crack rocks/‘hard’, some of that hardness has rubbed off on his vocal delivery and lyrical content, so Jeezy’s saying that his rap flow is as hard (badass) as the hard crack rocks he used to cook and sell.", "annotation_id": "7961377", "url": "https://genius.com/7961377", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "I be creepin’ like the shadow and the sun, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I be creepin' like the shadow and the sun, nigga"], "meaning": "When you see a shadow it appears motionless, but after time the shadow slowly moves, almost without notice it is in a different position.\n\nThis plays into the double edged sword of “by the gavel or the gun nigga”, shadows move without anybody’s notice, so it could depict cocaine distribution, or murdering someone mysteriously in the daylight.\n\nBitch, real g’s move in silence like lasagna", "annotation_id": "7328523", "url": "https://genius.com/7328523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Woo, pourin’ Pink Panther in the punch, nigga Think faster than these dumb niggas", "vault_lines": ["Woo, pourin' Pink Panther in the punch, nigga"], "meaning": "The Pink Panther is an animated character, who generally remained silent, speaking only in two theatrical shorts. Punch is the term for a wide assortment of drinks, both non-alcoholic and alcoholic, generally containing fruit or fruit juice.\n\nLil Wayne depicts codeine as Pink Panther, for their color similarity. He’s basically will pour cough syrup in a tasteful fruit juice, so it can lose its bitter taste.\n\nAlthough the usage of codeine would make him slow down, Tunechi still thinks faster than other rappers, and put his lyrics together perfectly with outstanding punchlines and metaphors.", "annotation_id": "7229303", "url": "https://genius.com/7229303", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Ooh, I’m Mick Jagger as a young nigga", "vault_lines": ["Ooh, I'm Mick Jagger as a young nigga"], "meaning": "Reference to Mick Jagger, who has written some of the most recognizable rock anthems of the last fifty years. Songs that are the staple of every covers band in the world; songs that have been performed by just about everyone who is singing or playing in a band.\n\nLil Wayne sees himself as successful, and recognizable as the lead singer of The Rolling Stones. The only difference between them is 39 years.", "annotation_id": "7229217", "url": "https://genius.com/7229217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Please back it up one more time, girl You act shady and I’ma put this dick Where the sun don’t shine, girl", "vault_lines": ["Please back it up one more time, girl"], "meaning": "When children misbehave, adults will threaten them with, “I’ll put my foot where the sun don’t shine.” This refers to their behind.\n\nWayne uses the phrase in a similar way, but plays off the act of being “shady”. Ultimately, bad attitude from his girl and Weezy will punish her by putting his penis into her anus.", "annotation_id": "7221918", "url": "https://genius.com/7221918", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Only bird that your flippin’, nigga Is the ones in the studio (Haha)", "vault_lines": ["Only bird that your flippin', nigga"], "meaning": "To “flip birds” means to sell cocaine as “bird” is slang for a brick of cocaine. The birds you’re flipping are only imaginary, which makes you a “studio gangster.”", "annotation_id": "7220803", "url": "https://genius.com/7220803", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "We serial killers, you can get your Captain Crunch, nigga", "vault_lines": ["We serial killers, you can get your Captain Crunch, nigga"], "meaning": "Tunechi and Jeezy are mass murderers, they kill beats, pussies, and snitches. It’s also a play on the homophone serial/cereal with Cap'n Crunch being a cereal brand.", "annotation_id": "7220784", "url": "https://genius.com/7220784", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Girl (Girl), I got that white girl (Woo), girl (Girl, yeah) Girl (Woo), I got that white (Girl) girl (Yeah) I got that white (Wassup?) That blonde hair, blue eyes (Yeah; eyes) I’m sellin’, I’m sellin’, hurry up and buy (Hurry up and buy, nigga) Hurry up and buy (Ah)", "vault_lines": ["Girl (Girl), I got that white girl (Woo), girl (Girl, yeah)"], "meaning": "Tunechi claims to be selling cocaine aka “white girl”. As he describes “her” looks, he most likely compares the pureness of his cocaine to the Nazi ideology of being aryan, racial pure.\n\nNas said on “Triple Beam Dreams”:\n\nWhere I’m from ain’t far from Washington Heights to cop Aryan\n\nThe verse ends in a double entendre – he says “I’m selling her, re-up and buy” as in he sells the white girl, profits, and buys some more off it\nThen he repeats it, saying “hurry up and buy” cause he needs that re-up money buy some more to flip.", "annotation_id": "7220754", "url": "https://genius.com/7220754", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "That Britney, that Iggy Azalea, I tell ya", "vault_lines": ["That Britney, that Iggy Azalea, I tell ya"], "meaning": "Jeezy uses the old hip hop meme of “white girl”, which is a code word for cocaine. Both Iggy Azalea and Britney Spears are white.\n\n\n\nThe reason he chose two of these artists in particular is because Iggy and Britney recently dropped a song together.", "annotation_id": "7220718", "url": "https://genius.com/7220718", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "It’s twenty-two five for the B Eleven five for the Solange, yeah", "vault_lines": ["It's twenty-two five for the B"], "meaning": "A kilogram of cocaine is valued at around 22 thousand dollars. Beyoncé Knowles, or “B”, and Solange Knowles are sisters, with Queen Bey being the older, richer, and more popular of the two. Tunechi can flip a kilo for $22,500 for the whole brick (“for the B”) and $11,500 for the “Solange,” or half a brick, metaphorically comparing how Solange is only a part of the whole that is Beyoncé.", "annotation_id": "7220254", "url": "https://genius.com/7220254", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "My plug name is Hector But back in his country, they call him Héctòr", "vault_lines": ["My plug name is Hector"], "meaning": "Wayne’s dealer, Hector, hails from Mexico. The pronunciation of his name is different in Mexico than in the United States, as there’s inflection on “tor” in Spanish.", "annotation_id": "7220113", "url": "https://genius.com/7220113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Trap house jump out the gym Trap house jump like LeBron, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Trap house jump out the gym"], "meaning": "Wayne’s crack house is poppin' with customers – the ideal scenario for any dealer. Instead of a busy crack house, LeBron James has some of the dopest hops in basketball.\n\n\n\nThis line alludes to Rich The Kid and Migos' song “Jumpin' Like Jordan,” which he remixed in the past.", "annotation_id": "7219421", "url": "https://genius.com/7219421", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "White Girl", "line": "Call a sixteen Montana, a eight a Steve Young And a four a Brett Favre", "vault_lines": ["And a four a Brett Favre"], "meaning": "Quarterbacks Joe Montana, Steve Young, and Brett Favre wore uniform numbers #16/#8/#4, respectively, throughout their NFL careers. Wayne combines their uniform numbers with firearm gauges.\n\n\n\n(Clockwise from left: Favre, Rodgers, Young, Montana.)", "annotation_id": "7219389", "url": "https://genius.com/7219389", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
