{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It Like a Slave", "line": "I whip it like Penny got it on Good Times", "vault_lines": ["I whip it like Penny got it on Good Times"], "meaning": "Penny was a character on 1970s sitcom, played by Janet Jackson, who also got hit by her father", "annotation_id": "23329", "url": "https://genius.com/23329", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It Like a Slave", "line": "I whip it like Joe Jackson used to whip Mike", "vault_lines": ["I whip it like Joe Jackson used to whip Mike"], "meaning": "Joe Jackson was Michael Jackson’s father. In a famous interview with Oprah in 1993, Michael said he abused him emotionally and also whipped him with a belt", "annotation_id": "23328", "url": "https://genius.com/23328", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It Like a Slave", "line": "Snow white, no dwarfs, just snow flakes", "vault_lines": ["Snow white, no dwarfs, just snow flakes"], "meaning": "His cocaine is pure, so it’s “snow white” instead of the more common off-white or yellowish color coke takes on when cut with other substances\n\nReference to the Brother Grimm fairytale, Snow White and the Seven Dwarves", "annotation_id": "23322", "url": "https://genius.com/23322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It Like a Slave", "line": "Let ’em out the cage, grilled chick filets", "vault_lines": ["Let 'em out the cage, grilled chick filets"], "meaning": "“Chickens” are kilos of cocaine, so he’s cooking entire kilos of coke", "annotation_id": "23321", "url": "https://genius.com/23321", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It Like a Slave", "line": "Check my kitchen every spoon in there bent", "vault_lines": ["Check my kitchen every spoon in there bent"], "meaning": "He should be more careful with his silverware. My parents, for example, still have the place settings they received for their wedding. And they’ve been married 30 years!\n\n(He is saying they are bent from smoking crack / melting)", "annotation_id": "23178", "url": "https://genius.com/23178", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It Like a Slave", "line": "I take a brick karate chop it like a sensei", "vault_lines": ["I take a brick karate chop it like a sensei"], "meaning": "He takes a brick of crack and breaks it down into smaller doses to sell on the street, much like karate experts (“sensei” is the Japanese word for master and used in their martial arts systems to denote the bosses) chop bricks in half with their fists", "annotation_id": "23173", "url": "https://genius.com/23173", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It Like a Slave", "line": "I’m talking sugar talking dough like a beignet", "vault_lines": ["I'm talking sugar talking dough like a beignet"], "meaning": "“Sugar” here means both coke and eloquence. “Dough” means money. A beignet is a French sugary treat made out of dough and popular in New Orleans because of the Cajun population", "annotation_id": "23169", "url": "https://genius.com/23169", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It Like a Slave", "line": "Then I whip it like Kunta Kinte", "vault_lines": ["Then I whip it like Kunta Kinte"], "meaning": "Kunta Kinte was a slave in the 1970s TV series Roots, based on a novel. As a slave; he got whipped a lot\n\nHere’s the famous scene: I bet they watch this on repeat at the Aryan Brotherhood", "annotation_id": "23168", "url": "https://genius.com/23168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
