{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "Now watch me and my chain gang", "vault_lines": ["Now watch me and my chain gang"], "meaning": "This line is actually very smart, this is because he is talking about whipping his “work,” like a slave. Now he says watch me and my chain gang, which is the name of a painting by an African American artist named William H. Johnson and the painting “Chain Gang,” is a painting about slaves.", "annotation_id": "11497652", "url": "https://genius.com/11497652", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "I was ballin’ in New Orleans way before the Charlotte Hornets", "vault_lines": ["I was ballin' in New Orleans way before the Charlotte Hornets"], "meaning": "1) In this line Wayne is saying that he was getting money way before the Charlotte hornets (A basketball team hence; balling) which were only founded in 1988, while Wayne was born in 1982.\n2) The Charlotte hornets were previously called the Charlotte Bobcats until 2014, and Wayne was getting money well before 2014.\n3) The New Orleans Pelicans used to be called the New Orleans hornets; and Wayne was balling before all of this changed, and since Wayne is from New Orleans he knew all of this.", "annotation_id": "11497049", "url": "https://genius.com/11497049", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "I’ma kill ’em when I drop like I’m holding a baby", "vault_lines": ["I'ma kill 'em when I drop like I'm holding a baby"], "meaning": "When Wayne drops his music he is going to kill the competition. He talking about when you are to drop a baby or fall with a baby in your arms, chances are you will kill the baby also.", "annotation_id": "11497043", "url": "https://genius.com/11497043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "And I roll with my riders like it’s a harley bar And we roll with them choppers like it’s a harley party", "vault_lines": ["And I roll with my riders like it's a harley bar"], "meaning": "1) A Harley bar is a bar that has motorcycle seats or is sponsored by Harley Davidson;\n\n\n\n2) When he says “I roll with my riders” it is a double-entendre because he mentions Harley which is a type of motorcycle hence; “riders,” and motorcycle gangs usually ride Harley Davidson’s and carry guns/choppers but motorcylcles are also called choppers.", "annotation_id": "11496980", "url": "https://genius.com/11496980", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "And from the top, everybody look 1'9", "vault_lines": ["And from the top, everybody look 1'9"], "meaning": "When you are at the top/peak or in Wayne’s case at the the top of the rap game, looking down makes everyone look smaller. Hence; “everybody look 1'9,” as in height.", "annotation_id": "11496960", "url": "https://genius.com/11496960", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "But I ate a plate of loot for dinner", "vault_lines": ["But I ate a plate of loot for dinner"], "meaning": "He ate a plate of loot or treasure, which are two ways of saying the same thing. This can also be a reference at the diamonds in Lil Wayne’s teeth.", "annotation_id": "11496937", "url": "https://genius.com/11496937", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "Weezy F, the F for fury Hang 12 witnesses That’s what I call a hung jury", "vault_lines": ["Weezy F, the F for fury"], "meaning": "Ample means enough or a lot of; Weezy brings a lot of fury. When he hangs 12 witnesses literally, that is what he calls a hung jury. Its a play on words because he is taking the hung in hung jury literally when in actuality a hung jury is a jury that cannot come to a decision.", "annotation_id": "11496920", "url": "https://genius.com/11496920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "Bricks get shipped, bricks get cut Dr. Carter, nip and tuck", "vault_lines": ["Bricks get shipped, bricks get cut"], "meaning": "Wayne’s bricks are packaged nicely, nipped and tucked the way a plastic surgeon would nip and tuck their patient while doing something crazy like a facelift.\n\nYou know Wayne’s already been willing to engrave diamonds in his teeth, so who know what procedures he’ll go under the knife for when he’s old and everything’s sagging and/or wrinkled.", "annotation_id": "6580910", "url": "https://genius.com/6580910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "Weezy F, the F is for “Don’t forget the baby”", "vault_lines": ["Weezy F, the F is for \"Don't forget the baby\""], "meaning": "Wayne makes the “F” stand for a Bunche shit…actually there’s a song where he literally makes it stand for “a buncha shit.”: https://genius.com/37768\n\nMy other favorite “F” meaning is PHENOMENAL. (and yes I 100% believe he knew it started with “ph”, that’s what makes it funny. the guys DOES have a college degree you know)", "annotation_id": "6580826", "url": "https://genius.com/6580826", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "I’m in the garden selling fruit to sinners Like apple’s to Shaq", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the garden selling fruit to sinners"], "meaning": "Whilst in the Garden of Eden, Adam and Eve were tempted by the forbidden fruit, and thus sin was created. Wayne’s serving up the drug of choice to his sinful clientele.\n\nShaquille O'Neal played Center, which sounds similar to “sinner.”  O'Neal was never tempted by money to play for a team from New York (the Big Apple), but Wayne thinks his product is good enough to entice him to switch allegiances.", "annotation_id": "6580721", "url": "https://genius.com/6580721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "Kunta Kinte on my shit, nigga Like I ate a plate of roots for dinner", "vault_lines": ["Kunta Kinte on my shit, nigga"], "meaning": "Kunta Kinte was the main character in the 70s TV mini-series “Roots”, which examined slave trading to, and slavery in, America. When Kunta refused to accept his given “white” name, they whipped him viciously until he gave in. So since Wayne keeps talking about “whipping” up drugs, he felt the words had a connection.\n\nYeah we’ll file this in the “tasteless” folder of Wayne’s rhyme catalog.\n\nAnd it’s not the only time he’s used the Kunta Kinte character as a reference:https://genius.com/23168\n\nAnd also here, brilliantly uses the Kunta Kinte as a positive reference:\n\n“I’m Notorious/I’m Tupac in Juice/I’m the dude from off Reading rainbow, but in roots”\n\n-“A Milli Freemix”", "annotation_id": "6579988", "url": "https://genius.com/6579988", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "Weezy at the plate, I could bunt it out of here Safe as a muhfucka", "vault_lines": ["Where the safe, muhfucka?"], "meaning": "In baseball, a but is the type of hit that purposely doesn’t go very far: it’s many to catch the defending team off guard and advance the runners already on base.\n\nSo Weezy claiming he could bunt a home run means he’s REALLY strong or just really high.\n\nHe actually uses the idea of bunting in its traditional sense here:https://genius.com/6579892", "annotation_id": "6579804", "url": "https://genius.com/6579804", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "Boy, you should catch up It must of been mine, theys", "vault_lines": ["Beat it up, beat it"], "meaning": "Wayne must be really hungry because he just  doused us in lyrical condiments:\n\nKetchup = catch up\n\nMustard = Must of\n\nMayonnaise = Mine, theys", "annotation_id": "3279910", "url": "https://genius.com/3279910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "Ain’t no safety button on this muhfucka", "vault_lines": ["Ain't no safety button on this muhfucka"], "meaning": "Most likely referring to a Glock, which would not have an external saftey.", "annotation_id": "2488157", "url": "https://genius.com/2488157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Whip It", "line": "Two grand still get you four and a baby", "vault_lines": ["Two grand still get you four and a baby"], "meaning": "Double Entendre!\n\n\nReferring Brenda’s Baby from the previous line\nAs Jeezy has said many times, $2000 will buy you 4.5 ounces of cocaine", "annotation_id": "2488134", "url": "https://genius.com/2488134", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
