{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to the Concrete Jungle", "line": "Let’s see: B-E-N-Z, A-M-G", "vault_lines": ["Let's see: B-E-N-Z, A-M-G"], "meaning": "Wayne spells out Benz AMG, which is the performance model from Mercedes-Benz (similar to M from BMW and R from Audi). Wayne owns a 2010-ish black Mercedes-Benz AMG GT", "annotation_id": "20015665", "url": "https://genius.com/20015665", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to the Concrete Jungle", "line": "Welcome to the concrete jungle Got snakes, got bears, got lions, no muzzles (No muzzles) And they all tryin to cause me trouble Got the big trey-8, long nose, no muzzle (Gangsta Gri-Zillz!) Hold up, hold up!", "vault_lines": ["Got snakes, got bears, got lions, no muzzles (No muzzles)"], "meaning": "An attempt to play on different senses of “muzzle”:\n\n\nThe kind of device placed over the snout of an animal (such as a bear or lion) to prevent it from biting\nThe open end of the barrel of a gun\n\n\n\nJuelz’s .38 certainly has an opening at the end of its long barrel, but there’s nothing to “muzzle” its “bite” for anyone who doesn’t back off him.", "annotation_id": "14424380", "url": "https://genius.com/14424380", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to the Concrete Jungle", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-welcome-to-the-concrete-jungle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Welcome to the Concrete Jungle", "genius_id": 213885, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Young Money", "vault_lines": ["Young Money, we're rolling"], "meaning": "Young Money is Lil Wayne’s label.", "annotation_id": "37944748", "url": "https://genius.com/37944748", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "And I’m higher than the peak of Everest)", "vault_lines": ["Young Money, we're rolling"], "meaning": "Mount Everest is the highest mountain above sea level in the world.\n\nGiven the lines that proceed it, Weezy is likely talking about his unparalleled stature, that he’s elevated to an uncontested level, one that nobody’s above.\n\nIt could also denote an immense state of euphoria from being drug-induced, as a way of saying his high is so strong that it’s unfathomable to most.", "annotation_id": "36074264", "url": "https://genius.com/36074264", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Sit your five-dollar ass down, do not make me Turn your five-dollar ass to coupons, baby", "vault_lines": ["Sit your five-dollar ass down, do not make me"], "meaning": "“Sit your five-dollar ass down” is a reference to the movie New Jack City, where Nino Brown says, “Sit your five-dollar ass down before I make change.” Basically, it’s a way of putting someone in their place. Wayne then is saying not only are you cheap, but I’ll make you even cheaper like turning a small bill into a discount code.", "annotation_id": "36073790", "url": "https://genius.com/36073790", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "I’m higher than your tube socks stretch", "vault_lines": [", I'm higher than your tube socks stretch"], "meaning": "Wayne uses a clever visual metaphor here. Tube socks, which typically stretch up to the calf or knee, represent a standard of “high.” By saying he’s higher than even those socks reach, he’s saying just how elevated he is, either from drugs (faded) or from success and skill (talent).", "annotation_id": "36073674", "url": "https://genius.com/36073674", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "[Chorus: Angel Aponte & Lil Wayne] Welcome to Tha Carter (Welcome to Tha Carter, Young Mulah, baby) Where the mountains align (Welcome to Tha Carter) Where the strong survive (Welcome to Tha Carter, six) Welcome to Tha Carter, where dreams never die, oh (Six, six, bitch)", "vault_lines": ["Welcome to Tha Carter, where dreams never die, oh (Six, six, bitch)"], "meaning": "The chorus is officially credited to Angel Aponte, also known as ONHEL. She’s a longtime collaborator and producer for Wayne.\n\nDespite the official credits, some fans speculate the chorus might be AI-generated because of its polished, vintage soul sound, reminiscent of other AI-produced tracks. However, there are no statements from Lil Wayne or Angel Aponte confirming AI use, so it remains just a fan theory.\n\nWhile the AI debate reflects how modern music blurs lines between human and artificial, all evidence (credits, Aponte’s history, and no AI confirmation) points to her as the true voice behind the chorus.", "annotation_id": "35978803", "url": "https://genius.com/35978803", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "[Chorus: Angel Aponte] Welcome to Tha Carter (Welcome to Tha Carter) Where the mountains align (Welcome to Tha Carter) Where the strong survive (Welcome to Tha Carter) Welcome to Tha Carter, where dreams never die, oh, hey", "vault_lines": ["Welcome to Tha Carter, where dreams never die, oh (Six, six, bitch)"], "meaning": "The chorus is officially credited to Angel Aponte, also known as ONHEL. She’s a longtime collaborator and producer for Wayne.\n\nDespite the official credits, some fans speculate the chorus might be AI-generated because of its polished, vintage soul sound, reminiscent of other AI-produced tracks. However, there are no statements from Lil Wayne or Angel Aponte confirming AI use, so it remains just a fan theory.\n\nWhile the AI debate reflects how modern music blurs lines between human and artificial, all evidence (credits, Aponte’s history, and no AI confirmation) points to her as the true voice behind the chorus.", "annotation_id": "35978799", "url": "https://genius.com/35978799", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "I’ma stand behind her and rapid fire", "vault_lines": ["I'ma stand behind her and rapid fire"], "meaning": "Weezy delivers a double entendre packed with heat: “stand behind her and rapid fire” works as both a sexual punchline and a street-coded metaphor for protection and aggression.", "annotation_id": "35978735", "url": "https://genius.com/35978735", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "I scramble and swerve, scam and I serve, planned it out first", "vault_lines": ["I scramble and swerve, scam and I serve, planned it out first"], "meaning": "Wayne paints a picture of organized hustle, balancing movement (scramble and swerve), grind (scam and serve), and strategy (planned it out first). He shows how success, whether in the streets or the studio, is part instinct, part blueprint, keeping with the short “a” vowel sound /æ/ followed by repeated “ɝ” sounds (swerve, serve, first); makin' the whole line flow like a downhill drift.\n\nThat consistency adds to the feel of speed, rhythm, and control, like he swervin’ but never slippin’.", "annotation_id": "35978678", "url": "https://genius.com/35978678", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Man of my word, I stand on my word", "vault_lines": ["Man of my word, I stand on my word"], "meaning": "Fans who recall delays with Tha Carter V will recognize this as a full-circle moment: this time, when Wayne said June 6, he stood on it.\n\nAlso, this bar sets the tone for the verse but also the rhyme scheme he commited to: The first rhyme pocket leans on the short “a” vowel sound /æ/ in “man”, “stand”, and “land”, before transitioning into a longer, sustained rhyme sequence based on the rhotacized vowel /ɝ/ — as heard in “word”, “bird”, “deserve”, etc.\n\nKnown for his sharp one-liners and consistent rhyme craftsmanship, Weezy showcases his ability to build complex flows off subtle phonetic pivots.", "annotation_id": "35978645", "url": "https://genius.com/35978645", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "[Verse 3: Lil Wayne]", "vault_lines": ["Young Money, we're rolling"], "meaning": "A showcase of the external and internal rhymes of this third verse, as well as the last few lines in the final chorus.", "annotation_id": "35966529", "url": "https://genius.com/35966529", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "I slurs", "vault_lines": ["Young Money, we're rolling"], "meaning": "Off the xanny, wayne tends to slur his speech", "annotation_id": "35870214", "url": "https://genius.com/35870214", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "My standards superb", "vault_lines": [", my standards superb"], "meaning": "Not on this album!!", "annotation_id": "35870207", "url": "https://genius.com/35870207", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "[Verse 1: Lil Wayne]", "vault_lines": ["Young Money, we're rolling"], "meaning": "Arguably one of the best verses on the whole album. The rhyme scheme to this verse is incredible!\n\nhttps://youtu.be/BrtEKWJCMBM?si=uybeyj1y3CfFl7Sa", "annotation_id": "35870204", "url": "https://genius.com/35870204", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "[Pre-Chorus: Angel Aponte] Young Money, we’re rolling It’s the only way In the hustle of the streets We make our play [Chorus: Angel Aponte & Lil Wayne] Welcome to Tha Carter (Welcome to Tha Carter) Where the mountains align (Welcome to Tha Carter, yeah) Where the strong survive (Welcome to Tha Carter) Welcome to Tha Carter, where dreams never die, oh, hey", "vault_lines": ["Young Money, we're rolling"], "meaning": "Whether the woman singing here is AI or not is currently uncertain. Obviously this isn’t an authentic classic soul sample as we hear the woman say, ‘Young Money’ and ‘Welcome to tha Carter’‘, but people have also said that the producer 'ONHEL’ who also goes by the persona Angel Aponte when he’s singing, performs this hook. If AI was used on the track, however, that’s pretty fucked up and it just shows the sad direction in which modern music is headed.", "annotation_id": "35870099", "url": "https://genius.com/35870099", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "But this is where the peace never rests", "vault_lines": ["But this is where the peace never rests"], "meaning": "This is a double entendre utilizing the homophones ‘piece’ and ‘peace.’ Weezy implies his gun (piece) is always active which ironically contrasts with the idea of having peace.", "annotation_id": "35842135", "url": "https://genius.com/35842135", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Time and place, here and now, I’m bearin’ down Got you starin’ down a barrel, baow-baow-baow-baow That’s how the ammo sound It’s Tha Carter VI, pussy, pull your tampon out, huh", "vault_lines": ["Time and place, here and now, I'm bearin' down"], "meaning": "‘Bearing down’ is the phrase of University of Arizona’s Wildcats. This implies he’s a wild cat capable of getting you to stare down the barrel of a gun. In Wayne fashion, he sprinkles a bit more on top as ‘baow-baow’ is a more aggressive version of ‘meow meow’ and supposed to represent the sound of a gun. The final line mentions ‘pussy’ (pussycat) and calls out people acting like a ‘bitch’ with a tampon in that he’s disgusted by (gags at the end). To top it all off, it’s also a double entendre about going down on a woman. He’s bearing down on her and she’s staring down at him delivering shots with his tongue in her pussy. Only to realize she has a tampon in and he gags.", "annotation_id": "35831039", "url": "https://genius.com/35831039", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Welcome to Tha Carter VI and I’m servin’ my guests", "vault_lines": ["Welcome to Tha Carter VI and I'm servin' my guests"], "meaning": "This song is basically the post intro for the Carter6,Dwayne uses these lyrics to welcome a listener to the song just as a customer at a shop ,the bar continues to “Her Lip Service the Best”  referencing lip service which is to support something but do nothing about it but can also be seen as a lip service as the “service” is the word used in hotels /shops as in “room services” meaning the girl’s way of talking was the best in the carter v (shop/house)\n\n[The lip service is also the British Show  that aired in BBC 3  in 2010 but was later cancelled ,some reasons they had Less viewers than the” L WORLD” which was another show  in US that had same content(showing lesbians life style) and notice how wayne was talking about smoking  on a pure L’S first verse which contradicts to this as wayne’s viewing himself as a victorious person.]\n\nThe word Lip service is also used as a Blowjob during sex as  after the line wayne says a bitch was her guest in her Carter house/hotel  in these lines 👇🏽\n\n“Im serving my guests ,i guess your bitch is my guests “\n\nWayne continues to do a wordplay with guests and guess as the hotel usually having guests and the a bitch was the Carte6 House guest", "annotation_id": "35828182", "url": "https://genius.com/35828182", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "My flow like the flu I catch", "vault_lines": ["My flow like the flu I catch"], "meaning": "His flow is coldddd!", "annotation_id": "35824393", "url": "https://genius.com/35824393", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Pray for cloudy weather, world peace, shout out to Metta", "vault_lines": ["Pray for cloudy weather, world peace, shout out to Metta"], "meaning": "This lyric is all about confidence and staying power. When he says, “my sun’s gon’ shine forever,” he means his success, energy, or presence isn’t going anywhere. Telling others to “get your clouds together” is a clever way of saying, “you can try to block my shine, but it won’t work.”\n\nThe last line adds humor and a smart reference. “Pray for cloudy weather” is a jab at people hoping he’ll fall off, and the “world peace” line ties into a shoutout to Metta World Peace, the basketball player. It mixes swagger with wordplay, showing he’s unbothered and in control.", "annotation_id": "35822292", "url": "https://genius.com/35822292", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Emphatic, I splurge, on Addys, I surge, on Xannys,", "vault_lines": ["Emphatic, I splurge, on Addys, I surge, on Xannys,"], "meaning": "Wayne is emphasizing that he uses Adderall and Xanax in abundance.", "annotation_id": "35820572", "url": "https://genius.com/35820572", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Who got next?", "vault_lines": ["Young Money, we're rolling"], "meaning": "Who got next is a sports phrase (specifically basketball.) Wayne is asking who wants to oppose him next in this case, relating to the last bar in which he defeats and turns someone “to coupons.”", "annotation_id": "35820544", "url": "https://genius.com/35820544", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "I mean, the house so fuckin’ massive, I just added concierge", "vault_lines": ["I mean, the house so fuckin' massive, I just added concierge"], "meaning": "A concierge is an employee who helps guests at a multi-tenant building, like an apartment building or hotel. Wayne’s house is so big that he needed a front desk!\n\nhttps://plcsecured.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/condo-concierge-2048x1365-1.webp", "annotation_id": "35820361", "url": "https://genius.com/35820361", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Respect, I don’t demand, I deserve", "vault_lines": ["Respect, I don't demand, I deserve"], "meaning": "subtle throwback to his Tha Carter II banger, Best Rapper Alive\n\n‘'Ánd they still don’t R-E-S-P-E-C-T Me’'\n\nWayne, after selling hundreds of millions of albums over the course of his 25 year career and influencing many of hiphop’s biggest acts in recent years, feels that he deserves all the respect and honour after all his achievements and efforts.", "annotation_id": "35819615", "url": "https://genius.com/35819615", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Life a bitch, you got me flirtin’ with death", "vault_lines": ["Life a bitch, you got me flirtin' with death"], "meaning": "Wayne keeps up with the life’s a bitch phrase wordplay, this time to describe how risky his life is.\n\nHe says his life is so dangerous and on the edge, he’s ‘'flirting with death’'", "annotation_id": "35819568", "url": "https://genius.com/35819568", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "High in the mountains, nigga, uh", "vault_lines": ["High in the mountains, nigga, uh"], "meaning": "First teased in the official Carter VI animation trailer through instagram, Weezy was heard spitting this specific portion of the song during the transition into Tha Carter VI part, at the very end of the trailer.", "annotation_id": "35816455", "url": "https://genius.com/35816455", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Thanks for shoppin’ at Tha Carter, where the verses stay fresh, yes", "vault_lines": ["Thanks for shoppin' at Tha Carter, where the verses stay fresh, yes"], "meaning": "Weezy paints his discography as a supermarket where his rhymes are top-shelf, always-fresh goods", "annotation_id": "35815292", "url": "https://genius.com/35815292", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Her lip service the best", "vault_lines": ["Her lip service the best"], "meaning": "“Lip Service” is said to be when someone publicly supports or approves of something, while actually taking no action to produce it. It could also be a double entendre for oral sex.", "annotation_id": "35815258", "url": "https://genius.com/35815258", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "You’ve been to Hell and back, but have you been to Heaven before?", "vault_lines": ["You've been to Hell and back, but have you been to Heaven before?"], "meaning": "Wayne flips the cliché to ask something heavier: surviving the worst isn’t the same as touching something divine.\n\nIt echoes the themes from “Let It All Work Out” from Tha Carter V, where after a near-death experience, he says:\n\n“I shot it, and I woke up with blood all around me\nIt’s mine, I didn’t die, but as I was dying\nGod came to my side and we talked about it\nHe sold me another life and he made a prophet”\n\nThat moment felt like his glimpse of Heaven — not just survival, but rebirth. So here, Wayne’s not just being rhetorical — he’s speaking from experience, asking if others have truly felt that kind of redemption.", "annotation_id": "35815228", "url": "https://genius.com/35815228", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "Don’t act like my nigga today and a white man tomorrow", "vault_lines": ["Don't act like my nigga today and a white man tomorrow"], "meaning": "Wayne’s calling out performative loyalty: people who act like they’re down with him (“my n****”) one day, then switch up and play the role of authority, judgment, or betrayal (“a white man”) the next, tapping into deeper cultural codes too: the contrast reflects how some folks flip between solidarity and control, especially in environments where race, power, and authenticity clash.", "annotation_id": "35815215", "url": "https://genius.com/35815215", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to Tha Carter", "line": "I treat sweet smart bitches like candy, like Nerds", "vault_lines": ["I treat sweet smart bitches like candy, like Nerds"], "meaning": "“Nerds” refers both to the candy and to smart girls (the stereotype of being a “nerd”). 🤓☝🏼", "annotation_id": "35815180", "url": "https://genius.com/35815180", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
