{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "Bitch I’m from the murder capital", "vault_lines": ["Bitch I’m from the murder capital"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is from New Orleans which was the murder capital of the U.S. around the time when this song came out (2011).\nhttps://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2018/11/13/despite-recent-violence-chicago-far-from-u-s-murder-capital/", "annotation_id": "21726143", "url": "https://genius.com/21726143", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "Tell the warden kiss my ass, pockets on Monique", "vault_lines": ["Tell the warden kiss my ass, pockets on Monique"], "meaning": "This is a clever line that includes a double entendre and homophone. While telling the jail warden to kiss his ass and is leaving jail, Lil Wayne is saying that he has so much money in his pockets that his pockets are fat like Monique the actress. “Monique” is a homophone because when said, it sounds like “money”.", "annotation_id": "21726054", "url": "https://genius.com/21726054", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "I talk a lot of shit and practice what I preach", "vault_lines": ["I talk a lot of shit and practice what I preach"], "meaning": "This is a double entendre. “Talk a lot of shit” means to insult someone and talk bad about them. “Preach” referring to the way he talks. Lil Wayne is saying that he does what he talks about and since he is talking shit, this implies that he is also shitting on other people. To shit on someone means to out preform them and to show off in front of them.", "annotation_id": "21726026", "url": "https://genius.com/21726026", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "And they say time flies, well mine’s first class", "vault_lines": ["And they say time flies, well mine’s first class"], "meaning": "In this pun Lil Wayne is comparing time to flying in first class. When flying in first class most people are living luxurious and are enjoying it. Lil Wayne takes the popular saying “time flies” and says that his time is in first class. Meaning that he is enjoying his time and is living  luxurious life.", "annotation_id": "21726014", "url": "https://genius.com/21726014", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "Rozay!", "vault_lines": ["Cash Money, Young Money, We the Best"], "meaning": "Rozay is used as a nickname or moniker for Rick Ross himself. Beside he also likes to call himself Yung Rénzél or Ricky Rozay. The word rosé is a type of wine and a flavor of the Rick Ross-endorsed Belaire champagne.\n\nRoss' Twitter account is called @rickyrozay.", "annotation_id": "19220364", "url": "https://genius.com/19220364", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "Back from hell, cell 23", "vault_lines": ["Back from hell, cell 23"], "meaning": "Wayne had just recently been released from Rikers Island penitentiary where he served an eight-month sentence for the possession of an unlicensed firearm.", "annotation_id": "13241668", "url": "https://genius.com/13241668", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "They outside playing hopscotch", "vault_lines": ["Welcome to my hood, they outside playing hopscotch"], "meaning": "Hopscotch is a hardcore drinking game involving mass consumption of Scotch.", "annotation_id": "10498087", "url": "https://genius.com/10498087", "state": "accepted", "votes": -10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "Some are on child support", "vault_lines": ["Some are on house arrest, some are on child support"], "meaning": "There are many babies born in the hood.  Because it is generally a place of low income, many of the children need child support", "annotation_id": "148135", "url": "https://genius.com/148135", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "(Woop, woop) That’s the sound of the police", "vault_lines": ["(Woop, woop) That's the sound of the police"], "meaning": "A reference to KRS One’s 1993 classic “Sound of da Police”.\n\n\n\n“Woop, woop” (the sound of a police siren) is an onomatopoeia commonly used to warn others that the police are coming.", "annotation_id": "118132", "url": "https://genius.com/118132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "The Ferrari just a front, got the Lambo in the back", "vault_lines": ["The Ferrari just a front, got the Lambo in the back"], "meaning": "He keeps the Ferrari out front for decoration, but just because it’s what you see in his yard or driveway,some people might assume it’s the only “show-off” car he’s got, which is not at all true. There’s a Lamborghini just out of plain view, which we can assume Ross pulls out when people are least expecting it.", "annotation_id": "118101", "url": "https://genius.com/118101", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "I’m red hot", "vault_lines": ["Young Money, Cash Money, blood bitch, I’m red hot"], "meaning": "This is clever word play off him shouting out his Blood gang affiliation (red is the color of the Bloods)", "annotation_id": "98970", "url": "https://genius.com/98970", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "I know some niggas from my hood that would rob Noriega I’m talkin’ Noriega, nigga, the real Noriega", "vault_lines": ["I know some niggas from my hood that would rob Noriega"], "meaning": "Considering he’s languishing in US prison and suffered a stroke, I don’t think it takes much courage to jack from Panama’s notorious coke-selling dictator nowadays\n\n\n\nAlso a play on Rick Ross' line from “Hustlin”:\n\nI know Pablo, Noriega, the real Noriega", "annotation_id": "98268", "url": "https://genius.com/98268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Welcome to My Hood", "line": "I landed in the sky, I fell from the streets", "vault_lines": ["I landed in the sky, I fell from the streets"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne went from being broke in the streets to landing in the skies of success at the TOP of the rap-game.\n\n\n\nThis line is a clever paradox, since one can’t literaly fall from the street and land in the sky. Metaphorically, though, Wayne is defying gravity, the class system. He is an anomaly in American society, a man turned myth.", "annotation_id": "97414", "url": "https://genius.com/97414", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
