{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Vroom! I had to bring the hog out", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "A hog is a Harley Davidson motorcycle.", "annotation_id": "30619310", "url": "https://genius.com/30619310", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "We’ll be in the M-I-A-mi", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "Wayne is from New Orleans but would frequent Miami.", "annotation_id": "30619307", "url": "https://genius.com/30619307", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Touch it, bring it, push it—systematic", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "A reference to the Daft Punk song “Technologic”\nhttps://genius.com/Daft-punk-technologic-lyrics", "annotation_id": "30619297", "url": "https://genius.com/30619297", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Homeboy got a mind that a map wouldn’t find", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "“Homeboy got a mind that a map wouldn’t find” This means he has a mind that no one can comprehend.", "annotation_id": "28982349", "url": "https://genius.com/28982349", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Great Scott— Storch, can I borrow your yacht?", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "More excellent wordplay from Weezy.\n\nLike “Yikes,” “Great Scott” is an old-fashioned interjection.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_e24kdjdbtw\n\nScott Storch is a famous record producer who was based in Miami. Prior to 2007 (when “We Takin' Over (Remix) was released), Storch had scored a string of hits with the likes of Fat Joe, 50 Cent, Christina Aguilera, Beyoncé, Dr. Dre, and many others; however, a severe cocaine addiction sent him into a downward spiral, during which he blew through most of his accumulated fortune in a matter of months. Among his extravagant purchases was this 117-foot yacht, which reportedly cost $20 million.", "annotation_id": "14024181", "url": "https://genius.com/14024181", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "I’m a prince, too demanding, like my mom Too bold", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "A reference to the Prince classic “When Doves Cry”:\n\nMaybe I’m just too demanding\nMaybe I’m just like my father, too bold\nMaybe you’re just like my mother\nShe’s never satisfied", "annotation_id": "14024062", "url": "https://genius.com/14024062", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "And yeah, I done squashed the bullshit with Zoe", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "After Cash Money Records moved its headquarters from NOLA to Miami following Hurricane Katrina, Weezy and CMR briefly ran afoul of Zoe Pound, a local street gang founded by Haitian immigrants. An escalating series of incidents apparently culminated in Weezy’s RR Phantom being torched before the beef was squashed.", "annotation_id": "14023958", "url": "https://genius.com/14023958", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "And I’m his kid—I stunt with my daddy", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "The previous year (2006) had seen Birdman and Weezy team up for the album Like Father, Like Son, whose first single was “Stuntin' Like My Daddy.” (The song stalled just outside the Billboard Top 20, but Rolling Stone named it one of the 100 Best Songs of 2006, and it won the BET Viewer’s Choice Award in 2007.)", "annotation_id": "14023852", "url": "https://genius.com/14023852", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "One time for me, one time for the DJ", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "Weezy gives a shoutout to the originator of this track, the infamous DJ Khaled.\n\n\n\nWayne and Khaled have collaborated many times, including Khaled’s first single “Holla At Me”.", "annotation_id": "2880067", "url": "https://genius.com/2880067", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "It’s a bakery here, just tryin’ to get dough", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "A play on words – he’s tryin to get “dough” (slang for money), like a bakery where they bake using dough", "annotation_id": "1048270", "url": "https://genius.com/1048270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Hello, hip-hop, I’m home—it’s your daddy", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "Weezy has previously referred to himself as the father of rap (even though he was born in 1982, and his first hip hop single was released in 1979)\n\nWayne may not have fathered hip hop, but he certainly fathered a style of his own which he and his Young Money crew continue to thrive off of.  Hip hop was waiting for someone like him the way a young boy waits for his father to get home – with excitement.", "annotation_id": "106927", "url": "https://genius.com/106927", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "When I say “deez,” I don’t mean the Caddy", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "D’s is a common term rappers use to refer to 20+ inch rims on their Cadillacs (“caddy”). However, Wayne using its homophone “deez” and saying that you should not confuse “deez” (his nuts) with “D’s” (large rims).", "annotation_id": "106926", "url": "https://genius.com/106926", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Like the ball in the stands, we balls out, ugh!", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "When the ball is in the stands, the ball is out of bounds, so Wayne is comparing a ball being out of bounds to him and his crew being “balls out” (a slang phrase meaning “to work as hard as possible)", "annotation_id": "30807", "url": "https://genius.com/30807", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "It’s me The Rapper Eater Haha! Feed me, feeed me, feed me!", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "He refers to himself often as the Rapper Eater, meaning that he metaphorically “eats” (completely destroys) other rappers in battle\n\nAlso a reference to his verse on the original song:\n\nI am the beast\nFeed me rappers or feed me beats", "annotation_id": "29959", "url": "https://genius.com/29959", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "I’m a king—you can ask Stephen if I’m lying", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "Continuing off of the “scary movie” line before it, Wayne references famous horror novelist Stephen King to say that he is as rich and famous as a king.\n\n\n\nAlso add to the fact that Lyin' sounds like lion, the king of the jungle.", "annotation_id": "29957", "url": "https://genius.com/29957", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "I mean “deez nuts,” Akons and Khaled Rick Ross, Tip, Stunna and Fat Joe And I ca-ca-can’t forget Brisco", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "All the artists that Wayne shouts out here are on the original version of “We Takin' Over” (except Brisco)\n\n\n\nBrisco did a remix of “We Takin' Over” with Triple C’s, DJ Khaled, Flo Rida, Ashley Ross and Rick Ross. This could be why Wayne’s shouting him out here, or it could just be because they’re on the same label.", "annotation_id": "29955", "url": "https://genius.com/29955", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "I’m the best rapper alive", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "Wayne makes this claim a lot: he even has a song titled “Best Rapper Alive”", "annotation_id": "29954", "url": "https://genius.com/29954", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Every day Christmas, I’m eggnogged out", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "Continuing off of the “bring a log out” line (lighting logs at Christmastime is a tradition), every day is like Christmas for Wayne, because he gets so many gifts due to all his wealth. Also, drinking eggnog is another Christmas tradition, but drinking too much will make you drunk; Wayne is inferring that he’s always intoxicated\n\nThe term “eggnogged” out may also be referring to Wayne’s heavy use of Promethazine Cough syrup. “Eggnogged out” could be a play on words alluding to “nodding out” from lots of syrup.", "annotation_id": "29952", "url": "https://genius.com/29952", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Beef! Yes! Chest! Feet! Tag! Bag! Blood! Sheets!", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "The bullets will go through your chest, killing you. The coroner will put a tag on your big toe to identify you (as is procedure with dead bodies). You’ll also be put into a bag at the morgue\n\n\n\n(Notice how “chest” and “feet” are grouped together, because they are both body parts)", "annotation_id": "29942", "url": "https://genius.com/29942", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Homeboy got a nine that a cop wouldn’t find", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "Around the time Da Drought 3 was recorded, Wayne was arrested for attempted criminal possession of a weapon. What this line means is that he has a 9mm gun (a nine) that is so well hidden that cops are oblivious to it. You’re not going to stop him from carrying guns.", "annotation_id": "29940", "url": "https://genius.com/29940", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "Damn right, I kissed my daddy", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "A reference to a photograph of Wayne kissing his surrogate father, Birdman, on the mouth, which was released by Wayne’s rival Gillie da Kid to discredit him. Instead of denying the accusations, Wayne admitted it.", "annotation_id": "29934", "url": "https://genius.com/29934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Takin’ Over", "line": "You looking for me, ho? I’m in the three-oh-— —five", "vault_lines": ["On up to Minnesota where it storm in the winter"], "meaning": "305 is the area code for Miami-Dade County, Florida, where Wayne lives", "annotation_id": "29497", "url": "https://genius.com/29497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
