{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "And my weight up like six gyms", "vault_lines": ["And my weight up like six gyms"], "meaning": "Play on words: he comparing his weight gain to gyms, which not only have a whole bunch of heavy equipment but are also the places where you work out to put on weight through muscles.", "annotation_id": "1845364", "url": "https://genius.com/1845364", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Put fam second and God first", "vault_lines": ["Put fam second and God first"], "meaning": "A reference to the Bible verse Matthew 20:37:\n\nIf you love your father or mother\nmore than you love me,\nyou are not worthy of being mine;\nor if you love your\nson or daughter more than me,\nyou are not worthy of being mine.\n\nThe verse states that to be worthy of God, you’ve got to love Him more than you love your family. Ace follows this code by stating God is his #1 priority, followed by his family.", "annotation_id": "1779246", "url": "https://genius.com/1779246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Lil bro, you know I got you", "vault_lines": ["Lil bro, you know I got you"], "meaning": "Ace is signed to Khaled’s We The Best label, which is distributed under YMCMB. Thus, Ace can be considered a YMCMB artist, so Lil Wayne has Ace’s back.\n\n\n\nAlso, Ace is younger than Wayne and looks kinda like him, hence Weezy calling Ace his “bro”.", "annotation_id": "1779135", "url": "https://genius.com/1779135", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Real life Ace Hooooood, yeah We the motherfuckin’ best, huh?", "vault_lines": ["We the motherfuckin' best, huh?"], "meaning": "A usual Ace Hood intro: a double entendre, referring to the fact that:\n\n\nHe (along with Weezy) are among the best rappers in the game right now.\nThe record label he is signed to.", "annotation_id": "1778300", "url": "https://genius.com/1778300", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "I made my way from the bottom and I’m grindin’ (I’m grindin’)", "vault_lines": ["I made my way from the bottom and I'm grindin' (I'm grindin')"], "meaning": "A common theme in rap is to make your way to the top after starting with nothing. This could be Ace’s rendition of Drizzy’s Started From The Bottom.\n\nAlso, Khaled’s single Out here Grindin' was one of the first songs that Ace Hood was featured on.", "annotation_id": "1777427", "url": "https://genius.com/1777427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "All I do is count my blessings, Weezy F for Fortunate", "vault_lines": ["All I do is count my blessings, Weezy F for Fortunate"], "meaning": "The blessings here refer to the millions that Wayne has stacked over the years, rapping for the masses.\n\nOh and by the way,\n\nThere’s another one! The list of things that the F in Weezy F Baby has stood for is really stretching. We’re thinking Wayne’s going to run down every F word there is. To see the whole list, check the end of our blog post dedicated to Wayne’s obsession for nicknames.", "annotation_id": "1774613", "url": "https://genius.com/1774613", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Kill the beat, it’s premeditated", "vault_lines": ["Kill the beat, it's premeditated"], "meaning": "A premeditated murder refers to a planned killed. Ace had planned to kill this beat, therefore it is a premeditated murder.", "annotation_id": "1774608", "url": "https://genius.com/1774608", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Hatin’ niggas ain’t phasin’ me You couldn’t walk a mile in my footwear", "vault_lines": ["Hatin' niggas ain't phasin' me"], "meaning": "A play on the old saying, “You can’t walk a mile in my shoes” which could refer to the fact haters wouldn’t understand the struggle he endured to get where he is, and that they would never be able to live the lavish life that he does.", "annotation_id": "1774606", "url": "https://genius.com/1774606", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Shout out my Zoe’s, Sak Pase", "vault_lines": ["Shout out my Zoe's, Sak Pase"], "meaning": "Zoe’s are a reference to the Zoe Pound (a gang founded by Haitian Immigrants, based in Miami, Florida)\n\n\n\nSak Pase is Haitian Creole language that generally means What’s Up or What’s Happening.\n\nSak Pase is also a reference to the line “Sak Pase them killers is here” said by Ace Hood in the song “Bugatti”.", "annotation_id": "1772735", "url": "https://genius.com/1772735", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Stay on my toes, no ballet", "vault_lines": ["Stay on my toes, no ballet"], "meaning": "Similar to a “no homo” declaration, Wayne quickly clears the air: the line is meant to be interpreted in a figurative way ONLY. He is NOT into ballet. Ballet is a GAY activity. And Wayne is not gay, right?\n\n\n\nPs. “Stay on my toes” means staying alert at all times. A way more street interpretation than ballet, a dance involving a lot of balancing on toes.", "annotation_id": "1772686", "url": "https://genius.com/1772686", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Yeahhhh ’Cause we outchea (we outchea) We outchea (we outchea) Ain’t no sleepin’ (ain’t no sleepin’) ’Cause we outchea (’cause we outchea) And we outchea (and we outchea) We outchea (we outchea) Grindin’ all damn night, ’cause we outchea", "vault_lines": ["'Cause we outchea (we outchea)"], "meaning": "Outchea is slang which literally means “out here” – and it basically refers to the fact that Ace & Weezy are out in the streets (because they’ve earned the respect of their hood to do so) or that they’re in the studio grinding all night in order to achieve success.\n\n\n\nThis could a reference to an old Lil Boosie song.", "annotation_id": "1772462", "url": "https://genius.com/1772462", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Nigga, I was born and raised in that jungle", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, I was born and raised in that jungle"], "meaning": "Ace was born in Port St. Lucie, FL but raised in Deerfield Beach, FL. He was basically raised in the hood, which might’ve contributed to his stage Ace Hood.", "annotation_id": "1771853", "url": "https://genius.com/1771853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "We The Best, where my team at?", "vault_lines": ["We The Best, where my team at?"], "meaning": "Ace Hood is signed to Khaled’s We The Best Music Group and reps them in pretty much all of his songs.", "annotation_id": "1771489", "url": "https://genius.com/1771489", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Hundred dollars to a couple grand Couple grand to a hundred bands Hundred bands to them big Ms", "vault_lines": ["Hundred dollars to a couple grand"], "meaning": "Ace personifies a true hustler: he parlayed a couple hundred dollars into a major record deal and is now worth approximately $5 million.", "annotation_id": "1771488", "url": "https://genius.com/1771488", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "Young Mula babyyy", "vault_lines": ["'Cause we outchea (we outchea)"], "meaning": "Young Money or “Young Mula” is Lil Wayne’s music group.", "annotation_id": "1771199", "url": "https://genius.com/1771199", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "I go dumber than Lamont Sanford", "vault_lines": ["I go dumber than Lamont Sanford"], "meaning": "Lamont Sanford of the TV show Sanford and Son was frequently derided as “you big dummy” by his TV father, Fred Sanford\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lMTrthePKU0", "annotation_id": "1770939", "url": "https://genius.com/1770939", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "line": "I came up from that bottom, now I’m richer than chocolate", "vault_lines": ["I came up from that bottom, now I'm richer than chocolate"], "meaning": "Unless you’re having it dark, chocolate is sweet and milky, and anything sweet and milk based is said to have a “rich” taste. Well Wayne is richer, so ha! take that chocolate!\n\n\n\nThe line is also a nod to Started From The Bottom by Drake, a long time friend and collaborater.", "annotation_id": "1770860", "url": "https://genius.com/1770860", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Outchea", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Ace-hood-we-outchea-lyrics", "genius_title": "We Outchea", "genius_id": 151639, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Livin’ Like That", "line": "Sour grapes, nigga, ’cause you whine like brats", "vault_lines": ["Sour grapes, nigga, 'cause you whine like brats"], "meaning": "Wine is made from grapes, but Wayne uses it as a way to diss anyone against him. Calling them soft.", "annotation_id": "24271072", "url": "https://genius.com/24271072", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Livin’ Like That", "line": "My Nina a twin I don’t know two other bad sisters meaner than them I call them Venus and Serena, they hyena your limbs", "vault_lines": ["Magazine extend, and my Nina a twin"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne calls his Nina a twin, which means he has two pistols (Nina refers to a 9mm handgun, but colloquially the term has evolved to referring to pistols or even guns in general). Wayne opened this song speaking about how he had two guns:\n\n“Burner by the car door\nAlso the torso”\n\nWayne goes on to say that these twin weapons are mean, and there are no sisters meaner. He calls them Venus and Serena, two sisters that are two of the best tennis player’s in the game today.\n\n\n\nThey are both extremely beautiful, which Wayne refers to as “mean,” a sometimes negative word like “bad” used to positively describe a woman’s beauty.", "annotation_id": "23718039", "url": "https://genius.com/23718039", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Livin’ Like That", "line": "I need a theme song, nigga, I’m a masked man", "vault_lines": ["I need a theme song, nigga, I'm a masked man"], "meaning": "It seems that Lil Wayne is alluding to wrestling, specifically luchadores, a Spanish word for a specific type of wrestler, typically Mexican, that wears a luchador mask.\n\n\n\nFor the ideas of a “theme song” and “masked man” to be compared/mirrored in these lines, it makes the most sense that Wayne was referring to wrestling.", "annotation_id": "23717983", "url": "https://genius.com/23717983", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Livin’ Like That", "line": "I’ma put my niggas where my heart go (Heart go)", "vault_lines": ["I'ma put my niggas where my heart go (Heart go)"], "meaning": "There’s an understated play on words here, with Wayne implying that he’s “heartless,” so to speak. Wayne plans on devoting his love and consideration solely to his closest friends.\n\nThis ties into the song’s core themes of a detachment from others and an inability to escape from a life of violence.", "annotation_id": "20272085", "url": "https://genius.com/20272085", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Livin’ Like That", "line": "Now I feel like 50 Cent ’cause I see many men", "vault_lines": ["Now I feel like 50 Cent 'cause I see many men"], "meaning": "A reference to 50 Cents classic song Many Men.", "annotation_id": "20250884", "url": "https://genius.com/20250884", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Livin’ Like That", "line": "These pussycat niggas need a CAT scan (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["These pussycat niggas need a CAT scan (Yeah)"], "meaning": "A classic Wayne double entendre. Playing on the words pussy and cat.", "annotation_id": "20250874", "url": "https://genius.com/20250874", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "We Livin’ Like That", "line": "I cut his wings off, make a nigga crash land (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["I cut his wings off, make a nigga crash land (Woo)"], "meaning": "A subtle jab at Weezys former label boss Birdman. Since Wayne left, the label has faded into un-relevance. Wayne has said a similar line on this track.\n\nWayne made the label big after artists as Juvenile left, single handily keeping it alive through the early 2000s and later finding and signing big artists like Drake, Tyga, and Nicki Minaj", "annotation_id": "20250840", "url": "https://genius.com/20250840", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
