{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Watch My Shoes", "line": "I act so New Orl-e-ans,", "vault_lines": ["I act so New Orl-e-ans,"], "meaning": "Act is slang for lean. Wayne bends the word ‘Orleans' to emphasize the ‘lean’ part while also giving it an extra syllable to match the 3 syllables in ‘Angeles’.", "annotation_id": "28608621", "url": "https://genius.com/28608621", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Watch My Shoes", "line": "Call my nigga Gudda if you trying to get your mammy back All up in another nigga woman, I be ramming that", "vault_lines": ["Call my nigga Gudda if you trying to get your mammy back"], "meaning": "A really long multisyllabic rhyme, using 15 syllables. This is very rare in hip hop.", "annotation_id": "28608553", "url": "https://genius.com/28608553", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Watch My Shoes", "line": "I dress so Los Angeles, but I love Miami, though", "vault_lines": ["I dress so Los Angeles, but I love Miami, though"], "meaning": "At the time of this song Wayne was going through his “rockstar” phase (his next project would be Rebirth), and could often be seen in skinny jeans, vans, plaid shirts and fedoras, a style often associated with Los Angeles.\n\nHowever, unlike many celebrities Wayne lives in Miami, as it is close to his home state of Louisiana.", "annotation_id": "20729404", "url": "https://genius.com/20729404", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Watch My Shoes", "line": "Gudda, why they started me? Marley, why they started me?", "vault_lines": ["Gudda, why they started me? Marley, why they started me?"], "meaning": "Wayne is talking about fellow New Orleans rapper and Young Money signee Gudda Gudda, who has been with Weezy since the Sqad Up days, and Marley is Marley G, one of Wayne’s closest friends outside of rap who has been acknowledged on many other Wayne songs. Not to be confused with the New Orleans rapper Marley G who was murdered in 2018.", "annotation_id": "20729389", "url": "https://genius.com/20729389", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Watch My Shoes", "line": "And I ain’t gassed up, ’cause I’m more like a hybrid", "vault_lines": ["And I ain't gassed up, 'cause I'm more like a hybrid"], "meaning": "Double Entendre:\n\n1)\n\nWayne doesn’t let people mess with his head, or get gassed up. He puns on this phrase by bragging about his versatility musically, referring to himself as a hybrid, which is commonly known to be a variety of cars (cars use gas), but its literal meaning is something that is made up of more than one thing.\n\nWays Lil Wayne is a musical hybrid:\n\n\nHe can switch up his flows in the middle of a beat, as seen on this track.\nHe can make club hits, thug songs, or even love songs.\nHe can tell stories in his music or just brag on a song; also seen on this track.\n\n\n\n2)\n\nWordplay: Homophone\n\n\nThis line can also be heard as:\n\n\n\nI ain’t gettin' stuck, because I’m more like a hybrid\n\nWeezy has verses for days. Usually when people freestyle, eventually they get caught up and run out of bars; not Wayne. He can rap for as long as a hybrid vehicle can drive. The most popular hybrid during the release of this song, the 2010 Toyota Prius, gets 50 miles per gallon of gas.", "annotation_id": "2890574", "url": "https://genius.com/2890574", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Watch My Shoes", "line": "And I’m such a philanthropist, the God to these evangelists", "vault_lines": ["And I'm such a philanthropist, the God to these evangelists"], "meaning": "An evangelist is someone who likes to preach their religion and convert others to feel the same way. Since Wayne is a philanthropist, he does the same; except he spreads love and welfare to the world instead of religion.", "annotation_id": "2743958", "url": "https://genius.com/2743958", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Watch My Shoes", "line": "Sheesh, gosh, Oshkosh B’gosh", "vault_lines": ["Sheesh, gosh, Oshkosh B’gosh"], "meaning": "Oshkosh B'Gosh is a children’s clothing line. Wayne uses their name as a replacement for the phrase “Oh my gosh”.", "annotation_id": "2735333", "url": "https://genius.com/2735333", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Watch My Shoes", "line": "Mr. Officer, stop arresting your bitches", "vault_lines": ["Mr. Officer, stop arresting your bitches"], "meaning": "“Cuffing” a girl is slang for trying to marry her and make her your wife.\n\nWayne is saying that you shouldn’t treat standard bitches like respectable women. Don’t marry them, just fuck them and leave.\n\nCould also be a reference to Wayne’s very own song Mrs. Officer", "annotation_id": "2735318", "url": "https://genius.com/2735318", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
