{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk Out", "line": "This was Tha Carter, slam the door Go", "vault_lines": ["I say I'm at your throat, this was Tha Carter, slam the door"], "meaning": "Wayne simultaneously ends the tour of his house and the first installation of the All-Time Great album series, Tha Carter series, which, to many critics and listeners alike, is the magnum opus of his musical career.\n\nRather than simply “close” the door on his house (and the album), he “slam[s] the door” on the listener, and shouts at him/her to “Go!”\n\nWhile an artist might be expected to encourage a listener to give the project additional listens, Wayne has his own way of going about things, and he typically does so with the utmost emphasis.\n\n“It [is not] about what you [are] talking about; it [is] about how you [are] talking about it.” – 1 minute, 30 seconds into the following interview with ABC News: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L6mBZSQdGCE", "annotation_id": "32702324", "url": "https://genius.com/32702324", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk Out", "line": "That’s Mama Carter, she’s a basket case But I make sure her sandwiches and napkins straight You pick with this picnic and I’m at your face, fucker", "vault_lines": ["That's Mama Carter, she's a basket case"], "meaning": "Wayne refers to his mother, Mama Carter, as a “basket” case.\n\nHe makes sure her “sandwiches” (necessary means to survive) and “napkins” (extra money on top of the necessaries) are “straight” (taken care of by him).\n\nIf you “pick” (mess) with this aforementioned “picnic” (the arrangement whereby Wayne takes care of Mama Carter), then Wayne is at your face, fucker!", "annotation_id": "32702323", "url": "https://genius.com/32702323", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk Out", "line": "One window to let the dawn in ’Cause we hustle in this bitch midnight to morning, yeah We dump in and pump out, ow I showed you this window for you to jump out, ow", "vault_lines": ["'Cause we hustle in this bitch midnight to morning, yeah"], "meaning": "Wayne introduces the listener to the only window in his house, which he uses to let in daylight after a hard night’s work in the drug-making process.\n\nJust two lines later, however, Wayne explains that the true reason he brought the listener to the window is for the listener to jump out of it. Thus, the window serves two purposes in continuing the theme of a “trap-house of horror.”\n\nMoreover, the final line in this section demonstrates that Wayne likely would not have disclosed the window’s relatedness to the drug-making process unless he planned to kill the listener shortly afterward.", "annotation_id": "32702278", "url": "https://genius.com/32702278", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk Out", "line": "Illiterate are you? That’s okay, man, I got the medicine for you I got, I got, I got the amphetamine for you Got that vitamin D-R-U-G for your shorty", "vault_lines": ["That's okay, man, I got the medicine for you"], "meaning": "As Wayne continues to give a tour of his house to the listener (continued from “Walk In” and “Inside”), he notices the listener does not know what “embalming fluid” is and, thus, assumes the listener is illiterate.\n\nTaking advantage of the listener’s illiteracy, Wayne assures that he “has the medicine” to fix the listener’s condition.\n\nWayne offers the listener “Em-phet-a-min,” rather than “Am-phet-a-meen.” This mispronunciation cleverly disguises the substance’s true nature as a drug, as the listener may not recognize the former pronunciation (which sounds similar to the word “vitamin”).\n\nAdditionally, Wayne offers “Vitamin D-R-U-G” to the listener who, if truly illiterate, will be unable to piece those letters into a complete word in order to understand he/she is taking drugs. By using individual letters to spell out “drug,” Wayne cleverly dupes the unsuspecting listener because actual vitamins follow a similar naming convention: Vitamins A, E, K, D, B, etc.", "annotation_id": "32702155", "url": "https://genius.com/32702155", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk Out", "line": "You on the backyard lawn where a nigga got the ganja growin’ No cats or dogs, rats or snakes, Sams or Jakes Around here, it’s all gravy, ham and steak", "vault_lines": ["You on the backyard lawn where a nigga got the ganja growin'"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne explains what can be found in his “backyard” (i.e., the hood). More specifically, after stating that he grows ganja in his \"backyard,” he explains what this location lacks.\n\nThere are no cats or dogs because pets are an unaffordable luxury, and this is not a safe environment for pets.\n\nThere are “no rats or snakes” because the rest of the “jungle” (i.e., the hood) eliminates these creatures. “Rats” refer to people who snitch, and “snakes” refer to backstabbers.\n\nThere are three meanings when Wayne says there are “no Sams or Jakes” in his backyard:\n1. There are no federal law enforcement officers (Uncle Sams) or police officers (Jakes).\n2. No one goes by their birth name–everyone has a nickname.\n3. There are no white people (Sam and Jake are basic, traditional names predominantly assigned to  white Americans).\n\nDespite this, Wayne concludes by assuring the listener that “around here, it’s all gravy” (i.e., it’s all good). Moreover, the meats listed in the final line of this annotation refer to pork (ham) and beef (steak)—noticeably absent is chicken, implying that people who are “chicken” (i.e., cowardly) are no longer “around here.”", "annotation_id": "32660289", "url": "https://genius.com/32660289", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk Out", "line": "And in here is where the heroin boil And I also got a large pot of cigarette oil That’s embalming fluid,", "vault_lines": ["And in here is where the heroin boil"], "meaning": "This is where Wayne keeps the drugs. He got heroine and embalming fluid… That’s some hard stuff Weezy!!\n\n\n\nA good way to do he stuff is dipping your cigarette into it.", "annotation_id": "631919", "url": "https://genius.com/631919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk Out", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-walk-out-lyrics", "genius_title": "Walk Out", "genius_id": 28615, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk It Out", "line": "My nigga T-Streets know a girl named Duchess And every time he see her in the street, she be cussin’ I told him he should wave the black flag ‘cause she buggin’ He said, “The pussy good,” he said, “You dig?” So I dug him", "vault_lines": ["My nigga T-Streets know a girl named Duchess"], "meaning": "Through a two-sided metaphor involving pirates and a brand of insecticide, Wayne tells T-Streets to get rid of his girl, but he won’t because the sex is good.\n\nWayne describes T-Streets' relationship with a girl who curses frequently named “Duchess.” A person who curses frequently is said to have “a sailor’s mouth.” The Duchess was a privateer commanded by Englishman Woodes Rogers from 1708 to 1711 during the golden age of piracy.\n\nDuring the golden age of piracy, a red flag on an attacking ship meant “No quarter”—the attacking ship would unconditionally attack the target ship, leaving no prisoners or survivors. A target ship would “wave the white flag” to indicate surrender. In contrast, a black flag on an attacking ship signified that people aboard the target ship would live if they surrendered their loot and cargo without contest.\n\nWayne advises T-Streets to “wave the black flag,” meaning T-Streets should just have sex with Duchess then leave her alone.\n\n\n\n“Black Flag” also refers to a brand of insecticide products. Because Duchess is “bugging,” Wayne suggests that T-Streets should metaphorically spray her with Black Flag insecticide (“wave the Black Flag”) to make her go away.\n\n\n\nNevertheless, T-Streets explains that Duchess’ pussy feels too good to get rid of, he asks whether Wayne understands the dilemma, and Wayne confirms that he does.", "annotation_id": "36322954", "url": "https://genius.com/36322954", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk It Out", "line": "And I know plenty freaky hoes that probably won’t fuck ya Even if you pay ’em, boy, they probably won’t fuck ya", "vault_lines": ["And I know plenty freaky hoes that probably won't fuck ya"], "meaning": "Wayne obliterates his enemy here. He says they’re so ugly and swagless that even the freakiest of hoes wouldn’t want to sleep with them. Even if they paid them, they STILL wouldn’t fuck them. Colddd!", "annotation_id": "35860784", "url": "https://genius.com/35860784", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk It Out", "line": "Man, I’m just tryna get my peanuts, I’m like Charlie Brown", "vault_lines": ["Man, I'm just tryna get my peanuts, I'm like Charlie Brown"], "meaning": "Charles “Charlie” Brown is the principal character of the comic strip Peanuts, syndicated in daily and Sunday newspapers in numerous countries worldwide. Depicted as a “lovable loser,” Charlie Brown is one of the great American archetypes and a popular and widely recognized cartoon character. Charlie Brown is characterized as a person who frequently suffers, and as a result, is usually nervous and lacks self-confidence. He shows both pessimistic and optimistic attitudes: on some days, he is apprehensive to even go outside because his day might just be spoiled, but on others, he hopes for the best and tries as much as he can to accomplish things. He is easily recognized by his trademark zigzag patterned shirt.\n\nThe character’s creator, Charles M. Schulz, said that Charlie Brown “has to be the one who suffers, because he is a caricature of the average person. Most of us are much more acquainted with losing than we are with winning.” Despite this, Charlie Brown does not always suffer, as he has experienced some happy moments and victories through the years, and he has sometimes uncharacteristically shown self-assertiveness despite his frequent nervousness. Schulz also said: “I like to have Charlie Brown eventually be the focal point of almost every story.” Charlie Brown is the only Peanuts character to have appeared regularly in the strip throughout its entire 50-year run.\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlie_Brown", "annotation_id": "30619371", "url": "https://genius.com/30619371", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Walk It Out", "line": "And when we on the jet, you don’t come up in the discussion", "vault_lines": ["And when we on the jet, you don't come up in the discussion"], "meaning": "the target of this line is irrevelant to high level Cash Money discussions on the jet airplane", "annotation_id": "26361025", "url": "https://genius.com/26361025", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
