{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Now I’m lookin’ at the Robb Report", "vault_lines": ["Now I'm lookin' at the Robb Report"], "meaning": "The line is “Now I’m looking at the Robb Report” A reference to  the American, luxury-lifestyle magazine featuring products, including automobiles, aviation, boating, real estate and watches.", "annotation_id": "17079525", "url": "https://genius.com/17079525", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "I’m an OG up in OT And it’s no sleep ’til it’s roast beef", "vault_lines": ["I'm an OG up in OT"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne has been in the rap game since the early 90s, and has been widely known as an Original Gangster as far as the rap game goes. “OT” could be the provincial acronym for Ontario, Canada, home of many YM artists. Alternatively, Wayne could have used “OT” as short for Overtime, meaning when it comes to a close call, rely on Wayne to get it done.", "annotation_id": "15746150", "url": "https://genius.com/15746150", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Eunonymus", "vault_lines": ["Never been anonymous, money is eunonymus"], "meaning": "The actual lyric is “money is eunonymus” not unanimous, eunonymus is a fairly common evergreen ornamental shrub, ie his money is eternal, evergreen, etc. I use to do landscaping. You can clearly hear it when you listen again that hes not saying “unanimous” and its not a mispronunciation.\n\nAlso “unanimous” would make no sense", "annotation_id": "14780274", "url": "https://genius.com/14780274", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Talkin’ dirty money? Check the hygienes All the dirty money get dry-cleaned Talkin’ dirty money, Sam Rothstein", "vault_lines": ["Talkin' dirty money? Check the hygienes"], "meaning": "Dirty Money is a term used to descrbe money obtained through unlawful and/or immoral activities, such as selling drugs or prostitution etc.\n\nWayne uses a double entendre here to state the method in which the dirty money is concealed –  Dry Cleaners, (aka Laundromats), hence the word “money laundering” a term popularized by the legitimate clothes and linen cleaning businesses that were commonly used by organized crime networks (alongside other businesses such as Casino’s), to conceal dirty money.\n\n\n\nSam ‘Ace’ Rothstein was a character portrayed by Robert DeNiro in the 1995 movie, “Casino”.\n\nBoth the movie “Casino” & Sam Rothstein’s character were inspired by and based on the life of Frank ‘Lefty’ Rosenthal, a casino owner and crime associate who was known for running America’s biggest illegal bookmaking office for the Mafia.", "annotation_id": "14497190", "url": "https://genius.com/14497190", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "My niggas catch a case, feel like I got Moss’d", "vault_lines": ["My niggas catch a case, feel like I got Moss'd"], "meaning": "To “catch a case” means to be arrested and charged with a crime, therefore “catching” a legal case.\n\n“Moss’d” is a term that originates from American football. When referring to the sport, a player gets “moss’d” when another player jumps over them to catch the ball. The word was created and popularized with the career of former NFL star receiver Randy Moss, who was renowned for his extraordinary athletic ability and skill at catching contested passes over defenders.\n\n\n\nIn this context, Wayne means that those close to him take responsibility for crimes that he commits or is involved in. It’s common practice for celebrities to have “fall guys”, people who will take a majority (or all) of the blame if the celebrity becomes involved in illegal activity.\n\nDespite this, Wayne has a long history of legal troubles, mostly involving drug and weapon charges.", "annotation_id": "14476807", "url": "https://genius.com/14476807", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "My nigga treat the K just like a loft", "vault_lines": ["My nigga treat the K just like a loft"], "meaning": "The K could be for Kilo or for a grand. and he states that he treats it just like a love likely saying that he treats it well making sure that nothing were to happen to it also making another basketball player reference in Kevin Love", "annotation_id": "14233318", "url": "https://genius.com/14233318", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "I done seen so many titties on Bourbon", "vault_lines": ["I done seen so many titties on Bourbon"], "meaning": "Here Wayne’s talking about the famous Bourbon Street in his hometown of New Orleans, Louisiana. Bourbon Street is a key location for New Orleans Mardi Gras celebration, in which women typically expose their breasts for necklaces.\n\nHowever, since Weezy has lived in New Orleans his whole life, only minutes from Bourbon Street, he has probably spent countless nights at its bars and strip clubs.", "annotation_id": "14049853", "url": "https://genius.com/14049853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "It’s a cold world, I got an ice grill", "vault_lines": ["It's a cold world, I got an ice grill"], "meaning": "The world might be a cold place, but Weezy smiles back at it with his shiny teeth, showing that he’s made the most of his time on it.\n\nWayne is known for having a full top and bottom set of fully iced out grills, which cost $150,000 and required surgery for removal.", "annotation_id": "14049845", "url": "https://genius.com/14049845", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Sippin’ on purple like it’s herbal", "vault_lines": ["Sippin' on purple like it's herbal"], "meaning": "Lots of people drink herbal tea to feel relaxed, but Wayne’s drink of choice is lean. However, this has possibly led to many problems in his life as well.\n\nOn many occasions, the media has reported Wayne having seizures due to lean, but some of these have not been confirmed as being caused by the drug.", "annotation_id": "14036400", "url": "https://genius.com/14036400", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Cut off the wings, now I’m soaring", "vault_lines": ["Cut off the wings, now I'm soaring"], "meaning": "“Cut off the wings” could be a reference to Birdman or “Bird Gang”. Since Lil Wayne was looking to leave Cash Money, which is owned by Birdman and Ronald “Slim” Williams, he cut off Birdman (or the wings), and is now free to spread his wings.\n\nWeezy also used to call himself the “Birdman Junior.” On his 2011 song “MegaMan” Weezy rapped,\n\nBirdman Junior\nGot the world in my wingspan", "annotation_id": "14021597", "url": "https://genius.com/14021597", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "As your eyes close and mines bleed I need Vizine", "vault_lines": ["As your eyes close and mines bleed"], "meaning": "Visine (Stylized at “Vizine” by Wayne) are a brand of eyedrops produced by pharmaceutical company Johnson & Johnson.\n\nEye drops are typically used to lubricate dry eyes and provide relief to those suffering from allergic pink eye.\n\nOn “Vizine,” Wayne brings up his eyesight problems during the chorus.\n\nWhy can’t I trust what I see?\n\nAnd in the outro:\n\nTry me, why do I see these bloody bodies?\nWhy do I love what I see?\nWhy me? Why oh-why-oh why me?", "annotation_id": "14016241", "url": "https://genius.com/14016241", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "On turbo, you don’t wan’ disturb him Momma put too much sugar in my Gerber", "vault_lines": ["On turbo, you don't wan' disturb him"], "meaning": "Gerber is a baby food company, made for babies and toddlers not relying on pure liquid food. Lil Wayne talks about how his mom put sugar in his Gerber, which normally would make a kid “Hyper.” However, for Wayne it made him “Turbo” or at a pace where you can’t reach him.", "annotation_id": "14015362", "url": "https://genius.com/14015362", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "And I never feel like poor me I’d rather feel like the old me", "vault_lines": ["And I never feel like poor me"], "meaning": "Wayne uses a double entendre when he says “I never feel like poor me.” He is saying that he never feels poor, while also never feeling pity upon himself (poor me).\n\n\n\nSaying “I’d would rather feel like the old me” could be self-reflection upon his past when he was the “Best Rapper Alive,” and able to sell a million records the first week.\n\nHe could also mean that he wants to be able to release music (albums, specifically) freely like he used to before his issues with Cash Money and Birdman.", "annotation_id": "14015334", "url": "https://genius.com/14015334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Bad brown bitch, Lisa Turtle", "vault_lines": ["Bad brown bitch, Lisa Turtle"], "meaning": "This a reference to the fictional charcater Lisa Turtle who appeared in movies such as Saved by the bell & Good Morning, Miss Bliss and was played by actress Lark Voorhies.\n\nWayne had previously made references to Lisa Turtle in his Dedication 4 song “The Burn”  on which he made a reference to the movie “Saved by the bell”\n\nThat lean got me slow as Lisa Turtle ask Screech\n\nand also later in his No Ceilings remix of “Where Ya At”:\n\nI’m movin' slow, Lisa Turtle, Lark Voorhies", "annotation_id": "14014901", "url": "https://genius.com/14014901", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Tunechi like Pablo, kis in the rowboat Tunechi in the yacht though, still don’t showboat Sippin’ on the slowpoke, movin’ like a tugboat Smokin’ like a steamboat, fuckin’ on a love boat", "vault_lines": ["Tunechi like Pablo, kis in the rowboat"], "meaning": "Tunechi is comapring himself to Pablo as in Pablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria who was a Colombian drug lord and narcoterrorist. His cartel supplied an estimated 80% of the cocaine smuggled into the United States.\n\nLil Wayne has a net worth of approximately $140 million, and even with all this money and assets, Wayne doesn’t show off or showboat.\n\nHe refers to lean or codeine in most of his songs, and this one is no different. He states that he sips lean (slowpoke) which makes him move like a tugboat. A tugboat is a powerful boat used for towing larger vessels, especially in harbor. This boat is extremely slow due to its weight.\n\n\n\nLastly, he says he smokes so much like a steamboat referring to the fumes. Love boat is marijuana dipped in formaldehyde or PCP, making a connection with the fumes that he mentioned before.", "annotation_id": "14012846", "url": "https://genius.com/14012846", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Cocaine price low, call that a low blow", "vault_lines": ["Cocaine price low, call that a low blow"], "meaning": "Cocaine is often referred to as “Blow”. Wayne says that he gets Cocaine, or blow, for a very low cost. Hence the reason why he calls it a “low blow” which is also an illegal blow that strikes below an opponent’s waist, in other words, a punch to the crotch area.", "annotation_id": "14012763", "url": "https://genius.com/14012763", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Young Mula, bitch, I die for the logo", "vault_lines": ["Young Mula, bitch, I die for the logo"], "meaning": "Wayne always says that he would die for his brand, Young Money (or as he sometimes calls it, Young Mula. “Mula” means money).  Young Money’s president is Lil Wayne’s lifelong friend Mack Maine. The label is an imprint of Cash Money Records and is distributed by Republic Records.", "annotation_id": "14012742", "url": "https://genius.com/14012742", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Big Bs, nigga, honor roll", "vault_lines": ["Big Bs, nigga, honor roll"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne says that he has big B’s as in his blood gang members. However, he makes a connection with getting B’s as grades, placing him on the honor roll which is for students who have been recognized for their academic achievement on lists published periodically throughout the school year. This can also be interpreted as his Bloods are excellent at what they do.", "annotation_id": "14012635", "url": "https://genius.com/14012635", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "I’m the GOAT nigga, no goatee", "vault_lines": ["I'm the GOAT nigga, no goatee"], "meaning": "GOAT is an acronym for Greatest Of All Time. By many artists and music listeners in general, Lil Wayne is considered the greatest of all time at what he does. With over 25 years in the rap game and over 20 millions albums sold, Weezy claims that title, and rightfully so.\n\nHe then uses a play on words with goat and goatee. He did actually have a goatee during his 20’s and early 30’s, but he shaved it in 2014 and got an “eye” tattoo on his chin.", "annotation_id": "14012506", "url": "https://genius.com/14012506", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "On my face, I tatted me a fleur-de-lis Should I get it covered with a coca leaf?", "vault_lines": ["On my face, I tatted me a fleur-de-lis"], "meaning": "There are various versions of the Fleur de Lis, but generally it looks something like this.\n\nNew Orleans, Louisiana, where Wayne is from, also uses this symbol as a nod to the city’s French heritage. Wayne has the Fleur de Lis tattooed on his right cheek, paying homage to his beloved hometown.\n\n\n\nIn his recent music, he has heavily been indicating that he uses cocaine. This line implies that his addiction is so strong that he is willing to tattoo him a coca leaf over his Fleur de Lis tattoo, embracing the drug and rejecting his identity.", "annotation_id": "14011846", "url": "https://genius.com/14011846", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Why me? Oh why, oh why, oh, why me? Why do I look up and see my feet? Am I rising? But why do my peak Look like it’s out of my reach? Why can’t I trust what I see?", "vault_lines": ["Oh why, oh why, oh, why me?"], "meaning": "Wayne paints us a picture very accurately here.\n\nWayne used to be at the peak of his career from 2007 all the way to 2011, having two major releases (Tha Carter III in 2008 and Tha Carter IV in 2011 – both of which were multiple-platinum) and also having numerous songs on the charts, whether he was the main artist or was featured.\n\nBusiness with his label, Cash Money, started falling apart in mid-2014 and he openly expressed his frustration towards his label on December 04 of the same year.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/LilTunechi/status/540569858287493120?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw&ref_url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.complex.com%2Fmusic%2Flil-wayne-cash-money-beef-timeline%2F\n\nAlthough he has released plenty of music during the drama, he hasn’t and can’t have any major releases anymore without the label’s support.\n\nWayne wonders “why me?”, as he has generated a lot of money for Cash Money, by his very successful career, and, also, by birthing Drake and Nicki’s careers – both of which have been very successful as well.\n\nWayne looks up and sees his legs, implying that he is actually falling down rather than rising up – people have been claiming that Wayne has “fallen off” as far as his career and music’s quality go.\n\nAs time passes by, his label situation isn’t getting any better and therefore his career stays stagnated, Wayne is constantly straying from his triumph era.\n\nAs an artist, who isn’t allowed to express himself and as entrepreneur, watching his businesses failing, Wayne has become very suspicious with the people around him. The last line could also mean that he is in denial, he does not accept the possibility that he is finished with the Hip Hop scene. In both cases, he needs Visine (stylized “Vizine” by Wayne), which is an eye drops brand, just to clear things up for him.", "annotation_id": "14011615", "url": "https://genius.com/14011615", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Try me Why do I see these bloody bodies? Why do I love what I see?", "vault_lines": ["Oh why, oh why, oh, why me?"], "meaning": "Only horrible things can happen to those who question Wayne. In fact, Wayne admires the results of his enemies questioning him, as he also rapped on “Street Chains” off of the Free Weezy Album;\n\nI pull up and paint your whole fucking block red\nThen get out of my car\nAnd admire my art\nThen smile at my thoughts", "annotation_id": "14011587", "url": "https://genius.com/14011587", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Oh, why am I so kamikaze? Why can’t I stay in my seat? I’m so hyphy", "vault_lines": ["Oh why, oh why, oh, why me?"], "meaning": "Wayne has been a very active user of drugs such as marijuana, cocaine and codeine syrup. Although lean doesn’t make you hype, cocaine definitely does by increasing your heart rate.\n\nAfter quite a few epileptic seizures, most recently in September 2017, and despite many efforts having been made for him to “clean up,” Wayne here acknowledges that he is still addicted to harmful substances. After all, he did say it is hard to stop.", "annotation_id": "14011510", "url": "https://genius.com/14011510", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "Dressed in all red like Olajuwon", "vault_lines": ["Dressed in all red like Olajuwon"], "meaning": "Hakeem Olajuwon, a prolific basketball player and Hall Of Famer, played for the Houston Rockets who are affiliated with the color red in their logo and team jerseys. Wayne being dressed in all red discloses his affiliation with the “blood” gang.", "annotation_id": "14011507", "url": "https://genius.com/14011507", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Vizine", "line": "No one man should have all that power If he can’t afford to pay the light bill", "vault_lines": ["No one man should have all that power"], "meaning": "“No one man should have all that power” is an interpolation of a Kanye West bar from his 2010 song “Power,” but it’s also a jab at Cash Money boss Birdman. Wayne’s former friend and mentor lost his Miami mansion due to unpaid loans, which Wayne seems to be referencing with his “light bill” line.\n\nBirdman allegedly ripped off Drake, Nicki Minaj, Tyga and he still hasn’t paid Wayne for his long-awaited Tha Carter V album.", "annotation_id": "14009180", "url": "https://genius.com/14009180", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
