{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Way more gully gully than buddy buddy Never needed your acceptance, never needed nothin’", "vault_lines": ["Way more gully gully than buddy buddy"], "meaning": "This diss could be a possible jab at famous hip-hop enthusiast, Joe Budden. Drake has gone after Budden multiple times including in his songs  4pm In Calabasas  &  No Shopping  with French Montana where Drake says\n\nPump, pump, pump it up (Skrrt)\nShe got a good head on her, but I pump it up (Skrrt)\nI’m not a one hit wonder, they know all my stuff (Haan)\nYou let me turn into the nigga that you almost was”\n\nBudden fully explained the beef to Hot 97’s Ebro Darden back in 2016.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/5G_EwfZ85Wk", "annotation_id": "25290424", "url": "https://genius.com/25290424", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Make sure the plane got a phone now So when we ’bout to land I can call to tell the woes I’m home now", "vault_lines": ["Make sure the plane got a phone now"], "meaning": "In an interview with Noisey, Toronto rapper and OVO Sound affiliate Devontee defines the meaning behind his and Drake’s ‘woes’:\n\n“Woe is my crew. It stands for ‘working on excellence.’ It’s just my whole brand and my whole movement and my way of life for everyone. I want everyone to work on excellence. So, all my friends are my Woes and I feel anybody working on excellence in life is a Woe in life as well.”\n\nWhen Drake returns to his home in the six (Toronto), he’s able to call his crew to get together.", "annotation_id": "20086446", "url": "https://genius.com/20086446", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "[Produced by WondaGurl]", "vault_lines": ["Jewels look like I found a motherfuckin' treasure map"], "meaning": "In an episode of The Creator Class' ORIGINALS series, WondaGurl unveiled the sample (Marcello Giombini’s “Synthotango”) and how she sent this beat to Drake:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/J7unBlyNGeg?t=137", "annotation_id": "8338874", "url": "https://genius.com/8338874", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Y’all don’t understand, I’m the one they want to hear from Y’all don’t understand that it’s me or nothin’", "vault_lines": ["Y'all don't understand, I'm the one they want to hear from"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the beef between Drake and Diddy. Originally, Diddy wanted to use the beat that Boi-1da had sent him. However, Drake quickly hopped on the beat, and released 0 to 100/The Catch Up, which infuriated Diddy, causing him to punch Drake.\n\n\n\nDrake responds here by saying that the people would rather hear from him than anyone else, being that he is a legend.\n\nAnd just like every single other thing in my life, you can have her when I’m finished", "annotation_id": "4920497", "url": "https://genius.com/4920497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Shaq postin’ up on niggas that I used to have posters of", "vault_lines": ["Shaq postin' up on niggas that I used to have posters of"], "meaning": "This line might refer to the retired basketball player Shaquille O'Neal, who was known for his dominant plays in the post.\n\nDrake is a big fan of basketball and is currently working as the Global Ambassador of his hometown team in the NBA: Toronto Raptors.\n\n(e.g., Shaq with some post moves)\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MxZQw-dEydU\n\nHe could also be referring to the fact that the same artists that have influenced his drive for music (e.g., Kanye West and Jay-Z) are currently the same individuals he is up against in Hip-Hop, and possibly will overcome towards the latter of his career.", "annotation_id": "4898112", "url": "https://genius.com/4898112", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Real quick, man, you couldn’t have hated that Let’s be real, nigga, you couldn’t have made it that, woo!", "vault_lines": ["Real quick, man, you couldn't have hated that"], "meaning": "It is rumored that Diddy smacked Drake over the song “0 to 100.” In the chorus he says, “0 to 100 nigga real quick,” because Diddy gave the instrumental to Drake to write something for him to rap over, but instead he put it out for himself.\n\nHere, Drake refers to the controversy saying that Diddy couldn’t have hated the move he made because he couldn’t have made such an amazing record on his own.", "annotation_id": "4891897", "url": "https://genius.com/4891897", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Yeah, I’m fuckin’ glowin’ up", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm fuckin' glowin' up"], "meaning": "Drake is not only glowin' up in the rap industry, he’s outshining everyone else around him. Recently, Drake has been dominating the game and with the recent release of If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, Drake is further emphasizing his dominance.\n\nThis is also more than likely a reference to the Glo Up Challenge on Twitter where you post pictures of your past and compare them to the current.\n\nYeah, you ain’t really fuck with me way back then, girl but How Bout Now?\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to Chief Keef and his Glo Gang.", "annotation_id": "4888712", "url": "https://genius.com/4888712", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "[Verse 2: Drake]", "vault_lines": ["Jewels look like I found a motherfuckin' treasure map"], "meaning": "Although this song appeared on both Lil Wayne’s Sorry 4 The Wait 2 and Drake’s If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, this verse was only featured on the latter.", "annotation_id": "4888426", "url": "https://genius.com/4888426", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "I be with the bands Like a nigga went to Jackson State or Grambling Young Nick Cannon with the snare drum, dancin’ Watch the way I handle it, uh", "vault_lines": ["I be with the bands"], "meaning": "We were not really feeling this line…AT ALL", "annotation_id": "4768262", "url": "https://genius.com/4768262", "state": "verified", "votes": -854, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Trap pumpin’ all night like Chevron", "vault_lines": ["Trap pumpin' all night like Chevron"], "meaning": "Chevron is an energy corporation that owns gas stations. The “trap” can refer to Wayne’s trap music, or his trap house. Regardless, the trap is pumping like the gas pumped at a Chevron gas station – 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.", "annotation_id": "4767972", "url": "https://genius.com/4767972", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Boy, do I smell beef? Mmmm, pheromones", "vault_lines": ["Boy, do I smell beef? Mmmm, pheromones"], "meaning": "Beef is the common term for a fight or feud and a lot of animals are attracted to each other by chemical releases called pheromones. Weezy is saying that rather than run from it, he loves a good fight; anyone who wants to beef with him had better beware.", "annotation_id": "4761331", "url": "https://genius.com/4761331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Promethazine over pinot", "vault_lines": ["Jewels look like I found a motherfuckin' treasure map"], "meaning": "Wayne prefers promethazine, an ingredient in lean, which he was strongly addicted to, rather than wine – pinot is a grape used to make wine.", "annotation_id": "4761277", "url": "https://genius.com/4761277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Goin’ at a nigga throat like a herringbone", "vault_lines": ["Goin' at a nigga throat like a herringbone"], "meaning": "Herringbone is a weaving pattern in fabric, typically seen in neckties (or chains). Blood would splatter all over the herringbone tie if the wearer’s throat was slashed. Manners, Wayne.", "annotation_id": "4761061", "url": "https://genius.com/4761061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "I got, mind control over Deebo", "vault_lines": ["I got, mind control over Deebo"], "meaning": "A reference to the classic film, Friday.\n\nhttp://m.youtube.com/watch?v=Kw31fE_AWsI", "annotation_id": "4760645", "url": "https://genius.com/4760645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Shout out to the G’s from the ends We don’t love no girls from the ends", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to the G's from the ends"], "meaning": "In a nod to Skepta’s 2014 banger “That’s Not Me”, Drake informs the listener that even neighborhood girls don’t get love from him or his crew.\n\n“Ends” is a London slang term for “neighborhood”, as referenced in Skepta’s track. This can also apply to Drake because in Toronto they refer to different parts of the city as the “East End and West End” of the city.\n\nDrake credits Skepta as an inspiration in the album notes for If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late, and has praised the artist in a series of recent Instagram posts.", "annotation_id": "4760266", "url": "https://genius.com/4760266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "When you get to where the fuck I’m at You gotta remind ’em about where you been About all of the money that done came and went About the two cents I ain’t never spent When they say you’re too famous to pack a gat I gotta remind ’em about where I’m from", "vault_lines": ["And ain't told no one where the fuck it's at"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is from Hollygrove, New Orleans, where the crime rate is high. Due to the high crime rate, it’s a no-brainer that he knows how to handle a gat (slang for gun).\n\nNow, Wayne is sitting on $140 million in net worth. He looks at his current state of living, and compares it to when he was growing up.\n\n\n\nThe idiom two cents is used to describe what someone has to put in with their wisdom. Wayne uses the idiom to say that he has never told somebody how to do their job, so they should not tell him how to do his! This could be in reference to the Cash Money beef.", "annotation_id": "4760032", "url": "https://genius.com/4760032", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "They gon’ say your name on them airwaves They gon’ hit you up right after like it’s only rap", "vault_lines": ["They gon' say your name on them airwaves"], "meaning": "This could be a shot at Kendrick Lamar, who called out Drake, along with many other rappers,  in his blown up “Control” verse. In the track, Kendrick mentioned that in the end, it’s all just rap.\n\nCould also  be a subliminal at Chris Brown who said during an interview with Sway:\n\nIt’s rap man, it’s hip-hop. I say whatever I want, I’m not a rapper, so you gonna be stupid rapping back to a singer, but I'ma keep coming\n\nhttps://youtu.be/vQdL6XROV90?t=1m5s\n\nDrake and Chris Brown have been beefing for a while.", "annotation_id": "4759707", "url": "https://genius.com/4759707", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Only see the truth when I’m staring in the mirror Lookin’ at myself like, there it is there Yeah, like there it is there man, whoo!", "vault_lines": ["Only see the truth when I'm staring in the mirror"], "meaning": "Drizzy alludes to the popular song named “Whoomp! (There It Is)” by Tag Team.\n\nLooking for the truth in a mirror is a waste of time with Drake; it’s standing there right in front of him!", "annotation_id": "4759149", "url": "https://genius.com/4759149", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "And when my bloods start shootin’ that’s B-roll, bitch", "vault_lines": ["And when my bloods start shootin' that's B-roll, bitch"], "meaning": "B-roll is a production term used in film theatres hence the reference to “shooting” both in the sense of making a movie (or making it big) or defending your clique by firing back.\n\nB is also a blood type.", "annotation_id": "4758844", "url": "https://genius.com/4758844", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "I ain’t tryna chance it,", "vault_lines": ["I ain't tryna chance it,"], "meaning": "As Drake said in “Draft Day”:\n\nAnd if I left this shit to chance I would’ve picked a name like Chance the Rapper", "annotation_id": "4758824", "url": "https://genius.com/4758824", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Yeah—sound, sound, sound Yeah, real 6 side shit Sickos, ah man", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, real 6 side shit"], "meaning": "Drizzy sets the tone by shouting out his label OVO Sound and his home town, Toronto (416).\n\nComing from the 6 side", "annotation_id": "4758795", "url": "https://genius.com/4758795", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "I was only five, but still remember the drought in ’87", "vault_lines": ["I was only five, but still remember the drought in '87"], "meaning": "In 1987 the United States suffered a terrible drought.\n\nHowever Lil Wayne is probably referring a series of mix tapes he released earlier during his career called Da Drought. Although he was only five in 1987 he had already seen things that left an impression in his life forever and he incorporated these memories in his music. He has always been a young visionary!", "annotation_id": "4756799", "url": "https://genius.com/4756799", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "Suck a nigga dick for a iPhone 6", "vault_lines": ["Suck a nigga dick for a iPhone 6"], "meaning": "A reference to the classic line in DJ Jimi’s “Where They At”\n\nI said BITCH! stop talking that shit\nAnd suck a nigga dick for an outfit\n\nWeezy previously (and famously) referred to this line on French Montana’s “Pop That”\n\nBitch! Stop talkin' that shit\nAnd suck a nigga dick for some Trukfit", "annotation_id": "4756536", "url": "https://genius.com/4756536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 106, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "You can get buried for a ounce of Katy Perry", "vault_lines": ["You can get buried for a ounce of Katy Perry"], "meaning": "Is Wayne really talking about RiFF RAFF’s girl, when he is on the intro and outro?\n\nAnyway, an ounce of Katy Perry refers to an ounce of cocaine, as Katy Perry is a white girl, and “white” is a commonly used term when talking about cocaine.", "annotation_id": "4756420", "url": "https://genius.com/4756420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "I ain’t felt the pressure in a little while It’s gonna take some getting used to (What you say?) Floatin’ all through the city with the windows down Puttin’ on like I used to (What you say?) They never told me when you get the crown It’s gon’ take some getting used to (What you say?) New friends all in their old feelings now They don’t love you like they used to, man", "vault_lines": ["I be with the bands"], "meaning": "Unlike The Throne, Drake feels like no one’s pressuring him musically. Since every song really is Drake featuring Drake – he can afford to forget about Jay Z & Kendrick Lamar’s lyrical jabs.\n\nKeeping the same “No New Friends” policy keeps Drake fleek – those “do you remember me?” acquaintances just get shrugged off.\n\nShe says they miss the old Drake, girl don’t tempt me", "annotation_id": "4756367", "url": "https://genius.com/4756367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "I’m gon hit ’em with the wham once again I’ma always end up as a man in the end, dog It’s just apparent every year", "vault_lines": ["I'm gon hit 'em with the wham once again"], "meaning": "To wham someone is to verbally assault them. Drizzy’s coming for the number one spot in 2015, just like he did in 2014, and 2013.\n\nDuring an interview with GQ Magazine, Kanye West had this to say about Drake’s success in 2013:\n\n“[Drake]. He got last summer. And I’d never given it up till last summer.” Now he’s thinking about taking it back. “It’s a real question for me. Do I want to?”\n\nAdditionally, “gon hi'em with the wham once again” could allude to Drake deploying the vegetarian ham replacement ‘Wham\" as a weapon. This effectively captures Drake’s defining characteristics (can go hard but is obviously a softy deep down, hence the meat replacement..)", "annotation_id": "4756352", "url": "https://genius.com/4756352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "line": "I be with the bands Like a nigga went to Jackson State or Grambling Young Nick Cannon with the snare drum, dancin’ Watch the way I handle it, uh", "vault_lines": ["I be with the bands"], "meaning": "On “Believe Me”, Drake made a similar connection between his money and rock bands Nirvana and Coldplay. In this line he takes a new approach: college marching bands.\n\nJackson State, Grambling, Clark-Atlanta and Hampton University are all HBCUs with renowned marching bands. Jackson State even used Drake’s “Trophies” in one of their performances.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L8Db1WJ5-58\n\nTopping off this set of lines is another college band reference: the 2002 film, Drumline, where Nick Cannon plays the role of a talented drummer from Harlem who was recruited to play for the fictional Atlanta A&T University.", "annotation_id": "4756244", "url": "https://genius.com/4756244", "state": "accepted", "votes": 206, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used To", "status": "done", "targets": 31, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-used-to-lyrics", "genius_title": "Used To", "genius_id": 685070, "source": "api"}
