{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "My finger sitting on the trigger like a La-Z-Boy", "vault_lines": ["My finger sitting on the trigger like a La-Z-Boy"], "meaning": "Wayne is comparing the motion of pulling a trigger to pushing down/closing the leg rest of a la-z-boy with your legs", "annotation_id": "25316782", "url": "https://genius.com/25316782", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "And you still ain’t seen a fucking thing until you C5", "vault_lines": ["And you still ain't seen a fucking thing until you C5"], "meaning": "The name of his album, Tha Carter V, is often shorten “C5” in the different tracks.\n\nHere, Wayne make a wordplay with the letter C which we can hear see, so the verse have a meaning : you never seen anything until you listened to Tha Carter V", "annotation_id": "19929716", "url": "https://genius.com/19929716", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "Keep it on the B-side", "vault_lines": ["Keep it on the East Side, keep it on the B-side"], "meaning": "B-Side like B-Side tape or B-Side like the gang Blood’s side since Wayne is a Blood.", "annotation_id": "19686277", "url": "https://genius.com/19686277", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "But he be talking out his ass, what is all that shit about?", "vault_lines": ["But he be talking out his ass, what is all that shit about?"], "meaning": "Clever double entendre.\n\nWhen someone ‘talks out their ass’, it means they are making a false claim. In other words, they’re talking shit.\n\nWayne takes this a step further and implies his enemy is literally talking shit, because he’s speaking out his ass.", "annotation_id": "15652307", "url": "https://genius.com/15652307", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "With my bros will smoke you niggas, like we grow you niggas", "vault_lines": ["With my bros will smoke you niggas, like we grow you niggas"], "meaning": "Wayne plays on the word “smoke” saying that he and his goons will kill (smoke) you niggas like they smoke weed, which is grown.", "annotation_id": "15606805", "url": "https://genius.com/15606805", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "Percocet, promethazine, you can call me P-Rod", "vault_lines": ["Percocet, promethazine, you can call me P-Rod"], "meaning": "Wayne has been known to be an avid skateboarder, and he even has his own private skatepark.\n\n\n\nHere Wheezy could be referencing the professional skateboarder Paul Rodriguez Jr., who goes by the nickname “P-Rod”. He says that he does so many drugs that start with the letter “P” that you can refer to him as Paul Rodriguez.", "annotation_id": "15535079", "url": "https://genius.com/15535079", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "I tried to turn the fuckin’ page, oh my God, I ripped it out", "vault_lines": ["I tried to turn the fuckin' page, oh my God, I ripped it out"], "meaning": "Continuing on from the other Pusha T jabs. Wayne is saying that he tried to move on from their old beef, if you remember in 2012, they had a bit of beef. Well he tried to move on, but recently Pusha T has been sending shots at his team. Which has made it difficult for Wayne to move on. Instead he ripped the page out", "annotation_id": "15518700", "url": "https://genius.com/15518700", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "And you still ain’t seen a fucking thing until you C5", "vault_lines": ["And you still ain't seen a fucking thing until you C5"], "meaning": "Even if you had 3 eyes, as Weezy said on the previous line, you have never really seen anything quite like “Tha Carter V” album.\n\nWayne has clever wordplay here on the letter C and the word “See”.", "annotation_id": "15497445", "url": "https://genius.com/15497445", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "They in it too, and bitches too, they mention you, they dentures loose Run up in a nigga house, pistol in that nigga mouth Safe code now, nigga, cough it up or spit it out", "vault_lines": ["They in it too, and bitches too, they mention you, they dentures loose"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is simultaneously saying  that he will steal Pusha T’s wealth and that the people who mention Pusha T are only mentioning him because Pusha is sucking up to them (or how Wayne says sucking their dick).\n\nHe does this by making the word dentures sound like “dick is” ,pistol sounding like “pissed all” , and safe code being the equivalent of “safe word”.", "annotation_id": "15486565", "url": "https://genius.com/15486565", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "Taking shots at my team, you must be getting senile You goin’ at my slime then you’re going at me, slime", "vault_lines": ["Taking shots at my team, you must be getting senile"], "meaning": "Pusha-T dissed Young Money back in 2012 on his song “Exodus 23:1.” Also, Pusha is older than Wayne, which explains why he would be getting senile.\n\nDrake and Pusha-T also reignited their long beef in the summer of 2018. Wayne is defending Drake, who he has been allied with for many years.\n\nThis line also may be a sub aimed at Young Thug, because of his connection to the word slime. Young Thug and Wayne have a history of beef, with Thug taunting Wayne by naming one of his mixtapes Barter 6, and allegedly sending shooters to target Wanye.", "annotation_id": "15481731", "url": "https://genius.com/15481731", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "Put his words on the plate, that nigga ate ’em all", "vault_lines": ["Put his words on the plate, that nigga ate 'em all"], "meaning": "“Eating one’s words” is an expression that means that someone is redacting something that they have previously said.\n\nWayne uses clever wordplay to show how someone redacted something they had previously said when he confronted them with it. He does this by painting the picture that he literally served them a meal of their own words and they ate them all, leaving nothing on the plate.", "annotation_id": "15476096", "url": "https://genius.com/15476096", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "Rock your bells, LL, nigga locked and loaded, uh", "vault_lines": ["Rock your bells, LL, nigga locked and loaded, uh"], "meaning": "Wayne will shoot you in the head and metaphorically ‘rock your bells’.\n\n“LL” refers to LL Cool J who had a top-20 song in 1986 called “Rock The Bells”.\n\n“LL” also stands for locked and loaded in this case, as Wayne uses that abbreviation to connect these two lines.", "annotation_id": "15474909", "url": "https://genius.com/15474909", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "I remember you, I was never into you I tell my shooters, shoot you and whoever resemble you", "vault_lines": ["I remember you, I was never into you"], "meaning": "Potential diss to Pusha-T, jumping in on the Pusha vs. Drake beef.\n\nSimilar to Drake’s line in Duppy Freestyle about Pusha’s signature fading resembling his career.\n\nhttps://genius.com/14646254", "annotation_id": "15474896", "url": "https://genius.com/15474896", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "Shoot ya in ya head, give ya ass three eyes", "vault_lines": ["Shoot ya in ya head, give ya ass three eyes"], "meaning": "The ‘third eye’ is usually perceived to be above your eyebrows in between your eyes. This is also a good place to shoot someone, and a hole there will make you look like you have a third eye", "annotation_id": "15474890", "url": "https://genius.com/15474890", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "It’s money over bitches ’till the day I dizz-ie", "vault_lines": ["It's money over bitches 'till the day I dizz-ie"], "meaning": "“Money Over Bitches” is one of Wayne’s signature phrases. He even recorded a song called “M.O.B.”\n\nWayne loves the phrase so much that he even made it one of his most prominent tattoos.", "annotation_id": "15474879", "url": "https://genius.com/15474879", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "I feel like Ivan Drago, if he dies, he dies", "vault_lines": ["I feel like Ivan Drago, if he dies, he dies"], "meaning": "Ivan Drago is the villain in Rocky IV, played by Dolph Lundgren. He is a Russian boxer of tremendous size and strength. In one of the series' most infamous moments, he said, “If he dies, he dies” about Apollo Creed after defeating him. Creed ended up succumbing to his injuries and died in the ring.\n\n\n\nWayne likens himself to the fictional fighter because he would not lose sleep if his enemies died.", "annotation_id": "15468181", "url": "https://genius.com/15468181", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "line": "I still don’t know today Was he playing with the gun or was it an accident I still, I just don’t, I-I be wanting to ask him but I never asked him after all these years if- Was that a accident or did he- or was he playing with the gun So I never really found out about what, you know What happ- what really happened with him and that shooting", "vault_lines": ["I remember you, I was never into you"], "meaning": "Wayne’s mother is speaking about Wayne’s attempted suicide at age 12. When Wayne’s mother told him that he couldn’t rap anymore, Wayne pointed a gun at his chest and shot himself, just missing his heart. He has referenced this event in some other verses of his such as his verse on Solange’s 2016 track “Mad,”\n\nAnd when I attempted suicide, I didn’t die\nI remember how mad I was on that day\nMan, you gotta let it go before it get up in the way\nLet it go, let it go\n\nand he also mentions it on the album’s closer “Let It All Work Out.”", "annotation_id": "15468158", "url": "https://genius.com/15468158", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Used 2", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-used-2-lyrics", "genius_title": "Used 2", "genius_id": 3985344, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "line": "Shawty, I can carry you like Mariah", "vault_lines": ["Shawty, I can carry you like Mariah"], "meaning": "A reference to Mariah carey, who is larger than Lil' Wayne and thus an impressive show of strength as well.\n\nOf course Weezy is playing off of Mariahs name, “Carry” and “carey” sound very similar.", "annotation_id": "351663", "url": "https://genius.com/351663", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "line": "He can spend money but we can spend a day lil mama", "vault_lines": ["He can spend money but we can spend a day lil mama"], "meaning": "Unlike Trey Songz Lil' Wayne would rather spend quality time with a woman than whip out his pocketbook.", "annotation_id": "351661", "url": "https://genius.com/351661", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "line": "I married Snow White ’cause she make me cream", "vault_lines": ["I married Snow White 'cause she make me cream"], "meaning": "A heartfelt double entendre, Shawty Lo uses Snow White as a metaphor for cocaine and explains that she is making him money, making him cum, or both.", "annotation_id": "351660", "url": "https://genius.com/351660", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "line": "I got diamonds round my neck like a baseball field", "vault_lines": ["I got diamonds round my neck like a baseball field"], "meaning": "While most rappers carry fairly impressive, gaudy chains (see: Gucci Mane), Shawty Lo opts instead for the rarely sighted four-diamond chain.", "annotation_id": "351659", "url": "https://genius.com/351659", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "line": "You know I ain’t Sonny Bono but I play with them skis", "vault_lines": ["You know I ain't Sonny Bono but I play with them skis"], "meaning": "Most famously referred to on Eminem’s “Who Knew”, Sonny Bono died while skiing in Nevada in 1998. Shawty Lo is claiming he will not be killed while navigating the slopes of cocaine distribution.", "annotation_id": "351655", "url": "https://genius.com/351655", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "line": "I ain’t talkin’ ’bout Alicia, but I play with them keys", "vault_lines": ["I ain't talkin' 'bout Alicia, but I play with them keys"], "meaning": "A reference to Alicia Keys, who has nothing to do with drug smuggling anyway.", "annotation_id": "351653", "url": "https://genius.com/351653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "line": "Say that he’s king, well he’s a God damn liar", "vault_lines": ["Say that he's king, well he's a God damn liar"], "meaning": "An offhand insult to T.I., who has been involved in beef with Shawty Lo dating back several years.", "annotation_id": "351645", "url": "https://genius.com/351645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "line": "Yeah, I’m a supplier Call me Marlow ’cause I’m off the wire", "vault_lines": ["Call me Marlow 'cause I'm off the wire"], "meaning": "A reference to Marlo Stanfield from HBO’s The Wire. Factually inacurate as Marlo was on the wire (aka his phones were tapped) throughout his time on the series. This implies Shawty Lo may both be quick to speak on things he’s uninformed about and a dangerous person with which to make drug deals.", "annotation_id": "351642", "url": "https://genius.com/351642", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "line": "I got something that they need, I got something that they want I got something that they need, call me the supplier", "vault_lines": ["I got something that they need, I got something that they want"], "meaning": "Trey Songz assumes women want his dick and that he’s turned a clever phrase, but really what they want and need is his money because they don’t keep regular jobs. They just hustle rap stars and athletes.", "annotation_id": "351638", "url": "https://genius.com/351638", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ur Supplier", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Shawty-lo-ur-supplier-lyrics", "genius_title": "Ur Supplier", "genius_id": 55348, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Time to shake these haters off like the skin of a locust", "vault_lines": ["Time to shake these haters off like the skin of a locust"], "meaning": "A locust is an insect that sheds and shakes off its outer layer as it grows. Bun B is comparing haters to something he can simply shake off and leave behind.", "annotation_id": "40763648", "url": "https://genius.com/40763648", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Yeah, wrong way down a one-way", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, wrong way down a one-way"], "meaning": "Drake uses driving down a one-way as a metaphor to explain how he feels like he is going against everyone’s expectations and ignoring the path expected for him to follow. Many people didn’t think a Canadian actor from Degrassi would become one of the biggest rap artists of all time.", "annotation_id": "40763580", "url": "https://genius.com/40763580", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Weezy in ya mouth, now, Weezy what you talkin’ ’bout?", "vault_lines": ["Weezy in ya mouth, now, Weezy what you talkin' 'bout?"], "meaning": "reference to Weezer", "annotation_id": "38667938", "url": "https://genius.com/38667938", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Guess I might, know what? Fuck it, yes I will", "vault_lines": ["Guess I might, know what? Fuck it, yes I will"], "meaning": "Perhaps a subtle foreshadowing of Lil Wayne and Drake’s 2011 hit, She Will", "annotation_id": "36979349", "url": "https://genius.com/36979349", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Loc-ness", "vault_lines": ["The gangsta recognize me for my loc-ness"], "meaning": "Referring to ‘loco’ meaning crazy, therefore he’s recognizable for his craziness.", "annotation_id": "29520460", "url": "https://genius.com/29520460", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "That’s why I never follow y’all suggestions I just always did my own thing", "vault_lines": ["That's why I never follow y'all suggestions"], "meaning": "Drake didn’t allow other people to change him, and through that he found success", "annotation_id": "22262359", "url": "https://genius.com/22262359", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "’Cause you the type to lose her, and I’m about to get her", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you the type to lose her, and I'm about to get her"], "meaning": "Drake can get any girl that you can’t", "annotation_id": "22262341", "url": "https://genius.com/22262341", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Shit been goin’ good, but good can turn to better", "vault_lines": ["Shit been goin' good, but good can turn to better"], "meaning": "Drake is always improving himself, although he is arguably always at the top of the game", "annotation_id": "22262335", "url": "https://genius.com/22262335", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I’ll erase you like I drew you, bitch", "vault_lines": [", I'll erase you like I drew you, bitch"], "meaning": "Wayne could destroy you/your career as if he made it", "annotation_id": "22262320", "url": "https://genius.com/22262320", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "And now I’m on that rock shit", "vault_lines": ["And now I'm on that rock shit"], "meaning": "At this time, Lil Wayne tried to incorporate more elements of rock music into his sound.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lTurfn7vvCk\n\nIn 2010, he Wayne released his “rock” album Rebirth.", "annotation_id": "19125102", "url": "https://genius.com/19125102", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I drive two black cars, I named ’em Malcolm X and Martin Luther", "vault_lines": ["I drive two black cars, I named 'em Malcolm X and Martin Luther"], "meaning": "Drake boasts his affluence by drawing a comparison between his multiple cars and two of the most important figures of the civil rights movement, Martin Luther King, Jr., and Malcolm X. Both activists for desegregation and equal-race rights, King and Malcolm X are considered some of the most impactful figures of the civil rights struggle.\n\n\n\nLater in 2009, Drake would make another reference to Malcolm X on his hit single “Forever”:\n\nLabels want my name beside a X, like Malcolm\nEverybody got a deal, I did it without one", "annotation_id": "19092908", "url": "https://genius.com/19092908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "From the free-throw line and slam it down like I’m the great Mike", "vault_lines": ["From the free-throw line and slam it down like I'm the great Mike"], "meaning": "Michael Jordan is a retired NBA basketball player who is often considered one of, if not the, greatest athletes the sport has ever seen. Jordan, who rose to fame during the 1990’s, was known for his shooting skills. Bun B is comparing the way he’s taking control over the rap game to the way Jordan took control over basketball in his prime.", "annotation_id": "17772347", "url": "https://genius.com/17772347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": ["Now I run the game, you stupid motha-suckas"], "meaning": "Drake uses a melody very similar to the one he would use two years later on his sophomore album Take Care:\n\nAre you down, are you down? Yeah, you all the way down, every time\nAm I down, am I down? Yeah, I’m all the way down, We’ll be fine\nAre you down, are you down? Yeah, you all the way down, every time\nAm I down, am I down? Yeah, I’m all the way down, We’ll be fine", "annotation_id": "13350394", "url": "https://genius.com/13350394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Look at all these posers bite our swagger like a great white", "vault_lines": ["Look at all these posers bite our swagger like a great white"], "meaning": "In rap, “biting” is slang for imitating or copying another person’s flows or character. Much the way that a Great White Shark bites its prey, people around Bun and his crew often try to imitate his ways.", "annotation_id": "13350354", "url": "https://genius.com/13350354", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "They just lose to love, those are tennis games, lady", "vault_lines": ["They just lose to love, those are tennis games, lady"], "meaning": "Drake forms a double entendre with “lose to love”:\n\nThe most obvious meaning is the one that extends the previous line. Drake claims to not be a player, but he is in the game nonetheless. Therefore, some girls lose to love when they falsely assume that he is committed to them.\n\nThe second meaning is that a score of 0 in a game of tennis is referred to as “love,” and losing to love means that the losing player did not win a single rally in the tennis game.\n\nInterestingly, Drake was romantically linked to tennis star Serena Williams, and based on the way he felt on “Nothings Into Somethings” from his 2017 project More Life, it was Drake that lost to love as Serena moved on from him and got engaged to Reddit co-founder Alexis Ohanian.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RGGUzrUYXP0", "annotation_id": "11793381", "url": "https://genius.com/11793381", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "[Chorus: Drake]", "vault_lines": ["Now I run the game, you stupid motha-suckas"], "meaning": "The hook was originally going to be shared with Lil Wayne, using the opening bar of his verse “I am the leather jacket, black glasses all american bad boy” followed by “I’m so uptown, i’m so uptown, i’m so uptown.. and motherfucker if you aint don’t go uptown” followed by Drake’s part.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fbkvTiQCLIQ", "annotation_id": "6748412", "url": "https://genius.com/6748412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "And when they call you the answer, you are hardly questioned", "vault_lines": ["And when they call you the answer, you are hardly questioned"], "meaning": "Nobody is questioning Drake because he is “the answer” to what they want.  He could do anything with anyone doubting him.\n\nThis isn’t necessarily true, as Drake constantly get skewered for his singing, sweater styles, softness, etc.\n\nBesides it’s not always easy being “the Answer.”\nJust ask Allen Iverson, who went from being a premier baller to facing some serious struggles.", "annotation_id": "2188583", "url": "https://genius.com/2188583", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Sippin’ on Pink Floyd and puffin’ Wayne Brady", "vault_lines": ["Sippin' on Pink Floyd and puffin' Wayne Brady"], "meaning": "Pink Floyd was an English rock band that started making music in 1965. Though in this context, Drake is likely referencing codeine – which is generally referred to as purple drank, but is often pink in color.\n\n\n\nWayne Brady is what people call marijuana that makes you “choke” – Brady appeared on a famous Chapelle skit where he asked “Is Wayne Brady gonna have to choke a bitch?”.", "annotation_id": "1592669", "url": "https://genius.com/1592669", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I see all this money through my Ohio State Buckeyes", "vault_lines": ["I see all this money through my Ohio State Buckeyes"], "meaning": "A Buckeye is the name of OSU’s sports teams, it also refers to Ohio natives. But here “buck eyes” refer to the eyes Drake uses to look at all of his bucks/money!", "annotation_id": "557267", "url": "https://genius.com/557267", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I palm the game like it’s a Spalding ball and take flight", "vault_lines": ["I palm the game like it's a Spalding ball and take flight"], "meaning": "To “palm” refers to the control that Bun B has in the rap game. Bun B compares this control to that of a Spalding basketball in which he “takes flight”. “Flight” is the traditional moniker used by basketball legend Michael Jordan who is referenced in the next line.", "annotation_id": "69015", "url": "https://genius.com/69015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "I’m so uptown", "vault_lines": ["Now I run the game, you stupid motha-suckas"], "meaning": "An alternate version of this song excludes Waynes verese and uses part of it as the hook, which makes more sense to call it Uptown. Otherwise this song should be called It’s Okay (I’m On) or something.\n\nThe song is called uptown because they took a sample from Uptown Girl by Billy Joel.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hCuMWrfXG4E\n\nWhen he say’s “i’m so uptown”, he just saying he so hood or so from the hood, and that’s why the next line, “mutherfucka if you ain’t, don’t go uptown”, means if you ain’t from this hood, or you ain’t hood enough don’t go there. There meaning literally the hood, or even deeper don’t act hood if you aren’t hood.\n\nThe song is called uptown because they took a sample from Uptown Girl by Billy Joel.", "annotation_id": "65613", "url": "https://genius.com/65613", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "And yo’ frame makes me wanna bowl a strike, well alright", "vault_lines": ["And yo' frame makes me wanna bowl a strike, well alright"], "meaning": "“Frame” refers to a girl’s body/figure, and this girl’s frame is so fine that it makes Drake want to bowl a strike. A bowling pin could be said to resemble the frame of a curvy woman.\n\n\n\nThis is also a bowling reference, as frame is the word for each “turn,” and bowling a strike refers to knocking all of the pins over in one go. Drake is a notorious fan of bowling.", "annotation_id": "47155", "url": "https://genius.com/47155", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Soowoo, bitch, I do this shit", "vault_lines": ["Soowoo, bitch, I do this shit"], "meaning": "“Soowoo” is a gang call used by Bloods to alert another member of something.\n\nMany people call Lil Wayne a fake gangster, so here Wayne is affirming he is indeed a real member of the blood gang who is really about that life, hence the next line.", "annotation_id": "47154", "url": "https://genius.com/47154", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "And I keep that toaster, you can come and be my strudel, bitch", "vault_lines": ["And I keep that toaster, you can come and be my strudel, bitch"], "meaning": "Some nice wordplay by Weezy in this line.  It can be interpreted a few different ways:\n\n\nWeezy has that toaster (gun), and will have you leaking red like a toaster strudel.\nA chick can be his toaster strudel and when he comes, he’s gonna cover her in white frosting. Yuck.\n\n\n\nAt the end of it, both interpretations include a pun on toaster strudels (obviously). And if you don’t like toaster strudels, then you are a terrorist.", "annotation_id": "47153", "url": "https://genius.com/47153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Damn, whose line is it anyways?", "vault_lines": ["Damn, whose line is it anyways?"], "meaning": "Wayne Brady was a regular on the United States versions of the improvisational comedy show “Whose Line is it Anyway?”\n\n\n\nWayne Brady also refers to cocaine. Drake wants to know… whose line is it anyway??", "annotation_id": "47144", "url": "https://genius.com/47144", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Women don’t get saved ’round me, even on a Sunday", "vault_lines": ["Women don't get saved 'round me, even on a Sunday"], "meaning": "To “get saved,” which would happen in a church, would normally occur on Sunday.  Women don’t get saved by him because he isn’t a “Captain Save  A Hoe”.\n\nDrake would go on to contradict this belief in “The Real Her” when he rapped about wanting to save a stripper no matter what his friends say.", "annotation_id": "47142", "url": "https://genius.com/47142", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "When I’m awake, you always restin’", "vault_lines": ["When I'm awake, you always restin'"], "meaning": "Drake is infamous for his late-night, around the clock studio sessions. On the MTV Documentary ‘Better Than Good Enough’ Drake admitted to sleeping and eating terribly because of his music career. Drake is in the studio cooking up classics, while his competition are in their beds getting a good night’s rest.", "annotation_id": "47139", "url": "https://genius.com/47139", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Now I run the game, you stupid motha-suckas", "vault_lines": ["Now I run the game, you stupid motha-suckas"], "meaning": "Michael Blackson, a Ghanaian-American comedian, famously uses “motha-sucka” in his comedy. Since Season 8, he has been a cast member of the hip-hop sketch show Wild ‘N Out, hosted by Nick Cannon.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N8xCwEkp25M", "annotation_id": "45032", "url": "https://genius.com/45032", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uptown", "line": "Uh, hardly home but always reppin’", "vault_lines": ["Uh, hardly home but always reppin'"], "meaning": "Drake often laments the fact that he is away from his hometown of Toronto in his songs. He has many second homes, such as Atlanta, Houston, Miami, and Memphis, where he spent a lot of time as a kid because his dad lived there. However, despite his nomadic lifestyle, Drake always represents his city in his music. One of the most common colloquial nicknames for Toronto is “the 6”, due to the area code 416 and the amalgamation of Toronto from six boroughs in 1998.\n\nSimilar lines in Drake’s discography are:\n\n“Worst Behavior”\n\nYeah, back and forth across the border line\nHate to leave the city, but I’ve got to do the overtime\n\n“10 Bands”\n\nMan, I told my city I’d be gone till November then November came\nThen I came, right back on my worst behav'", "annotation_id": "44534", "url": "https://genius.com/44534", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
