{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Wayne time", "vault_lines": ["Ladies and gentlemen, C5, Wayne time"], "meaning": "llllll", "annotation_id": "39348990", "url": "https://genius.com/39348990", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Aimin’ at your head like a buffalo", "vault_lines": ["Aimin' at your head like a buffalo"], "meaning": "Carter’s use of bison combat as a simile for “aiming at your head” is accurate. Bison-on-bison confrontations usually involve opponents butting heads in a series of high-impact collisions.\n\nWayne could also be referring to the popular practice of mounting a hunted bison’s head on a plaque. If a hunter is hoping to obtain such a trophy, it is actually recommended that they aim for the lung area, keeping the head intact.\n\n\nPhoto courtesy of Yunner, via Wikimedia Commons", "annotation_id": "38596967", "url": "https://genius.com/38596967", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Put the gun inside, what the fuck for? I sleep with the gun, and she don’t snore", "vault_lines": ["Put the gun inside, what the fuck for?"], "meaning": "Wayne is saying that he’s always got his gun with him. In fact, he prefers sleeping with a gun near him since it does not make noise, unless he has to use it.", "annotation_id": "32309457", "url": "https://genius.com/32309457", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "I come out the scuffle without a scuff, woah", "vault_lines": ["I come out the scuffle without a scuff, woah"], "meaning": "scuffle is defined as “a short, confused fight or struggle at close quarters” and a scuff is defined as “a mark made by scraping or grazing a surface or object”. lil wayne is basically saying he comes out of a fight without appearing injured, implying not only that his opponent lost, but that he came out completely fine.", "annotation_id": "31593303", "url": "https://genius.com/31593303", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "(Weezy, Weezy, Weezy, Weezy Weezy, Weezy, Weezy, Weezy)", "vault_lines": ["(Weezy, Weezy, Weezy, Weezy"], "meaning": "“Weezy” is another name for Lil Wayne", "annotation_id": "30939724", "url": "https://genius.com/30939724", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "At your front door", "vault_lines": ["At your front door"], "meaning": "Subtle flashback to the end of Green Ranger (Dedication IV, 2012, ft. J. Cole), where Wayne makes a reference to Special Delivery because he’s rapping over the beat even though he says he doesn’t like it.\n\nCrazy how Weezy and the beat work together, tho.", "annotation_id": "29832178", "url": "https://genius.com/29832178", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "It’s a blood bath, where the suds go?", "vault_lines": ["It's a blood bath, where the suds go?"], "meaning": "Suds – the bubbles on the surface of water in which soap, detergent, have been dissolved\n\nIts to show the gravity of the situation and blood does not have suds so Wayne is saying its going to be a literal blood bath", "annotation_id": "29705954", "url": "https://genius.com/29705954", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "We grow up fast, we roll up slow", "vault_lines": ["We grow up fast, we roll up slow"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne describes the harsh realities of growing up in a neighborhood wracked by poverty and gang violence, and the way that that cycle can repeat itself over generations.\n\nKids ‘grow up fast’ after experiencing traumas and hardships early on in life. Often they are forced by their circumstances to join gangs for either protection or resources, and might therefore find themselves in situations where they have to ‘roll up slow’ on rival gang members (in order to hurt them). Thus the youths whose lives are made difficult by gang violence become the sources of the violence in the next generation.", "annotation_id": "27584494", "url": "https://genius.com/27584494", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "If she’s iffy, there the drugs go", "vault_lines": ["If she's iffy, there the drugs go"], "meaning": "here we see wheezy admitting to using drugs and alcohol to coerce a woman into sleeping with him.", "annotation_id": "27309114", "url": "https://genius.com/27309114", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "It’s a shit show, put you front row Talkin’ shit, bro? Let your tongue show", "vault_lines": ["It's a shit show, put you front row"], "meaning": "A “shit show” is a vulgar slang term to describe a chaotic situation or event. Basically, Wayne takes the term and flips it to a gentle diss towards rappers that are trying to compete with him.\n\nThis lyric isn’t targeted at a specific person: however, four years later, in May 2022, these words would get taken extremely out of context. That followed the result of the NBA Playoffs Game 7 between the Dallas Mavericks and Phoenix Suns, in which the Suns had been predicted to win, but eventually lost to the Mavericks with a score of 123-90. This came as a huge shock to people, considering the Suns had the best record in the NBA’s regular season and had made it to the finals the previous year.\n\nWayne himself attended the game in support of the Suns and their point guard Chris Paul, a close friend of his. Additionally, the rapper had already taunted the Mavericks earlier on in the season, calling their star player Luka Dončić a “ho” through a tweet.\n\nHowever, the tables turned against Weezy, as Dončić himself scored 35 points during Game 7 and helped Dallas get the win: in the process, a close shot of a perplexed Wayne sitting front row at the match went viral all across the Internet.\n\nFurthermore, Mark Cuban, owner of the Dallas Mavericks, went on to tweet these specific lines from “Uproar” in order to troll Wayne, who angrily replied to the provocation with a now-deleted tweet.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/mcuban/status/1526281168928350210?s=20&t=OB66wxTwSfn6dKEpGC6chQ", "annotation_id": "25899272", "url": "https://genius.com/25899272", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "We throw up gang signs, she throw up dope", "vault_lines": ["We throw up gang signs, she throw up dope"], "meaning": "wayne and his gang are all throwing up gang signs (possibly wayne famous illuminati lowercase ‘b’ over his eye) while the girl hes with is vomiting dope (possibly as she has had too much or as she could be a mule smugling it in her stomach for wayne", "annotation_id": "25748153", "url": "https://genius.com/25748153", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Put the green in the bag like a lawnmower", "vault_lines": ["Put the green in the bag like a lawnmower"], "meaning": "Many lawnmowers come compatible with a compartment that stores the freshly cut grass. Green refers to money or marijuana, meaning Wayne has either money in his bag or is selling weed and putting it in plastic bags.", "annotation_id": "21295777", "url": "https://genius.com/21295777", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Five, four, three, two, I let one go", "vault_lines": ["Five, four, three, two, I let one go"], "meaning": "Weezy has the same M.O. as Shan Yu, leader of the fearsome Huns: (1) kill ruthlessly, and (2) leave exactly one person behind to make sure the message gets back.\n\nSee this iconic scene from Disney’s Mulan (1998): https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fESubBqN00E", "annotation_id": "18185693", "url": "https://genius.com/18185693", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Five, four, three, two, where the ones go?", "vault_lines": ["Five, four, three, two, where the ones go?"], "meaning": "This line is a reference to throwing single dollar bills in a strip club. He threw too many too fast and has now found himself wondering where all the “ones” went.", "annotation_id": "18171778", "url": "https://genius.com/18171778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Trade the ski mask for the muzzle", "vault_lines": ["Trade the ski mask for the muzzle"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is referring to Kodak Black who asked him to have a fight and then he directed this line to him “You should’ve died as a baby”. Later he would diss Weezy again on Twitter. https://twitter.com/KodakBlack1k/status/810672063086264321\nHe ended up saying a lot of controversial things during a Breakfast Club Interview like “I’m Tupac resurrected”, while always wearing a black ski mask so Wayne is saying that he wants to put a muzzle on his mouth to shut it.", "annotation_id": "16977910", "url": "https://genius.com/16977910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "“Knock, knock!” ”Who’s there?” is how it won’t go", "vault_lines": ["\"Knock, knock!\" \"Who's there?\" is how it won't go"], "meaning": "“Knock, knock. Who’s there?” Paints a civilised image of how a normal greeting at someone’s door would go. Wayne isn’t here for formalities, he’s here to fight and prove he is better than you.", "annotation_id": "16197564", "url": "https://genius.com/16197564", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Dreadlock hang down like you dun know", "vault_lines": ["Dreadlock hang down like you dun know"], "meaning": "“You dun kno” is Jamaican Patois (nonstandard dialect) for the phrase “you already know.”\n\nWayne’s had dreads since around the time that the first of Tha Carter albums dropped in 2004. He says this in Jamaican Patois to reference the popularity of the dreadlocked hairstyle in Jamaica among Rastafarians and others.", "annotation_id": "15730782", "url": "https://genius.com/15730782", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Only way he comin’ back is through his unborns", "vault_lines": ["Only way he comin' back is through his unborns"], "meaning": "The victim is dead. Although he may already have children to carry on his bloodline, the only possibly way for him to “come back” is if he has yet to be born children that will carry on his name.", "annotation_id": "15633149", "url": "https://genius.com/15633149", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Just look up, bro, there the Scuds go", "vault_lines": ["Just look up, bro, there the Scuds go"], "meaning": "Scud is the name of a series of ballistic missiles developed by the Soviet Union during the Cold War.", "annotation_id": "15633127", "url": "https://genius.com/15633127", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Swizzy, he the chef, I like my lunch gross", "vault_lines": ["Swizzy, he the chef, I like my lunch gross"], "meaning": "“Swizzy he the chef” means Swizzy’s cooking up the beat, and Wayne eats it, or in other words, goes off. “I like my lunch gross” means he wants the beat to be nasty to go off on it.", "annotation_id": "15600674", "url": "https://genius.com/15600674", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Ayo, Mack, let the beat drop", "vault_lines": ["Ayo, Mack, let the beat drop"], "meaning": "Swizz Beatz is speaking to Mack Maine, who is a longtime friend of Wayne and is the President of his record label, Young Money Entertainment.", "annotation_id": "15502762", "url": "https://genius.com/15502762", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "The diamonds still shine in the rough, ho", "vault_lines": ["The diamonds still shine in the rough, ho"], "meaning": "A “diamond in the rough” is a person who is deemed to be kinder or of better character than they seem based on external characteristics like manners and social tact. This can often be attributed to the circumstances of their upbringing. The phrase implies that these people can be “polished,” and can also be perceived as a backhanded and patronizing compliment. Examples from popular media include Eggsy in Kingsman: The Secret Service and Eliza in My Fair Lady.\n\nWayne reminds us that despite the lack of polish, these idiomatic diamonds still shine.", "annotation_id": "15489101", "url": "https://genius.com/15489101", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Let me see your shoulders work", "vault_lines": ["Let me see your shoulders work"], "meaning": "This is in reference to the shoulder movement in the Harlem Shake dance, prominently featured in the original Special Delivery music video. Most hip hop fans of the era will almost instinctively start harlem shaking when this beat drops. CLASSIC", "annotation_id": "15477729", "url": "https://genius.com/15477729", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "If she sip lean, double-cup toast", "vault_lines": ["If she sip lean, double-cup toast"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne mentions his drug of choice, lean, a combination of promethazine and codeine mixed with soda and Jolly Ranchers for taste.\n\nIt is often stored in a “double cup” which is essentially two styrofoam cups stacked on top of each other. This method is used mainly to keep the drink’s coolness insulated.", "annotation_id": "15475010", "url": "https://genius.com/15475010", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Hair trigger pulled back like a cornrow (Woo!) Extra clip in the stash like a console", "vault_lines": ["Hair trigger pulled back like a cornrow (Woo!)"], "meaning": "A cornrow is a hairstyle where strands of hair are tightly braided from the scalp in a raised row by using an underhand. It is quite prominent in African-American culture and has been used as far back as 500 B.C.E.\n\n\n\nWeezy compares the image of a cornrow to a hair trigger, a technique in which a gun is set to go off at the slightest pressure. Despite both objects being “pulled back,” cornrows tend to be tightly braided as opposed to the loose nature used in a hair trigger. “Clip” may both refer to a bullet or a video game disk that can be inserted into a console.", "annotation_id": "15474952", "url": "https://genius.com/15474952", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "You a roughneck, I’m a cutthroat You’re a tough guy, that’s enough jokes", "vault_lines": ["You a roughneck, I'm a cutthroat"], "meaning": "Weezy seems to be none too impressed with the tough guy personas he sees around him, apparently they don’t scare him one bit.\n\n“Roughneck” is a term originally used to describe people employed in hard, manual jobs, often specifically associated with oil riggers.\n\nIt has, however, evolved into a catch-all term used to denote anybody who could be seen as strong or physically intimidating.\n\nWayne one-ups this however, describing himself is a cutthroat, a name that has for centuries been associated with someone who has committed murder, thus upping the ante from one he simply looks intimidating to one who is actually known to have followed through on their violent impulses.\n\nHe doubles up by playing on the jugular nature of both terms to establish himself as the superior party in the situation. Not only would one who merely looks tough by eliminated by one actually willing to end a life, but the physical state of actually having a strong, or rough, neck would still be of little use if somebody actually took a knife to it.", "annotation_id": "15473310", "url": "https://genius.com/15473310", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Listenin’ to Bono, you listen to Donald", "vault_lines": ["Listenin' to Bono, you listen to Donald"], "meaning": "Bono is the lead singer of rock band U2, famous for powerful political lyrics over melodic instrumentation as well as his activism.\n\n“Donald” is likely a reference to U.S. President Donald Trump. Weezy implies that he listens to Bono’s visionary songs and ideals.", "annotation_id": "15473029", "url": "https://genius.com/15473029", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Yellow diamonds up close, catch a sunstroke", "vault_lines": ["Yellow diamonds up close, catch a sunstroke"], "meaning": "Sunstroke is basically heat stroke but it occurs when you stay in the sun for too long in extreme temperatures.\n\nWayne’s diamonds are so large and bright yellow that up close they look like the Sun, and are just as powerful.\n\nhttps://i.scdn.co/image/cf012139c3b8681b46a66bae70558a8a336ab231", "annotation_id": "15472991", "url": "https://genius.com/15472991", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Puff, puff, bro; I don’t huff though", "vault_lines": ["Puff, puff, bro; I don't huff though"], "meaning": "Wayne is known to be an avid drug user, however here he clarifies that one drug he won’t partake in is inhalants, which is huffing dangerous vapors.\n\n“Puff, puff bro” is a referencing to smoking weed, which every Weezy fan knows he loves to do, as we all patiently await that lighter flicking sound.", "annotation_id": "15472958", "url": "https://genius.com/15472958", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "It’s a Swizz beat, there the drums go", "vault_lines": ["It's a Swizz beat, there the drums go"], "meaning": "Wayne shouts out this tracks producer; Swizz Beatz and signifies the fact that the beat is dropping at this point in his verse.\n\n\n\nSwizz is a legendary hip-hop record producer who has been working since the 1990s.  He produced Tha Carter III track “Dr. Carter” in which he received a shout out from Wayne on:\n\nBesides, Dr. Kanye West is one of the brightest\nAnd Dr. Swizz can stitch your track up the tightest\n\nSwizz has also produced a removed Carter IV track called “Dear Anne” and Wayne has rapped over Swizz' beats before on his iconic Da Drought 3 mixtape from 2007.", "annotation_id": "15468373", "url": "https://genius.com/15468373", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "line": "Money over bitches, and above hoes That is still my favorite love quote", "vault_lines": ["Money over bitches, and above hoes"], "meaning": "“M.O.B.” (Meaning “Money Over Bitches”) is a track by the late 2Pac from his album, Until the End of Time, that describes the philosophy of giving monetary opportunities a higher priority than women. The term is used often in hip-hop, but Pac was the first to implement it in his raps.", "annotation_id": "15468005", "url": "https://genius.com/15468005", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Uproar", "status": "done", "targets": 31, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-uproar-lyrics", "genius_title": "Uproar", "genius_id": 3985353, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "I circle your house like BB", "vault_lines": ["I circle your house like BB"], "meaning": "Wayne is Using a Play on Words\nImplying he’ll circle your house in a car in a drive by fashion\n\nBB Lewis (Abebe Lewis) is the CEO of Circle House Studios, the iconic Miami recording studio. Circle your house like BB is reference to Circle House Studios and BB Lewis .", "annotation_id": "41046767", "url": "https://genius.com/41046767", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
