{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Leave it to the flow, we getting dough like the bakery", "vault_lines": ["Leave it to the flow, we getting dough like the bakery"], "meaning": "“Dough” is slang for money. Wayne’s flow is so hot that he is making money on top of money off of his flow. Dough can also be taken literally as dough that you find in a bakery. A bakery makes bread and bread is also another slang word for money.", "annotation_id": "20439420", "url": "https://genius.com/20439420", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "I’m so motherfucking high, I can eat a star, haha", "vault_lines": ["I'm so motherfucking high, I can eat a star, haha"], "meaning": "Weezy loves to smoke weed. Getting high comes from smoking weed. High is used as a double entendre to compare the high from the drugs to being literally high in the sky. Wayne is so high that he is in space and can eat a star.", "annotation_id": "20439415", "url": "https://genius.com/20439415", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Like a tangerine", "vault_lines": ["I am bearing a ton like Levy"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION:\n\nPlay on words: “peel off” as in take off (in the car) and “peel off” as in removing the outer layer of a tangerine", "annotation_id": "16511925", "url": "https://genius.com/16511925", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "And me, I’m still spittin’ like a retard", "vault_lines": ["And me, I'm still spittin' like a retard"], "meaning": "When someone thinks of a mentally disabled person, they often imagine them drooling at the mouth, not able to control their saliva. Lil Wayne makes a rather simple but effective play on the word “spit”. Back in 2007 these double-entendres were still fresh, trust me. As a matter of fact, Lil Wayne arguably brought them into mainstream.", "annotation_id": "17212886", "url": "https://genius.com/17212886", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Seventy-five thousand for these fees, shit", "vault_lines": ["Seventy-five thousand for these fees, shit"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is bragging about how much money he charges per verse on other artists' songs. Keep in mind, that this mixtape was released in 2007 – the year when Lil Wayne didn’t have a solo label project, but became The Hottest Rapper Alive (and, arguably, the best too) – solely by having guest features on other people songs.", "annotation_id": "17212847", "url": "https://genius.com/17212847", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Bad yellow bitch keep my passenger seat warm", "vault_lines": ["Bad yellow bitch keep my passenger seat warm"], "meaning": "“Yellow” here refers to a light skinned black woman.\n\nSee, for example, “A Dedication,” the final track on Weezy’s 2012 mixtape, Dedication 4:\n\n“And this also dedicated to the bad bitches\nFirst off, them chocolate women\nI know I talk about them yella bitches in the real bits all the time\nBut um this dedicated to them chocolate women\nYa dig?”\n\nhttps://genius.com/Lil-wayne-a-dedication-lyrics", "annotation_id": "13985551", "url": "https://genius.com/13985551", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "I’m a dog, I’m a Hoya, homie", "vault_lines": ["I'm a dog, I'm a Hoya, homie"], "meaning": "“Hoyas” is the name given to the athletic teams representing Georgetown University, whose mascot is Jack the Bulldog.\n\nWeezy’s leaked 2008 song “I’m a Monster (Eat You Alive)” features a similar Hoya reference:\n\nDoctor I'mma dog, I'mma dog, I'mma dog you\nNot Georgetown but I'mma uptown Hoya", "annotation_id": "13985504", "url": "https://genius.com/13985504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Put a motherfucker on ice like the Maple Leaves That’s a hockey team, and I ain’t on no hockey team", "vault_lines": ["Put a motherfucker on ice like the Maple Leaves"], "meaning": "To “ice someone” or “put someone on ice” is to kill them.\n\nThe Toronto Maple Leafs are a National Hockey League franchise based in Toronto, Ontario.\n\n\n\nWeezy may not be on a hockey team, but that doesn’t mean he isn’t a pro at icing people.", "annotation_id": "13985477", "url": "https://genius.com/13985477", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "You know I’m nasty, excuse my behavior Let me just taste you, we can fuck later", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, let me upgrade you"], "meaning": "When it comes to Weezy on cunnilingus, Madeleine Holden hit it on the head it in her essay “Eat It Up and Lay Wit It: Hip Hop, Cunnilingus and Morality in Entertainment”:\n\nHe’s been rap’s most open, consistent and caveat-free cunnilingus champion for years, and he had enough serious rap cred to actually make it catch on […] When the rap game consensus was anti-cunnilingus, Weezy didn’t give a fuck. He was like the first kid who jumps off a rock into water, and then all the other kids see that it’s safe and fun and they start jumping off too. Others rapped about eating pussy — sometimes even quite charitably — but no-one made it a stance like Weezy did. That stance changed the whole game.", "annotation_id": "13985405", "url": "https://genius.com/13985405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "And if you hating, baby, you can get a side of these These nuts in your mouth, and can you swallow, please? Haha", "vault_lines": ["And if you hating, baby, you can get a side of these"], "meaning": "“Deez nuts” jokes are almost as old as time, but somehow never get old – witness this video from the 2016 election season:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R2CWK3Qrmy0\n\nThe locus classicus within hip hop is probably the opening skit of Dr. Dre’s “Deeez Nuuuts” from 1992’s The Chronic.", "annotation_id": "13985339", "url": "https://genius.com/13985339", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Damn, rest in peace, Apollo Creed", "vault_lines": ["Damn, rest in peace, Apollo Creed"], "meaning": "Continuing the Rocky references; The character Apollo Creed, portrayed by Carl Weathers, features in the first four Rocky films. In Rocky and Rocky II, he figures as Rocky Balbao’s primary opponent. In Rocky III, he becomes Rocky’s manager. And in Rocky IV, he is killed in the ring during an exhibition match with Soviet Olympic boxer Ivan Drago, dying dramatically in Rocky’s arms.\n\n\n\nR.I.P.", "annotation_id": "13984474", "url": "https://genius.com/13984474", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "But I’m a champion, where’s the fucking Rocky theme?", "vault_lines": ["But I'm a champion, where's the fucking Rocky theme?"], "meaning": "“Gonna Fly Now,” also known as the “Theme from Rocky,” hit number one on the Billboard Hot 100 chart in 1977 on its way to becoming a cultural phenomenon. (Alas, while the movie Rocky won the Academy Award for Best Picture that year, the theme song lost out for Best Original Song to “Evergreen (Love Theme from A Star Is Born).”)\n\nThe “Theme from Rocky” plays in the background during the iconic scene from the movie in which Rocky Balboa runs up the 72 steps leading to the entrance of the Philadelphia Museum of Art and raises his arms in triumph:", "annotation_id": "13984341", "url": "https://genius.com/13984341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "I can get a hundred-thousand up in these jeans Big stacks, my pockets on Creatine", "vault_lines": ["I can get a hundred-thousand up in these jeans"], "meaning": "Creatine supplements, which purport to increase muscle mass (as well as to improve strength, speed recovery, etc.), are especially popular with athletes. Up to half of all professional athletes report using them, though scientific research on the benefits of creatine has been mixed.\n\n\n\nBe that as it may, Tunechi definitely needs some super-sized pockets to hold his stacks.", "annotation_id": "13984245", "url": "https://genius.com/13984245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Understand, I am Michael Jordan balling, yes", "vault_lines": ["Understand, I am Michael Jordan balling, yes"], "meaning": "Wayne drops a fairly tough Jay-Z dis here. Hov constantly compares himself to Jordan, so Wayne’s doing so both sets up the comparison and acts as a kind of challenge.\n\nIt also didn’t escape Weezy’s notice that the “mami” who sings this song is Jay’s wife, Beyoncé.\n\nJay was at this time the president of Def Jam, so the “your man’s just an employer, mami” line has extra resonance.", "annotation_id": "11803328", "url": "https://genius.com/11803328", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "And I’m just murdering niggas, free of charge", "vault_lines": ["And I'm just murdering niggas, free of charge"], "meaning": "Double entendre:\n\n– He’s lyrically murdering rappers, however he doesn’t get caught and therefore gets off without a criminal charge.\n\n– This track appeared on Da Drought 3, a mixtape released for free. Therefore, he is literally murdering rappers “free of charge”.", "annotation_id": "2698846", "url": "https://genius.com/2698846", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Hollygrove gator, ain’t nobody greater", "vault_lines": ["Hollygrove gator, ain't nobody greater"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne hails from the Holly Grove neighborhood of New Orleans, Louisiana.\n\nLouisiana also has the greatest alligator population of any state in the USA.", "annotation_id": "150536", "url": "https://genius.com/150536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "She tell a blind bitch, and she say, “I gotta see,” ha", "vault_lines": ["She tell a blind bitch, and she say, \"I gotta see,\" ha"], "meaning": "He actually brought up a good question: How would the deaf girl communicate with the blind girl? But for Lil Wayne, they would go as far as learning how to communicate to go for him.\n\nNonetheless, despite their senses being hindered, they are dying to admire Wayne’s greatness.", "annotation_id": "150526", "url": "https://genius.com/150526", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "It’s a new game, and I’m the coach like Avery", "vault_lines": ["It's a new game, and I'm the coach like Avery"], "meaning": "He’s referring to Avery Johnson, who was a coach for NBA teams such as the Nets and Mavericks. Probably not coincidentally, he’s from New Orleans.", "annotation_id": "36931", "url": "https://genius.com/36931", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Seat way back, listening to Anita Baker", "vault_lines": ["Seat way back, listening to Anita Baker"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne’s a sucker for mood music. He even plays a sample of it in his song, “Best Thing”.", "annotation_id": "36923", "url": "https://genius.com/36923", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Colder than the heebie-jeebies", "vault_lines": ["Colder than the heebie-jeebies"], "meaning": "The ‘heebee jeebies’ is a colloquial term used to describe that feeling you get when the hair on the back of your neck stands up, usually when cold", "annotation_id": "36919", "url": "https://genius.com/36919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "We are Young Money, bitch, and I am the leader He are, Curren$y, Mack Maine, and T-Raww And I just signed a chick named Nicki Minaj", "vault_lines": ["We are Young Money, bitch, and I am the leader"], "meaning": "Young Money consists of these artists, plus a few more. As of now, though, Curren$y isn’t a part of Young Money anymore.\n\nInterestingly, this is the first time Lil Wayne mentions Nicki Minaj, and she has recently made to the top of the rap game…\n\n\n\nWho is “D-Raw”??", "annotation_id": "36142", "url": "https://genius.com/36142", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "I’m so five-oh Four", "vault_lines": ["Fuck you, ho, I'm so five-oh"], "meaning": "504 is the area code for the New Orleans area, which is where Lil Wayne is from", "annotation_id": "34712", "url": "https://genius.com/34712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "If you don’t like it, nigga, fuck you, no Vaseline", "vault_lines": ["If you don't like it, nigga, fuck you, no Vaseline"], "meaning": "Getting fucked is bad, but without Vaseline would probably hurt even worse.\n\nAlso a reference to the Ice Cube song, “No Vaseline”.", "annotation_id": "33673", "url": "https://genius.com/33673", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "I am bearing a ton like Levy", "vault_lines": ["I am bearing a ton like Levy"], "meaning": "A levee is a slope or wall used to hold back flood waters, in which case they are “bearing a ton” of water. This is more relevant since Hurricane Katrina (which caused the floods in Weezy’s hometown of New Orleans) happened a couple years before “Da Drought 3” was released.\n\nThis is also a double entendre, as it’s referring to Barrington Levy, a reggae singer.", "annotation_id": "33669", "url": "https://genius.com/33669", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "line": "Bitch, holler, it is Lil Weezy They cannot see me, they are like Stevie", "vault_lines": ["They cannot see me, they are like Stevie"], "meaning": "Stevie Wonder is a blind singer-songwriter who is famous for songs like “Isn’t She Lovely?” and “Superstition.”\n\n“They cannot see me” could be a reference to wrestler John Cena, whose often tells his opponents, “you can’t see me.”", "annotation_id": "33668", "url": "https://genius.com/33668", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Upgrade", "status": "done", "targets": 36, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-upgrade-lyrics", "genius_title": "Upgrade", "genius_id": 1110, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Up to Me", "line": "It’s up to you, Wayne, (I know) nigga, stay up and keep it real It’s all on you, man, my nigga, I know how you feel Just be strong, cause everythang gon’ be alright (Alright Dog) I know it’s hard tryna make it but it’s gon’ be alright (Alright Dog)", "vault_lines": ["But I know it wasn't meant to be"], "meaning": "Since his step-father died, it’s all up to Wayne now to provide for his family. This is a hard situation to go through especially considering Wayne was only 15-16 when this happened. Fellow HB member Turk is telling him to keep his head up and stay strong because even though things are hard now, they’ll get better.\n\n\n\nWayne & Turk\n\nPer Rolling Stone, Wayne’s “very first tattoo, on his right arm, reads IN MEMORY OF RABBIT: IT’S UP TO ME”.", "annotation_id": "7771743", "url": "https://genius.com/7771743", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Up to Me", "line": "Cita", "vault_lines": ["Look, where I go from here?"], "meaning": "Jacida Carter or “Cita”, Wayne’s Mom", "annotation_id": "3446790", "url": "https://genius.com/3446790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Up to Me", "line": "And I know that ya see I put out my own CD And picture this, your little boy even on TV", "vault_lines": ["And I know that ya see I put out my own CD"], "meaning": "Wayne was only 17 years old when his debut album, Tha Block Is Hot, dropped.\n\n\n\n He also had his music videos played on TV and got a record deal with Cash Money Records.\n\n\n\nHe wants his father to be proud of everything he’s accomplished despite still being so young.", "annotation_id": "2597914", "url": "https://genius.com/2597914", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
