{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "[Verse 1: Patrick Stump]", "vault_lines": ["Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, o-oh, oh"], "meaning": "This verse is partially taken from a Blogspot that Pete posted on February 18, 2008.\n\n\nmc hammer and miss piggy bank\ni get bright ideas in dark rooms\nred rooster combs on our head\nwe are galaxies\na catipillar that got stuck\nmr moth come quick with any luck\nlong walk in a dark house\na roman candle heart\nkeep us far apart\n\ntour is just thinking you have been in every hotel, club or truck stop before.\nit is deja vu personified.\n\nall full of love so much that my teeth are floating.\n\nnow.\nposted by xo at 11:48 PM", "annotation_id": "33360189", "url": "https://genius.com/33360189", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "[Bridge: Lil Wayne]", "vault_lines": ["Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, o-oh, oh"], "meaning": "No lighter flick?", "annotation_id": "33094368", "url": "https://genius.com/33094368", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "Your pupils (big), big (rollin’) Rollin’ like dice", "vault_lines": ["Your pupils (big), big (rollin')"], "meaning": "Drug use is insinuated here, as enlarged pupils are a side effect of “rolling”, which is a common description of someone who has recently taken a hit of ecstasy (MDMA).\n\nOnes’s eyes can also roll back when experiencing sexual pleasure.", "annotation_id": "22703946", "url": "https://genius.com/22703946", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "A Roman candle heart, keep us far apart", "vault_lines": ["A Roman candle heart, keep us far apart"], "meaning": "Roman candles are a type of firework that give off a series of flaming colored balls and sparks. Roman candles can shoot out these flaming balls several feet.\n\nThus, it is possible the speaker is calling his heart fire like at sudden moments. This prevents the speaker from being close to the person they are walking to this house with, as stated in the previous line.\n\nRoman candles are often very faulty, so they may be alluding to the fact that their feelings and heart is faulty and it’s keeping them apart.", "annotation_id": "18123619", "url": "https://genius.com/18123619", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "I’m cocktail party doin’ alright, hate me baby", "vault_lines": ["I'm cocktail party doin' alright, hate me baby"], "meaning": "This line appears to critique the false nature of small talk at industry events and gatherings.\n\nPeople would rarely feel comfortable talking about their true feelings/ making themselves vulnerable in group situations, or in front of strangers, often choosing to lie and say “I’m alright” when asked how they are doing.", "annotation_id": "16142001", "url": "https://genius.com/16142001", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "A caterpillar that got stuck Mr. Moth, come quick with any luck", "vault_lines": ["A caterpillar that got stuck"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION:\n\nA catepillar will eventually turn into a moth => it makes sense to think that if Pete is the catepillar (in relation to his fame) the moth should simbolyze more successful musicians.\n\nHowever, being the moth a nocturnal animal, it’s very difficult to not associate it with death itself (night is commonly related with death in it’s symbology).\n\nSo I realized:What if it represents both?\n27 is a song in the same album as this one and it alludes to all of the celebrities who died young after struggling because of their fame.\nPete had a bad time after becoming so famous too.\n\nSo… if he’s a catepillar he’s not a great musician yet (a legend). He’s stuck (he’s having a hard time). Maybe he will find solace in the same fate of the other celebrities' represented by the moth (aka. death). He doesn’t care how(with any luck), but he wants his bad situation to end quickly.", "annotation_id": "4012373", "url": "https://genius.com/4012373", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "I can feel the weather in my bones Wish hard enough, I can turn it to what I like", "vault_lines": ["I can feel the weather in my bones"], "meaning": "“Feel the weather in my bones” is a metaphor that refers to people with some sort of hidden talent, like the ones who claim to have the “ability” to predict weather changes right before they happen, just by feeling the pain in their joints.\n\n\n\nIn this line, the author claims to have a special trait that appeals the girl, but still wants her to change her personality and “wish hard for it” in order to get a chance with him.", "annotation_id": "13108920", "url": "https://genius.com/13108920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "A long walk to a dark house", "vault_lines": ["A long walk to a dark house"], "meaning": "After a long and difficult walk, it turns out that the destination (the house) was not nearly as nice as one would imagine while working to get there. Pete uses this metaphor as a comparison to his own rise to fame. The long walk refers to how difficult it is to become famous, while the dark house refers to how now that Pete has achieved fame, it’s not all the glitz and glamor that he imagined while striving for it.", "annotation_id": "12387999", "url": "https://genius.com/12387999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "Maybe I’m a piece of art", "vault_lines": ["Maybe I'm a piece of art"], "meaning": "Art in itself is a very loose concept, and depends largely on the perception of others to how they view it. Wentz is saying that although he and the audience he is directly addressing doesn’t think he is special, he is suggesting there are people who think he is. This is most likely referring to fans of the band, who see him as ‘a piece of art’.\nhttp://intuitivecreativity.typepad.com/.a/6a00d83451dc5b69e201bb087309e5970d-pi", "annotation_id": "12216220", "url": "https://genius.com/12216220", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "Stuck on a little hot mess (little hot mess) Whoa, whoa", "vault_lines": ["Stuck on a little hot mess (little hot mess)"], "meaning": "A ‘hot mess’ refers to someone who is in emotional disarray, however still maintain an aesthetically pleasing appearance. The narrative of the chorus is referring to how this girl wants to get with him, however he is reluctant due to how much like everyone else she is. Wentz is sarcastically referring to himself as the ‘hot mess’, and elaborating on how she’s ‘stuck’ on him, or wants to get with him.", "annotation_id": "5062155", "url": "https://genius.com/5062155", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "Oh, three, two, one, we go live", "vault_lines": ["Oh, three, two, one, we go live"], "meaning": "“We’re going live in 3, 2 , 1!” is a common phrase heard in places like News Broadcasts. It is used a signal to start relaying the news or start the programme.\n\nThe fact that this phrase is dropped right before the chorus is sort of a signal that what he is about to say next is like the news- something we should pay attention to.", "annotation_id": "12129625", "url": "https://genius.com/12129625", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "You’re a faded moon", "vault_lines": ["you're a faded moon"], "meaning": "Wentz metaphorically refers to the moon as the life of a party, as it is essentially a ‘beacon of light’ when it reaches nighttime. He refers to the girl as a ‘faded moon’, as she blends in with everyone else and isn’t anything special.", "annotation_id": "4914443", "url": "https://genius.com/4914443", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "Oh baby, you’re a classic Like a little black dress,", "vault_lines": ["Oh baby, you're a classic"], "meaning": "A ‘little black dress’ is often used as an example of an outfit that fits any occasion. Wentz is stating that this girl fits into this stereotype of famous people or ‘fame whores’. He states that the girl is ‘a classic’, which is a way of saying she isn’t unique in any way.", "annotation_id": "4903842", "url": "https://genius.com/4903842", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "Dear gravity, you held me down in this starless city", "vault_lines": ["Dear gravity, you held me down in this starless city"], "meaning": "Gravity is a natural phenomena in which objects gravitate towards each other, including what keeps people bound to earth. He is accusing gravity as the driving force that is holding him in once place and stopping him from going out and living his dreams. “Starless city” screams of a mediocre life situation and gravity can be either the people around him or his own insecurities that are holding him down.\n\nThis line also references a lyric from “Sophomore Slump or Comeback of the Year”:\n\n“‘Cause I swear I’d burn this city down to show you the light”", "annotation_id": "4157085", "url": "https://genius.com/4157085", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "Oh, my friends all lie and say", "vault_lines": ["Oh, my friends all lie and say"], "meaning": "His friends are trying to help him, saying they only want him to do well in life, but he disregards their well-wishes, thinking they’re lying and are just trying to slow him down.\n\nThis idea is also mentioned in “I Don’t Care” with the lines “these friends, they don’t love you, they just love the hotel suites”. His ‘friends’ want him to do well, but only for their own benefit. They don’t really care about him, just the perks they get from being associated with him.", "annotation_id": "4012384", "url": "https://genius.com/4012384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "line": "I’m not a crybaby I’m the crybaby", "vault_lines": ["I'm not a crybaby"], "meaning": "The whole album reflects many themes about the band’s dislike of fame, or making fun of famous people who complain about their lives. Wentz is ironically placing himself above other celebrities who are part of this practice, stating that he is THE crybaby, not just A crybaby.", "annotation_id": "2345158", "url": "https://genius.com/2345158", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tiffany Blews", "status": "done", "targets": 17, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Fall-out-boy-tiffany-blews-lyrics", "genius_title": "Tiffany Blews", "genius_id": 118753, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "Yeah, and if you come from under that water then there’s fresh air", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and if you come from under that water then there's fresh air"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is reminding the victims of Katrina that there is still hope, there is still fresh air.\n\nHe could also be comparing the struggle of hurricane Katrina to struggling in life. Coming from “under that water” represents poverty or hardship, and the “fresh air” represents escaping from it.", "annotation_id": "24144050", "url": "https://genius.com/24144050", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "And don’t trip if I light one, I walk a tight one", "vault_lines": ["And don't trip if I light one, I walk a tight one"], "meaning": "This is a double entendre with the meanings being:\n\nA) comparing life to walking a tightrope, he has to maintain a proper balance and be on his best behavior now that he’s in the public eye as rapper/entertainer/mogul, which takes as much energy and focus as it would to walk a tightrope\n\nB) don’t be upset if he’s drinking or smoking because look at where’s he’s come from and how he became successful even though he’s coming from the hood where he was surrounded by negativity but yet his hometown is still suffering from poverty, unemployment, gang violence, drugs, police brutality, and hurricane Katrina, he’s smoking and drinking as a way to relieve the pressure from the daily stresses he can never escape", "annotation_id": "15642211", "url": "https://genius.com/15642211", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "The sky is falling", "vault_lines": ["The sky is falling"], "meaning": "The idiom “The sky is falling!” came into common usage from the popular folk tale Chicken Little (AKA Henny Penny), about a chicken who believes the world is about to end. In most contexts, the phrase is used to indicate a mistaken, and often hysterical, belief in some pending disaster (which would be somewhat at odds with the way Robin Thicke deploys it here).", "annotation_id": "14113969", "url": "https://genius.com/14113969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "How you feel, you’re not alone We’re all just jealous, jealous", "vault_lines": ["How you feel, you're not alone"], "meaning": "This line is speaking directly to the government and the powers that be, while they go home to their families and friends the victims of this tragedy are essentially abandoned. The ‘you’re not alone could also be referring to people who lost family friends and members, they are now alone.", "annotation_id": "14018878", "url": "https://genius.com/14018878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "A shot in the dark", "vault_lines": ["A shot in the dark"], "meaning": "At (4:05) you can also hear an ad-lib track of Robin Thicke singing:\n\n“Take that money, make that money\n Take that money…”\n\nWayne also recycled those lyrics in his following album, Rebirth (2010)\n\nGround Zero: (2:58)\n\n“Make that money yeah make that money\n Then watch the government take that money”", "annotation_id": "14008523", "url": "https://genius.com/14008523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "They try tell me keep my eyes open", "vault_lines": ["They try tell me keep my eyes open"], "meaning": "Double entendre:\n- The previous line, Weezy talked about him smoking and “walk a tight one” (having his eyes narrowed – an effect caused by marijuana), so “keep my eyes opens” means people’re telling him to stay sober.\n- After hurricane Katrina, looting, violence and crime rates started to rise significantly. “Keep my eyes open” in this case is to stay aware, watching out", "annotation_id": "10705813", "url": "https://genius.com/10705813", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "I’m gonna get my kicks before it all burns down", "vault_lines": ["I'm gonna get my kicks before it all burns down"], "meaning": "That’s rock and roll. Going to have a little fun before it’s over.", "annotation_id": "8636103", "url": "https://genius.com/8636103", "state": "verified", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "We are at war with the universe", "vault_lines": ["We are at war with the universe"], "meaning": "We were at war with the universe of all things, of nature and of humanity. Our’s is a universe of love, but then you have a natural disaster that was made worse by human error.", "annotation_id": "8636101", "url": "https://genius.com/8636101", "state": "verified", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "[Produced by Robin Thicke]", "vault_lines": ["We are at war with the universe"], "meaning": "I wrote the song after Katrina. Wayne is from New Orleans and he really connected with the idea of the lyrics. A couple years later, I did the pre recording of the vocals when we were hanging out in my studio in LA, and then it wasn’t until a year and a half later that I found out he was keeping it and putting it on his new album. He made it so powerful.\n\nThen we had the honor of performing that at the Grammys together. That was magical. Anytime you can perform on the stage like that in front of all your peers, it’s special enough. But to have a song that had such special meaning to him and I, that made it even more special.", "annotation_id": "8636100", "url": "https://genius.com/8636100", "state": "verified", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "Just breathe, baby, God’s got a blessing to spare (Oh) Yes, I know the process is so much stress", "vault_lines": ["Just breathe, baby, God's got a blessing to spare (Oh)"], "meaning": "He’s telling everybody still caught in the struggle to persevere no matter what. God is watching over them at all times and will see them through this disaster.\n\nThe stress and hard work that goes into rebuilding will be worth it in the long run after you see all the progress you’ve achieved and how you’ve changed people’s lives.", "annotation_id": "2578357", "url": "https://genius.com/2578357", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "I deny being down though they seem to hold us", "vault_lines": ["I deny being down though they seem to hold us"], "meaning": "In New Orleans (Wayne’s home town), they face many tragedies. So while Wayne will never admit to being sad, hopeless, scared etc. about the condition and future of his home town, he feels as if one horrible thing after another happens to them.", "annotation_id": "1394929", "url": "https://genius.com/1394929", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "And they wonder why black people still voting (Ooh) ’Cause your president still choking", "vault_lines": ["And they wonder why black people still voting (Ooh)"], "meaning": "Wayne was very vocal about Bush after the hurricane, as were many others about how he handled the clean up. See Georgia Bush.\n\nAlso, Bush choked on a pretzel back in 2002. Now not only is Bush choking on the pretzel, but additionally in his response to Hurricane Katrina.", "annotation_id": "1287581", "url": "https://genius.com/1287581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "First came the hurricane, then the morning sun", "vault_lines": ["First came the hurricane, then the morning sun"], "meaning": "He is referring to Hurricane Katrina, which ripped through New Orleans in 2005 and left the city in shambles.\n\n\n\nDespite the wreckage, the people of New Orleans demonstrated great strength and perseverance in enduring such a tragedy.", "annotation_id": "634140", "url": "https://genius.com/634140", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "These kids so fast they grow They learn so quickly now That there’s nowhere to go That there’s no future, future", "vault_lines": ["These kids so fast they grow"], "meaning": "Kids don’t act like kids anymore. They somehow outgrow the stage  of naivety rather quickly. Just as they stop believing in Santa Claus at a certain age, they also learn very quickly that in life, nothing is handed to you and dreams becoming a reality is almost  impossible when you’re Black and your government doesn’t care about your well-being.", "annotation_id": "218638", "url": "https://genius.com/218638", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "We just can’t overcome, no", "vault_lines": ["We just can't overcome, no"], "meaning": "No matter what they did, they couldn’t get by if their own government refused to help.", "annotation_id": "218633", "url": "https://genius.com/218633", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "No governor, no help from the mayor", "vault_lines": ["No governor, no help from the mayor"], "meaning": "The governor of Louisiana at the time was Kathleen Blanco. She took a large part of the blame for not better preparing Louisiana for the hurricane. She failed to realize how powerful the hurricane was until it was too late, so there were not many reinforcements.", "annotation_id": "160205", "url": "https://genius.com/160205", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "I lost everything? But I ain’t the only one", "vault_lines": ["I lost everything? But I ain't the only one"], "meaning": "Wayne did lose his New Orleans home, but he has many other places to live, and moved to Miami after the hurricane. Unlike the actual New Orleans citizens who literally had nothing but their surviving family members after Katrina. It doesn’t seem like Wayne is actually claiming that he lost everything to Katrina, he is just putting himself in the shoes of someone who did.", "annotation_id": "160189", "url": "https://genius.com/160189", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "I, knock on the door, hope isn’t home Fate’s not around, the luck’s all gone", "vault_lines": ["I, knock on the door, hope isn't home"], "meaning": "Personification of everything a victim would need to get through Katrina. None of these are present and able to help a survivor.\n\nThere is worldplay going on with the word lucks. It also sounds as if Weezy is saying the word “lux” which is short for luxury meaning that there is no more luxury due to the storm that destroyed a large part of New Orleans.", "annotation_id": "160187", "url": "https://genius.com/160187", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "I work at the corner store We all got problems, problems", "vault_lines": ["I work at the corner store"], "meaning": "Taking the role of an ordinary citizen affected by the hurricane. They had an ordinary job, but a natural disaster right in your area is a lot to overcome for someone in the middle class. This is from the perspective of one member of the community, but everyone similar to them in class and wealth has the same problems.", "annotation_id": "160185", "url": "https://genius.com/160185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "No one’s gonna fly down low No one’s gonna save us now", "vault_lines": ["No one's gonna fly down low"], "meaning": "The main point the song is trying to get across is President Bush doesn’t want to send anyone down to the New Orleans area to help give relief. The New Orleans residents feel abandoned as the only people who can help them are turning their backs on them.", "annotation_id": "160184", "url": "https://genius.com/160184", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "My whole city underwater, some people still floating", "vault_lines": ["My whole city underwater, some people still floating"], "meaning": "This is a metaphor for the people who are still getting by, but haven’t gotten out of the problem (water) yet, so they are just staying above the surface.\n\nIt’s also a literal reference to the event, as people qwew actually seen floating in the aftermath of hurricane Katrina.\n\n“ EDS NOTE: GRAPHIC CONTENT  A dead body lies in the floodwaters on Canal Street just outside the in New Orleans, La., Sunday, Sept. 4, 2005. The city is littered with bodies, some of them victims of Hurricane Katrina and some of them victims of the post hurricane violence that gripped the city for several days before troops arrived. (AP Photo/Rick Bowmer)”", "annotation_id": "148586", "url": "https://genius.com/148586", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "But it’s the progress that feels the best", "vault_lines": ["But it's the progress that feels the best"], "meaning": "The road to recovery in New Orleans is going to be tough, but the effort put in will be worth it in the end. The stress and sadness of the clean up operation will be alleviated by the pride of re-establishing the city as the vibrant place it once was.\n\n\n\nSo far, the city of New Orleans has indeed re-established its unique population, especially in the areas that weren’t totally under water.", "annotation_id": "106545", "url": "https://genius.com/106545", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "’Cause I came from the projects straight to success, and you’re next So try, they can’t steal your pride, it’s inside (Oh, woah) Then find it and keep on grinding (Woah) ’Cause in every dark cloud, there’s a silver lining, I know", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I came from the projects straight to success, and you're next"], "meaning": "By the age of 8, Lil Wayne had already been signed to a label for his rapping performance… at age 8. He grew up in Hollygrove, New Orleans which was his projects and literally he went straight to success considering by the age of 14 he knew he was to be a rapper.\n\nIn the projects, Wayne didn’t let the oppressive surroundings stop his ambition and that is his message for the hapless people of New Orleans", "annotation_id": "106536", "url": "https://genius.com/106536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "Take away the football team, the basketball team Now all we got is me to represent New Orleans, shit", "vault_lines": ["We all got problems, problems"], "meaning": "Following Hurricane Katrina, the New Orleans Hornets were forced to relocate to Oklahoma City. In 2005, the Saints were absent from any home games for the entire season. With all the sports teams moving out, Lil Wayne was the last major symbol of the city of New Orleans.", "annotation_id": "106501", "url": "https://genius.com/106501", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "You tie my hands, what am I gonna be? What have I done so bad? What is my destiny? (Oh-oh-oh) You tie my hands, what am I suppose to see? What have I done so bad? (What have I done) What am I gonna be?", "vault_lines": ["You tie my hands, what am I gonna be?"], "meaning": "Tie My Hands is a reference to slave treatment in which they are criticizing Bush for oppressing the people of New Orleans by a medium of leaving them stranded – according to them.", "annotation_id": "106498", "url": "https://genius.com/106498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "But they talked that freedom at us And didn’t even leave a ladder, damn", "vault_lines": ["But they talked that freedom at us"], "meaning": "Another criticism of George Bush. He spoke at them rather than to them.\n\n\n\nThey were given the idea that freedom and happiness is there for the people but they were never given help or shown a way to reach it.", "annotation_id": "106495", "url": "https://genius.com/106495", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "line": "Yeah, some say tragedy’s hard to get over But sometime that tragedy means it’s over", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, some say tragedy's hard to get over"], "meaning": "1,836 people were killed in the Katrina tragedy and a controversy of neglecting the African-American populace in New Orleans stirred much hatred for the American Gov’t and George Bush. He is also saying that it is going to be very difficult for the Katrina victims to recover. However, in some cases you can’t get over the disaster, and you just have to move on.\n\nThis line also refers to the countless victims who couldn’t get over this tragedy, either because they died or their houses were irreparably damaged. Everyone wants to overcome tragedy, but sometimes it is just too much to conquer.", "annotation_id": "106484", "url": "https://genius.com/106484", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tie My Hands", "status": "done", "targets": 27, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-tie-my-hands-lyrics", "genius_title": "Tie My Hands", "genius_id": 3781, "source": "api"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thunder*", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/A-ap-rocky-thunder-lyrics", "genius_title": "Thunder*", "genius_id": 6048946, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Life", "line": "Ratta-tatta, tatta-tatta, doves fly", "vault_lines": ["Ratta-tatta, tatta-tatta, doves fly"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is known for weaving movie references into his lyrics, showing his love for pop culture and cinema. This line is referring to a movie cliché called  foreboding fleeing birds trope, where birds take flight as an ominous sign or as a reaction to a loud noise. In this case, Wayne cinematically describes a scene where doves fly from the sound of gunshots.", "annotation_id": "33484364", "url": "https://genius.com/33484364", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Life", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-thug-life-lyrics", "genius_title": "Thug Life", "genius_id": 5821326, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "They don’t know what I been through Don’t know what I’m goin’ through As long as I get through, that’s what I look forward to", "vault_lines": ["They don't know what I been through"], "meaning": "Fax", "annotation_id": "16870610", "url": "https://genius.com/16870610", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Uh, wake up in the world and I’m just another nigga Call it public housin’ when you next door to the killers On them corners, it gets better as you go", "vault_lines": ["Uh, wake up in the world and I'm just another nigga"], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5236746150001", "annotation_id": "2876601", "url": "https://genius.com/2876601", "state": "verified", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Can’t believe we did it", "vault_lines": ["Can't believe we did it"], "meaning": "Mastermind was an album that had its fair share of delays, and many thought that due to the amount of delays the album may get shelved. The album was slated for a December 17th 2013 release, but the album did not drop officially until March 4th 2014.", "annotation_id": "2870129", "url": "https://genius.com/2870129", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Double M, we handle business", "vault_lines": ["Double M, we handle business"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is calling himself and Lil Wayne masterminds (“Double M” meaning masterminds), and that they are the big dogs handling business.\n\n\n\nAlso referring to MMG (Ross' Label) saying that they handle business.", "annotation_id": "2861279", "url": "https://genius.com/2861279", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "J.U.S.T.I.C.E. League", "vault_lines": ["Free as a bird, spoken word in my verse"], "meaning": "During a 2014 interview with XXL Magazine, Justice League explained how the track came about:\n\n\nRoss has sort of chosen to keep fucking with us because the chemistry is obviously there. Whether it be during the beginning stages of his projects or the ending stage of his project, we always kind of get in with him or get in contact with him about final touch ups or whatever. I think we all know that there’s a chemistry there.\n\nRoss tends to gravitate towards the music that we make that most people don’t know what to do with. They might be scared of it or they just don’t know what to do with it. He has a history of just picking off the beaten path basically. He’ll pick the tracks that nobody else picks and everyone is mad at us because we didn’t send them that beat or whatever. Most of the time, they just didn’t take that beat.\n\nThe hook was sung by Betty Idol. You gotta remember that the beat was made in 2013. So it was the twenty year anniversary of “93 ‘til Infinity.” So we did that. It was paying homage. That decade year long classic. We got Betty Idol to write and perform the hook on it. It was undeniable. We just went ahead and sent it off to Ross. You know Ross likes to push the boundaries, like we said earlier. Once you hear the hook? Ah, shit. That shit’s crazy.", "annotation_id": "2859528", "url": "https://genius.com/2859528", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Go against the odds youngin’, go and get a job Another country boy they want back on the farm", "vault_lines": ["Go against the odds youngin', go and get a job"], "meaning": "Possibly a nod to slavery times, from the previous line he is talking to a kid going against the odds.\nStatistics are against him and Rozay states they just want them back on the farm to pick cotton.\n\n\n\nMost likely the “yougin'” he’s talking about is African American, getting a job is going against the odds, as statistics say African Americans typically end up in prison before they turn 25—right where they want you.", "annotation_id": "2857155", "url": "https://genius.com/2857155", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Free as a bird, spoken word in my verse", "vault_lines": ["Free as a bird, spoken word in my verse"], "meaning": "‘Free as a bird’ is a common simile referring to the carelessness of one’s actions. Rozay can do whatever he wants. In his own words:\n\nThese niggas won’t hold me back – Rick Ross – “Hold Me Back”\n\n\n\nSpoken Word, generally speaking, is a common form of poetry. It generally focuses on the meanings of the words in a piece of work. Ross is telling stories in his verses, not just relying on a beat to give his song meaning.\n\nNotice the multi in this line:\n\nFree as a bird, spoken word in my verse.", "annotation_id": "2856265", "url": "https://genius.com/2856265", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "I lived on Billboard, where the fuck to go next?", "vault_lines": ["I lived on Billboard, where the fuck to go next?"], "meaning": "Rick Ross has been very successful on the Billboard charts during the span of his career—he’s had 13 singles as a lead artist and 12 as a featured artist chart in the top 100 as well as having four #1 albums and two #2 albums.\n\nAfter achieving all this success on the charts already, Ross ponders what can he do next?", "annotation_id": "2855201", "url": "https://genius.com/2855201", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Go get the yellow tape, it’s well orchestrated", "vault_lines": ["Go get the yellow tape, it's well orchestrated"], "meaning": "Yellow tape is used in a crime scene to keep bystanders at bay and prevent them from trespassing. Weezy also appeared on a Fat Joe song of the same name.", "annotation_id": "2854383", "url": "https://genius.com/2854383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Grind that motherfucker ’til it’s yellow brick road", "vault_lines": ["Grind that motherfucker 'til it's yellow brick road"], "meaning": "Referring to the famous film The Wizard of Oz: Rozay’s referring to grinding “bricks” as in cocaine, which can sell for the price of gold. This is how he had to try to make it at first hustling on the corners.\n\n\n\nThe music video also stars Wood Harris, known for playing drug kingpin Avon Barksdale in the The Wire. The video alludes to a cycle of drugs, violence, death, and incarceration that Ross managed to escape. http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jEy9ygty4JQ", "annotation_id": "2854372", "url": "https://genius.com/2854372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Money over bitches, first nigga with a Wraith", "vault_lines": ["Money over bitches, first nigga with a Wraith"], "meaning": "Rolls Royce released a new line of its luxurious cars called the Rolls Royce Wraith in 2014.It has been reported that this will be the fastest and most luxurious British car to be built by Rolls-Royce Ross claims he was the first nigga to own one of them", "annotation_id": "2851268", "url": "https://genius.com/2851268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Niggas hatin’ like it’s Salt Lake City", "vault_lines": ["Niggas hatin' like it's Salt Lake City"], "meaning": "Urban Dictionary says the meaning of Salty is:\nSALTY—to be pissed, upset\nFor example:\nShe was salty because she lost the game.\n\nSo basically he’s saying many haters or angry or salty about his success, making them envious.\nSo he’s comparing the amount of haters he has to, Salt Lake City. Get it?\nClever wordplay from “The Boss himself.\n\nHistorical Connection: Salt Lake City is where the Mormons finally settled after being hated on across the continental United States of America.", "annotation_id": "2851117", "url": "https://genius.com/2851117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "But to make a long story short, I need a shoulder ‘Cause the devil on one, the other one, I’m lookin’ over", "vault_lines": ["But to make a long story short, I need a shoulder"], "meaning": "To have a devil on ones shoulder is figurative language for having an inner-wicked voice attempting to influence you negatively. And the phrase I need a shoulder means having someone there for you to help.\n\nWayne is burdened by the devil on his shoulder and the constant need to look over his “other one” for potential threats; hence, he needs someone else’s shoulder to lie on for support. It’s good to have someone keep you company and help you when you’re being plagued by your inner-demons.\n\n\n\nNote: the implication that he needs another shoulder for support because the devil is weighing down on his.", "annotation_id": "2850786", "url": "https://genius.com/2850786", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Tunechi", "vault_lines": ["Free as a bird, spoken word in my verse"], "meaning": "Tunechi is one of Wayne’s many nicknames. He received this nickname from his grandmother and has been using it very frequently lately.", "annotation_id": "2850480", "url": "https://genius.com/2850480", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "On my knees prayin’, niggas shootin’ in the church", "vault_lines": ["On my knees prayin', niggas shootin' in the church"], "meaning": "Even while he is in Church praying for an end to violence, there are hits being carried out inside that very Church. This exemplifies just how hard the it is in the hood, Churches are supposed to be a place of peace and repentance, but in the hood they are just another place of sin and murder.", "annotation_id": "2850456", "url": "https://genius.com/2850456", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Get a bad bitch that posts up like comments", "vault_lines": ["Get a bad bitch that posts up like comments"], "meaning": "Wayne makes his hoes post up on street corners to sell themselves and bring him back the money they’ve earned.\n\n\n\nOf course, this being Wayne, he feels the need to tie this into a metaphor/punchline. This time playing on the word “post” and making it relate to posting comments online.", "annotation_id": "2850442", "url": "https://genius.com/2850442", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Came from the hood, ain’t nothing changed Still lemon pepper on my motherfuckin’ wings!", "vault_lines": ["Came from the hood, ain't nothing changed"], "meaning": "Ross loves his Wingstop, he even owns several of them.\n\n\n\nFun fact: This marks the 3rd Wingstop reference for Ross on Mastermind. More specifically, the second Lemon Pepper wing reference.", "annotation_id": "2850222", "url": "https://genius.com/2850222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "200 acres estates, a young nigga made it", "vault_lines": ["200 acres estates, a young nigga made it"], "meaning": "He recently purchased the largest mansion in Georgia, 54,000 square-foot with 109 rooms and sits on 235 acres. It was previously owned by retired boxer Evander Holyfield.", "annotation_id": "2850203", "url": "https://genius.com/2850203", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Can’t trust none of these niggas, I murk one of these niggas Then bury one of these niggas, still got dirt under my fingers That ain’t a threat, that’s a bet ‘Cause they comin’ at my neck like the best a man can get", "vault_lines": ["Can't trust none of these niggas, I murk one of these niggas"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne feels like he’s surrounded by shifty characters—he can’t tell the real from the fake. He’s not afraid to kill someone who wronged him; he’ll even bury their body. He’s so casual about it that hasn’t even bothered to scrub the dirt from under his fingernails after burying the last guy he killed.\n\n\n\nHe uses a simile to compare the people who are “coming at his neck” to the motto of razor company Gilette (“the best a man can get”). Razors are used on the face and neck, which Wayne cleverly relates to the rappers that are coming for his neck, or coming to kill him or take his throne in the rap game.", "annotation_id": "2850192", "url": "https://genius.com/2850192", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Smokin’ on that loud, pumpin’ up that volume", "vault_lines": ["Smokin' on that loud, pumpin' up that volume"], "meaning": "Double Entendre:\n\n1) Weezy is smoking more often, likely to cope with the fact that he feels he’s putting out mediocre music as of late.\n\n2) Wayne’s weed is strong, or loud, so he plays on the word volume to illustrate that his marijuana is now better than ever.", "annotation_id": "2850136", "url": "https://genius.com/2850136", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Get it crackin’ like an eggshell in this motherfucker, make omelettes", "vault_lines": ["Get it crackin' like an eggshell in this motherfucker, make omelettes"], "meaning": "“Get cracking” is an expression meaning to act quickly. Wayne relates this to the literal cracking of “eggshells,” and eggs are a primary ingredient in the popular breakfast meal, “omelettes.”\n\nHe also spits a similar line on his 2006 song, “Cannons”.\n\nNiggas know I’m sick, I don’t spit, I vomit, got it?\nOne egg short of the omelet", "annotation_id": "2850093", "url": "https://genius.com/2850093", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Thug cry for Mac Dre, nigga, throwin’ up Thizz", "vault_lines": ["Thug cry for Mac Dre, nigga, throwin' up Thizz"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is shouting out Mac Dre, who was one of the legends of the Hyphy movement in Hip-Hop, who helped popularize the term thizz, which refers to the effects of and the consumption of ecstasy, and features its own hand signal (which Mac Dre is displaying in the image above).\n\nMac Dre often used the term in his music and founded his own record label called Thizz Entertainment.  Unfortunately Mac Dre was shot and killed in Kansas City, Missouri on November 1st, 2004.", "annotation_id": "2850001", "url": "https://genius.com/2850001", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Thug Cry", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-thug-cry-lyrics", "genius_title": "Thug Cry", "genius_id": 365956, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throwed", "line": "Your honor, I ain’t a dealer, I’m a user, ya dig?", "vault_lines": ["Your honor, I ain't a dealer, I'm a user, ya dig?"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne swears that he doesn’t distribute drugs, he just uses them. Lil Wayne has also said this before during an interview:\n\nI haven’t sold a brick. I haven’t had to see one unless I wanted to snort it!”\n\nThis line is Lil Wayne talking to a judge. Weezy had recently served a year on Rikers Island, however, it was just for a gun charge.", "annotation_id": "2094575", "url": "https://genius.com/2094575", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throwed", "line": "Blood gang, bitch, big Bs, Boston Bruins", "vault_lines": ["Blood gang, bitch, big Bs, Boston Bruins"], "meaning": "Notice the good alliteration of the “B”. In case you didn’t here it is again.\n\nBlood gang bitch, big B’s, Boston Bruins\n\nThe Blood gang often rep themselves by wearing Boston Bruin merchandise because they come with big B’s on them. B as in Blood.", "annotation_id": "285886", "url": "https://genius.com/285886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throwed", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-throwed-lyrics", "genius_title": "Throwed", "genius_id": 52332, "source": "api"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw Some D’s Freestyle", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-throw-some-ds-freestyle-lyrics", "genius_title": "Throw Some D’s Freestyle", "genius_id": 1822178, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "I throw it up like a cap and tassel (Yeah), I got my rap diploma (Oh)", "vault_lines": ["I throw it up like a cap and tassel (Yeah), I got my rap diploma (Oh)"], "meaning": "A diploma is a certificate or deed issued by an educational institution, such as a college or university, that testifies that the recipient has successfully completed a particular course of study or confers an academic degree. In countries such as the United Kingdom and Australia, the word diploma refers to a level of academic award. Ludacris has his so called rap diploma, celebrating by throwing his cap and tassel in the air, often used to celebrate at the end of a graduation ceremony.", "annotation_id": "8427674", "url": "https://genius.com/8427674", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "I Got them burners on ya, have you lookin’ at a Russian Ruger", "vault_lines": ["I Got them burners on ya, have you lookin' at a Russian Ruger"], "meaning": "Ludacris has made it known throughout his music that him and his DTP affiliates like to be armed at all times. So having a gun such as a Russian Ruger shouldn’t come as a surprise.", "annotation_id": "8427541", "url": "https://genius.com/8427541", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "With Flipmode and DTP, shit be gettin’ ugly Weezy, tell ’em what you rep (I represent Young Money)", "vault_lines": ["With Flipmode and DTP, shit be gettin' ugly"], "meaning": "All three artist are representing their respective record labels. Flipmode Entertainment was the name of Busta Rhymes record label at the time (before the name was changed to The Conglomerate). Ludacris is representing his record label DTP, acronym for Disturbing Tha Peace. Lil Wayne is known for his record label Young Money Entertainment and it’s former joint unity with Birdman’s Cash Money Records.", "annotation_id": "8427434", "url": "https://genius.com/8427434", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "My niggas fight over ice like we been playin’ hockey", "vault_lines": ["My niggas fight over ice like we been playin' hockey"], "meaning": "“Ice” in Hip-Hop is often referred as diamonds. Rappers have used the word on their bars several times to flaunt about their wealth, like Lil Wayne raps on Kanye West’s “Barry Bonds”:\n\nMy teeth and my ice so white like Shady\nIce in my teeth so refrigerated\n\nHockey is played on ice and is one of the most dangerous sports in the world, with many fights being started as confrontations rise. Since all the diamond jewelry would be expensive, the fights that would occur over them would be pretty brutal.", "annotation_id": "8025350", "url": "https://genius.com/8025350", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "You couldn’t fill my shoes, You couldn’t fill my jockey", "vault_lines": ["You couldn't fill my shoes, You couldn't fill my jockey"], "meaning": "Ludacris is one of the last of a dying breed and considered an OG when it comes to southern rappers, repping his southern routes proudly in his music. Any rapper would be foolish to think that they could take Luda’s place, for they simply don’t have the talent to fill his shoes or the balls to even try.", "annotation_id": "8025338", "url": "https://genius.com/8025338", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "Hip-Hop’s God in these jeans, now that’s true religion", "vault_lines": ["Hip-Hop's God in these jeans, now that's true religion"], "meaning": "True Religion Brand Jeans is an American clothing company. True Religion Brand Jeans sell what they call “the best of the best and premium denim”, fabricated in America. Luda plays on the fact that he considers himself hip hop’s God, with hip hop being the true religion.", "annotation_id": "8025327", "url": "https://genius.com/8025327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "And the way that I come through And kill every single thing when I rhyme", "vault_lines": ["And the way that I come through"], "meaning": "The way Busta spits into the mic leaves everyone in awe at his amazing lyricism. It’s even so powerful that, if he put his words into action, they could potentially cause for the wipe-out of every living organism on the planet.\n\n\n\nSounds like a real Extinction Level Event, huh.", "annotation_id": "3994148", "url": "https://genius.com/3994148", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "When I spit, niggas be askin’ “Who dat” It be the god And I know you niggas really wanna know how the hell I “Do dat”", "vault_lines": ["When I spit, niggas be askin' \"Who dat\" It be the god"], "meaning": "Whenever you hear Busta Rhymes at the mic, your immediate reaction is “Whose this laying down hard verses?”, even though he knows what you really mean is “How is he rapping this adequately?”.\n\n\n\nWhen asking either of these questions, the answer to them is simply that Busta is a god and puts together verses that have a heavenly flow.\n\nThis isn’t the first time a rapper has compared themselves a god, even Butsa himself has made a statement such as this in a previous verse. Looks like the all-mighty Yeezus might be having some competition to look out for.", "annotation_id": "3920640", "url": "https://genius.com/3920640", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "Hey welcome to the jungle, and I’m the lion (Ahh)", "vault_lines": ["Hey welcome to the jungle, and I'm the lion (Ahh)"], "meaning": "Wayne welcomes all the new coming rappers into the rap game. He compares the rap game to a jungle considering they both contain a wide variety of species and are marked by their level of skill like a food chain. Of course, in this “jungle” Wayne has put himself on top as the one and only king of the jungle.\n\n\n\nNote that this is also a reference to the classic Guns N' Roses song, “Welcome to The Jungle”.", "annotation_id": "3917397", "url": "https://genius.com/3917397", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "line": "I’m about to blast off, call it rocket science", "vault_lines": ["I'm about to blast off, call it rocket science"], "meaning": "Watch out, Wayne is about to go in heavy on his verse and is showing no mercy! I guess this is understandable considering he is rapping with some pretty talented speed rappers, even though he has proven to hold his own on tracks like “Look at Me Now”.\n\nHe compares his initial blast into this verse to a high powered blast that follows a rocket once it takes off. This is one of the rare occasions in which you can’t say “It’s not rocket science”.\n\n\n\nThis could also be a reference to marijuana cigarettes which can go by the name “rockets” (A topic touched on with his song “Grindin'”). Wayne could be lighting up before spitting his verse which may help get him loose and rap more tastefully.", "annotation_id": "3917048", "url": "https://genius.com/3917048", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It Up", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Busta-rhymes-throw-it-up-lyrics", "genius_title": "Throw It Up", "genius_id": 496351, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "I’m gon’ cop my baby something with no ceilings", "vault_lines": ["I'm gon' cop my baby something with no ceilings"], "meaning": "Wayne is going to get his baby a vehicle with a convertible roof", "annotation_id": "31946137", "url": "https://genius.com/31946137", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "My pockets too deep, I fuck around and drown her, Weez’!", "vault_lines": ["My pockets too deep, I fuck around and drown her, Weez'!"], "meaning": "Weezy is saying that he has so much money in his pockets, that he’ll fuck around and drown his girl with money.\n\nThis line sums up the whole first verse (and alludes to the whole theme of the song) where Wayne references many top tier brands and activities that he’ll spend on his girl.", "annotation_id": "21119659", "url": "https://genius.com/21119659", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "Okay, swimming in Polo", "vault_lines": ["Okay, swimming in Polo"], "meaning": "“Polo” is a Name Brand designed by Ralph Lauren, so Wayne swims in Polo trunks.\n    -Also Wayne may play the game Marco Polo, or even the sport Water Polo. This would connect to the next part of the bar because Jordan is a Brand Name Athletic, Casual, Stylish apparel developed by Nike.", "annotation_id": "17943464", "url": "https://genius.com/17943464", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "You can’t be broke and happy, so, me—I’m mad rich", "vault_lines": ["You can't be broke and happy, so, me—I'm mad rich"], "meaning": "Few things going on here:\n-Wayne starts the bar out saying “you can’t be broke(no money)and happy,(emotion)”\n“So me, I’m madd(emotion)rich(lots of money)\n-This is a clever play on the saying “money can’t/don’t by happiness”\n-Wayne is saying, money may not buy happiness, but you’re also not happy when you’re broke(due to stress)So me I’m madd rich! Since I can’t be happy(broke/rich)I will  be madd and keep my money.(Weezy laugh)\n-Madd is slang that usually refers to “having a lot of” or “being crazy or absurd”", "annotation_id": "17943157", "url": "https://genius.com/17943157", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "And I live on the beach you can get that panty line out", "vault_lines": ["And I live on the beach you can get that panty line out"], "meaning": "Given the beach reference, I think “tan line” is what Wayne was going for here. He seems to be making a generous offer to let his girl sunbathe naked at his place, so as to get rid of any unsightly tan lines.\n(Apparently Wayne’s girl already sunbathes topless though.)", "annotation_id": "13867313", "url": "https://genius.com/13867313", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "Faviana prom dress with your spine out", "vault_lines": ["Faviana prom dress with your spine out"], "meaning": "Faviana is a New York-based designer of prom, evening, and celebrity dresses.\nWayne’s girl is sporting a backless or low back model.", "annotation_id": "13867308", "url": "https://genius.com/13867308", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "Catch us at the game, sitting on the baseline", "vault_lines": ["Catch us at the game, sitting on the baseline"], "meaning": "No cheap seats for Tunechi.", "annotation_id": "13840550", "url": "https://genius.com/13840550", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "I bought her ass a Mac, now we be iChatting", "vault_lines": ["I bought her ass a Mac, now we be iChatting"], "meaning": "iChat was an instant messaging software application developed for the Mac OS X operating system.\n\n\n\nIt was first released in 2002. Apparently Wayne and his girl weren’t early adopters.\nThe last version came out in early 2012. Starting with OS X 10.8 (“Mountain Lion”), iChat was replaced by Messages.", "annotation_id": "13840434", "url": "https://genius.com/13840434", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "I take her to the vet ’cause she a bad bitch", "vault_lines": ["I take her to the vet 'cause she a bad bitch"], "meaning": "Playing with “bitch” as the term for a female dog.", "annotation_id": "11712580", "url": "https://genius.com/11712580", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "Buying her them Fendi shoes", "vault_lines": ["Buying her them Fendi shoes"], "meaning": "Fendi is an Italian luxury fashion house whose specialities include leather goods, fragrances, eyewear, timepieces and writing instruments.\n\nFendi shoes aren’t really cheap, so by saying “buying her them Fendi shoes”, Wayne wants to show that it isn’t a problem for him to buy such expensive shoes.", "annotation_id": "3378388", "url": "https://genius.com/3378388", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Throw It In The Bag", "line": "She wear that on my jet, I fuck her after jet lag", "vault_lines": ["She wear that on my jet, I fuck her after jet lag"], "meaning": "A continuation from the line above.\n\nJet Lag: extreme tiredness and other physical effects felt by a person after a long flight across several time zones.\n\nThe girl Wayne is describing is wearing Louis flip-flips and pink sweat pants. After she got off a jet to see Wayne or when she got back from someplace, she is extremely tired but they still end up having sex.", "annotation_id": "3045031", "url": "https://genius.com/3045031", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
