{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "My charm Starburst, my watch Skittles", "vault_lines": ["My charm Starburst, my watch Skittles"], "meaning": "Here Wayne says his charm is like Starburst sweets. He could be insinuating that when he turns his charm on with ladies he is a real sweet-talker. Or perhaps he is suggesting that like his personal charm is “bursting with flavour” like the old Starburst slogan.\nHe also says his watch is Skittles, implying his watch is multi-coloured; most likely due to him having various coloured diamonds embezzled in the watch, or else in reference to the array of colours that catch the eye when his diamond watch glistens in the light.", "annotation_id": "18980696", "url": "https://genius.com/18980696", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "Give you your issue, nina wanna kiss you", "vault_lines": ["Give you your issue, nina wanna kiss you"], "meaning": "Weezy often refers to his pistol as “Nina” (derived from nine, as in a 9 millimeter handgun) and is using kiss as a metaphor for shooting.", "annotation_id": "18980677", "url": "https://genius.com/18980677", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "I’m the Birdman, Birdman Jr., bitch", "vault_lines": ["I'm the Birdman, Birdman Jr., bitch"], "meaning": "Wayne gives a shoutout to his Young Money mentor Birdman (aka Brian “Baby” Williams). Wayne and Birdman were inseparable for years, and first met in the mid-90’s during the early days of Young Money Entertainment. Lil Wayne has often compared himself to his father figure, calling himself “Birdman, Jr.” in several songs.", "annotation_id": "17662015", "url": "https://genius.com/17662015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "Holla at a player, I’m sorta like BD Y’all can’t stop my dribble or block the lay-up", "vault_lines": ["Holla at a player, I'm sorta like BD"], "meaning": "BD is Baron Davis, who averaged 22.9 points and 7.5 assists and played in the All Star Game for Weezy’s New Orleans Hornets during the 2003-2004 season.", "annotation_id": "16634084", "url": "https://genius.com/16634084", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "If gettin’ money is wrong, I ain’t right at all", "vault_lines": ["If gettin' money is wrong, I ain't right at all"], "meaning": "By 2004, Weezy had already released three studio albums (The Carter was his fourth), all of them certified by the RIAA. His debut The Block Is Hot (1999) went platinum on december 1999. His next albums, Lights Out (2000) and 500 Degreez (2002) went gold less then a year after being released.\n\nTherefore, it’s fair to assume Wayne had already sold a lot of CDs to back up the claim that “he ain’t right at all”. Today, Wayne is one of the richest rappers in the game.", "annotation_id": "11487796", "url": "https://genius.com/11487796", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "What up, Reel? Come on", "vault_lines": ["What up, Reel? Come on"], "meaning": "Shouting out one of his earliest collaborators, he was heavily featured in a lot of Wayne’s older work, most notably on this album session tracks and songs such as “I Miss My Dawgs”, “Everything Will Be Fine” and “On My Own”.", "annotation_id": "3915895", "url": "https://genius.com/3915895", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "I’m an offense coordinator, run and shoot", "vault_lines": ["I'm an offense coordinator, run and shoot"], "meaning": "A play on words of the American Football offense known as Run and Shoot and using it to mean, well, running up on people and shooting them.", "annotation_id": "1407415", "url": "https://genius.com/1407415", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "I got La-Z-Boy bank, that’s comfort loot", "vault_lines": ["I got La-Z-Boy bank, that's comfort loot"], "meaning": "La-Z-Boy is a well-known manufacturer of recliners and other comfortable furniture. Wayne takes the notion of “comfort” and extends it to suggest that he makes enough money (“bank/loot”) to “live comfortably”\n\nBut wouldn’t it be nice if the La-Z Boy company ACTUALLY had a bank? Imagine mobile chairs guiding you to the teller..", "annotation_id": "17006", "url": "https://genius.com/17006", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "I’m still Apple Eagle Weezle 1-7 Hollygrove, never, ever leave you", "vault_lines": ["I'm still Apple Eagle Weezle"], "meaning": "1-7 Hollygrove is the section on New Orleans Lil' Wayne is from. More specifically, the intersection of Apple Street and Eagle Street", "annotation_id": "16897", "url": "https://genius.com/16897", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Is the Carter", "line": "You watch your grill, what you oughta do ’Fore your ribs get barbecued", "vault_lines": ["You watch your grill, what you oughta do"], "meaning": "Playing on “grill” as in ‘face’ and grill as in ‘cooking device.’ “Ribs” continues the double meaning of the cooking imagery – Wayne is threatening to literally barbecue the ribs in your body, not some ribs you bought at the Winn-Dixie or some shit", "annotation_id": "7924", "url": "https://genius.com/7924", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
