{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "There’s an arms race, like I’m runnin’ on my hands", "vault_lines": ["There's an arms race, like I'm runnin' on my hands"], "meaning": "Lupe Fiasco begins with a metaphor comparing himself to someone involved in an arms race. But instead of a typical arms race, he’s saying he’s running on his hands, which adds a sense of unnatural effort or doing something impossible.", "annotation_id": "34737028", "url": "https://genius.com/34737028", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "But don’t gas yourself up like Sylvia Plath, jeez", "vault_lines": ["But don't gas yourself up like Sylvia Plath, jeez"], "meaning": "Sylvia Plath was a poet who died by suicide using a gas oven.\n“Gas yourself up” usually means hyping yourself up—but here, it’s twisted into a literal death reference.", "annotation_id": "34736977", "url": "https://genius.com/34736977", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "On top of that, this track is cracked like burnt Brillo (damn)", "vault_lines": ["On top of that, this track is cracked like burnt Brillo (damn)"], "meaning": "“Cracked” means fire (amazing), but also references crack cocaine.\n “Burnt Brillo” refers to how crack cocaine is smoked, where Brillo pads are used as filters.\n\nThis connects back to “get low”, because drug slang and street life often run parallel to the music industry’s corruption.", "annotation_id": "34736944", "url": "https://genius.com/34736944", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "I pledge allegiance to gasoline and bulletproof limousines And leans on the property of the poor And every night, I pray to the Lords Of War", "vault_lines": ["I pledge allegiance to gasoline and bulletproof limousines"], "meaning": "In the United States, the Pledge of Allegiance is an oath pledging allegiance to both one’s flag and country:\n\nI pledge allegiance to the Flag of the United States of America, and to the Republic for which it stands, one Nation under God, indivisible, with liberty and justice for all.\n\nWith these lines, Lupe implies that governments are not concerned with these values, but instead care more for gasoline and war. Rather than help the poor, the government wastes hundreds of billions of dollars on wars, effectively putting a burden on those less fortunate (read George Orwell’s 1984 for a better understanding). Especially in the past few decades, the US has been involved in countless wars in the middle east (a very oil-rich region) which many believe are conducted over oil.\n\n\n\nSupporting this claim is the fact that the United States has a very large military-industrial complex. In fact, in 2013, the US government spent $672.9 billion on the Department of Defence (which includes overseas military operations). Add to this the fact that the US has the largest oil consumption in the world at roughly 19 million oil barrels/day (nearly double that of second-place China) and you can certainly make a compelling argument.", "annotation_id": "4273957", "url": "https://genius.com/4273957", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "Drop mine’s first so that they bombs can’t", "vault_lines": ["Drop mine's first so that they bombs can't"], "meaning": "A preemptive war is when attacks are made before a formal declaration of war in an attempt to subdue a perceived offensive, or to gain a strategic advantage in a future conflict. This line refers to dropping bombs on an enemy so they don’t have a chance to bomb you.\n\nThe US in particular is well known for using preemptive strikes/wars, and often justifying these conflicts without concrete evidence. A vivid example is the fairly recent Iraq War in 2003, where the US invaded Iraq over allegations that they had weapons of mass destruction and therefore posed a threat to national security. No such weapons were found.", "annotation_id": "4273691", "url": "https://genius.com/4273691", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "Right now they got that number one spot Do you want that?", "vault_lines": ["Right now they got that number one spot"], "meaning": "Infinity On High dropped and reached #1 on the Billboard 200 upon release.", "annotation_id": "517687", "url": "https://genius.com/517687", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "Now I don’t know what the hell this song is talkin’ bout Do you?", "vault_lines": ["Now I don't know what the hell this song is talkin' bout"], "meaning": "Kanye is poking fun at Stump’s notoriously difficult-to-understand delivery, low key referencing the misheard lyrics videos that went viral in March ’07 as the original FOB song got Top 40 airplay; Kanye was one of the thousands of people who had no idea what the hell Stump was saying, no matter how many times they heard the song (“Also into cats, also into cats\").\n\nThough, Kanye isn’t just baffled by the song’s delivery. The content, too, seems absurd. The title and refrain “this ain’t a scene, it’s a goddamn arms race” invokes a longing for a scene that isn’t so competitive, that isn’t “so addicted and obsessed with new arts, cultures and loves”, but Kanye, fighting for recognition not just as a producer but also as a rapper and celebrating the intense competition that has been vital to hip-hop since day one, has never known a scene that isn’t an arms race.\n\nTo ask for a scene that’s less competitive, less obsessed with creating new forms of art and breaking down barriers between arts and cultures, is to bite the hand that feeds. Certainly, FOB wouldn’t have “raced straight to the top” if they didn’t satisfy this very desire for newness, if they didn’t make a new weapon, and isn’t that exactly what this remix is? Kanye knows it.", "annotation_id": "517683", "url": "https://genius.com/517683", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "That all good child rebel soldiers go to AK-47 heaven", "vault_lines": ["That all good child rebel soldiers go to AK-47 heaven"], "meaning": "Lupe is shouting out to his supergroup, Child Rebel Soldier which consists of himself, Kanye West and Pharrell Williams.\n\nAK-47 is the nickname of Pete Wentz’s Angel & Kings clubs. Angels & Kings has a location in Chicago where Lupe Fiasco and Fall Out Boy are both from.", "annotation_id": "444623", "url": "https://genius.com/444623", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "This ain’t an arms race, it’s Apocalypto", "vault_lines": ["This ain't an arms race, it's Apocalypto"], "meaning": "The nuclear arms race FOB referred to in the original is going to be the end of the world. Also a reference to the Mel Gibson directed movie Apocalypto, about the end of the Mayan empire.", "annotation_id": "224654", "url": "https://genius.com/224654", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "line": "I’m cool as the Fonz, and deadly as Charles Bronson I’m Gonzo, you can call me Travie S. Thompson", "vault_lines": ["I'm cool as the Fonz, and deadly as Charles Bronson"], "meaning": "Arthur Fonzarelli is known as a cool character while Charles Bronson is known for his characters who are deadly with a gun.\n\n\n\n\n\nTravis Troy, co-founder of Pure Gonzo Music, has gained a reputation amongst his followers for turning out venues with the newest and rarest in fresh and amazing .", "annotation_id": "205723", "url": "https://genius.com/205723", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Fall-out-boy-this-aint-a-scene-its-an-arms-race-2007-vma-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "This Ain’t a Scene, It’s an Arms Race (2007 VMA Remix)", "genius_id": 31873, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Third Strike", "line": "Tight circles in the race car, an eye jerker Or did I mean an eye catcher? Vice versa", "vault_lines": ["Tight circles in the race car, an eye jerker"], "meaning": "This is some clever Weezy, if you switch “eye jerker” and “eye catcher”, as implied by vice versa, you see the pun on “[eye] mean”", "annotation_id": "15022039", "url": "https://genius.com/15022039", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Third Strike", "line": "Talkin’ to myself my homies call me crazy", "vault_lines": ["Talkin' to myself my homies call me crazy"], "meaning": "This is not the first time Wayne mentions he is talking to himself. In Tha Carter IV lead single, “6 Foot 7 Foot,“ Wayne remarks,\n\nTalking to myself because I am my own consultant\n\nWayne talking to himself may come from a distrust of other people, and he knows the only person he can trust on all issues is himself, even if his closest friends think he is insane for it.", "annotation_id": "15000853", "url": "https://genius.com/15000853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Third Strike", "line": "Don’t seem to get why you earthlings observe me It irks me", "vault_lines": ["Don't seem to get why you earthlings observe me"], "meaning": "Over the years, Wayne has repeatedly referred to himself as a martian, being from out of this world. His Tha Carter III song, Phone Home , begins with a greeting from Planet Weezy.\nWayne is tired of people from Earth working to observe him, as it bothers them. He may feel people do not understand him, which is a nod to his tattoo on his face which reads, “Misunderstood”.", "annotation_id": "14963197", "url": "https://genius.com/14963197", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Third Strike", "line": "I’m tired of these motherfuckers, cut strings I’m first string", "vault_lines": ["I'm tired of these motherfuckers, cut strings"], "meaning": "To cut strings is to cease support or cut what relationship ties you have to another person. Wayne throughout the song hints that this is a constant issue pestering him.", "annotation_id": "14956089", "url": "https://genius.com/14956089", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
