{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "She with it if I’m with it, and I’m with it", "vault_lines": ["And she with it if I'm with it, and I'm with it"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne references “Miss Me” by Drake again in these lyrics:\n\n“And girl i’m fuckin’ serious, I’m with it if you with it”", "annotation_id": "35412362", "url": "https://genius.com/35412362", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "They want some new trim, we lust for some trust", "vault_lines": ["They want some new trim, we lust for some trust"], "meaning": "The famous ones with all the poonani just want a meaningful connection and the normal men with a meaningful relationship want the playboy lifestyle", "annotation_id": "31687868", "url": "https://genius.com/31687868", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "Niggas that are married don’t wanna go home But we look up to them, they wish they were us", "vault_lines": ["Niggas that are married don't wanna go home"], "meaning": "In these verses Andre alludes to the common trope, “We always want what we don’t have”. That is to say that married men seem to wish to have a single life again and be polygamous, however, on the other end of the spectrum those men that spend their time in strip clubs or with multiple women may wish for the opposite. In a following verse he mentions that both types of people end up color blind as they believe the “grass is greener” on the other side, but each neglects to be content in their own life as it is. In the end, both mindsets result in a lose-lose scenario (hence both become colorblind).", "annotation_id": "30436720", "url": "https://genius.com/30436720", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "And those who say they don’t, souls will later on say to them, “That ain’t true” All of them will have an opinion But y’all know what y’all can do with them", "vault_lines": ["All of them ain't all equipped"], "meaning": "Here, Andre 3000 is saying: to those people whom have worked through their problems, stemming from traumas, ego, etc…\n\nOther people will only look at to detest them, because they cannot believe somebody else has actually worked on themselves, rather than being as low as them… and using distractions/addictions to get away from them.\n\nAnd those ‘souls’ will only later on go on to study this person to look for the tiniest of flaw/ abnormality to blow it out of proportion and claim they were right and they’re just as low as them, with their ‘addictions’. to justify to them self and make themselves feel better or in an attempt to aggravate this person in the hope they will react and lower them self to their level/ standards.\n\n2nd line:\nwhich is why everyone always has something to say (an opinion) regardless of the truth or facts because they are just lowly addicts looking for an escape, a distraction.\n\nso to them he says you can shove your opinion up your a**.\n\np.s. this was my first time doing a citation I hope I did a good job :)", "annotation_id": "27992948", "url": "https://genius.com/27992948", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "Houston girls love the way it goes down Atlanta girls love the way it goes down Vegas girls love the way it goes down", "vault_lines": ["Atlanta girls love the way it goes down"], "meaning": "Drake has previously shown love for Houston, Atlanta and Vegas in “Houstatlantavegas.” Additionally, H-Town’s influence throughout Take Care is evident.\n\n\n\nWhy the allure for these three cities? Las Vegas has been a place rappers flock to as it’s known for its famous nightlife (as well as strippers). Atlanta is home of the Andre 3000, who is featured on this song. Also, Atlanta is a booming industry in hip hop. Lastly, Houston is home to ‘chopped and screwed music,’ which has a big influence on the South as well as Drake’s mentor Lil Wayne, who is also featured on the song. Each city holds a special place in the three rappers who are on “The Real Her.”", "annotation_id": "8518351", "url": "https://genius.com/8518351", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "[Verse 4: André 3000]", "vault_lines": ["People around you should really have nothing to say"], "meaning": "Andre 3000 rarely appears on a guest verse. 3 stacks only appears on highly anticipated albums and friends music, which can be credited to his rumoured charge of $250,000 per feature. This verse from Andre was ranked his 15th best feature by The Boom Box.\n\nThis feature was never intended to be on Take Care. Drake tried to get Andre on his first album Thank Me Later, but didn’t work out:\n\nI really tried to do a song with Andre 3000, but I think it was meant for my second album. [An]Dre is one of those people that needs you to do a little more. He’s one of those people that believe in his craft so much that he needs you to do more than just be overhyped or just be famous. In his mind, I have to prove myself and the truth is, I do. That’s just being real. I have a lot of work to do, so I respect him for that. I think on the second album I’ll probably have only two features. I have a few people in mind and [An]Dre probably will be one of them.\n\nLuckily for us, Drizzy must’ve proven himself worthy of Andre’s vocals.", "annotation_id": "8518293", "url": "https://genius.com/8518293", "state": "accepted", "votes": 116, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "They keep telling me don’t save you", "vault_lines": ["They keep telling me don't save you"], "meaning": "As The Mighty D.R.E. enlightened us, you can’t make a ho a housewife. So Drake’s friends are trying to convince him not to buy this stripper out of her job to be with him.", "annotation_id": "998687", "url": "https://genius.com/998687", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "And the weekend’s here, started it right Even if you only get part of it right", "vault_lines": ["But the weekend's here, started it right"], "meaning": "Drake gives another little sly shoutout to his friend, XO artist The Weeknd, who is also on the album. The actual weekend is one of the most celebrated things in life. “Even if we only get part of it right” Is implying since he got the start right, he doesn’t get the end right. The song has been compared to the song “I Don’t Wanna” by Aaliyah.", "annotation_id": "761934", "url": "https://genius.com/761934", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "And I can tell that she wants a baby And I can yell, girl, that you’re crazy Oh, what the hell? Nope, can’t be lazy Please be careful, bitches got the rabies", "vault_lines": ["And I can tell that she wants a baby"], "meaning": "A clever way of warning folks to watch out for gold diggers.\n\nThree Stacks is being asked by this girl to be the father of her child. He is starting to be tempted by her, and says “what the hell”, but then realizes that’s a bad idea. Bitches are female dogs, and rabies are a disease that they have- so giving into this girl and going unprotected could end up giving him an STD. That’s what he’s really warning about.", "annotation_id": "441023", "url": "https://genius.com/441023", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "Listening to Adele, I feel you, baby “Someone Like You,” more like someone unlike you Or something that’s familiar maybe", "vault_lines": ["Listening to Adele, I feel you, baby"], "meaning": "Andre can relate to Adele’s songs on her album, 21, in which many songs have the vibe/theme of how you feel after a bad break-up.\n\n\n\nHe references one of her popular singles, “Someone Like You,” a ballad that was inspired by the ending of Adele’s 18-month relationship, and speaks of her coming to terms with it.\n\nWith that said, he expresses his anger by saying that the person he was with was not like him.", "annotation_id": "434981", "url": "https://genius.com/434981", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "Now the both of us are colorblind ’cause the other side looks greener Which leaves your turf in a Boise state Can’t see a play or the team, ’cause", "vault_lines": ["Now the both of us are colorblind 'cause the other side looks greener"], "meaning": "The Boise State Broncos play on a blue field instead of a green field. You can’t see them because they also wear blue uniforms.\n\n\n\nGoing off on the “colorblind” line, saying “the other side looks greener” means it looks better for the future, but the Boise state line deals with the fact that they are sad. Being “blue” is synonymous for being sad; it’s hard to see things for what they really are when you are sad\n\nBoise states turf is blue, so the “cuz” that leads into the next line could also be a reference to Crips, who refer to members of their gang as cuz or cousin, and whose main color is blue. A little more color wordplay.", "annotation_id": "434964", "url": "https://genius.com/434964", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "And I know I’m not supposed to judge a book by its cover I don’t wan’ be in the blind, but sometimes I Stevie Wonder", "vault_lines": ["And I know I'm not supposed to judge a book by its cover"], "meaning": "Wayne continues in the long tradition of Stevie Wonder “blind” metaphors, something he has also done before.\n\n\n\nIronically, this line also goes hand in hand with the song “Doing it Wrong” on Take Care. The song has a Stevie Wonder harmonica solo at the end and it is the track right before “The Real Her” on the album.", "annotation_id": "408579", "url": "https://genius.com/408579", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "The Real Her", "line": "’Cause to her, I’m just a rapper and soon, she’ll have met another", "vault_lines": ["'Cause to her, I'm just a rapper and soon, she'll have met another"], "meaning": "Here, Wayne recycles one of Drake’s lines from a previous collaboration of theirs, 2010’s “Miss Me.", "annotation_id": "408562", "url": "https://genius.com/408562", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
