{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tha Blues", "line": "Technically I’ll murder anyone who disrespectin’ me", "vault_lines": ["Technically I'll murder anyone who disrespectin' me"], "meaning": "Wayne could mean two things by this:\n\n\nRap wise, if anyone doesn’t treat him with respect him, he will lyrically kill them.\nIf anyone doesn’t respect Wayne in his hood or on the streets, he will kill them physically.", "annotation_id": "974757", "url": "https://genius.com/974757", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tha Blues", "status": "done", "targets": 8, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-tha-blues-lyrics", "genius_title": "Tha Blues", "genius_id": 28698, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tha Block Is Hot", "line": "It’s the spot where they got fire girls and hot boys", "vault_lines": ["It's the spot where they got fire girls and hot boys"], "meaning": "“Hot boys” could be a reference to the New Orleans rap group that Wayne was a part of at the age of 15 in 1997.\n\n“The Hot Boys (often styled as Hot Boy$) are an American hip hop group formed in New Orleans, Louisiana, in 1997. The group is made up of B.G., Lil Wayne, Juvenile and Turk.\n\nThe group was originally formed in 1997, and released their debut recording studio LP Get It How U Live! the same year on then-independent label Cash Money to local success, selling 400,000 copies primarily in the south. They followed up with 1999’s Guerrilla Warfare, the group’s most commercially successful album to date, selling 142,000 copies in its first week and debuting at #5 on the Billboard 200. The album was certified Platinum on November 1, 1999, by the RIAA.[2] The group disbanded in 2001 when Juvenile, Turk, and B.G. left Cash Money, citing financial mismanagement, leaving Lil Wayne as the only original member signed to the label. Despite this, one more Hot Boys album was released, 2003’s Let ‘Em Burn, composed of material originally recorded between 1998 and 2000”\n\nhttps://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Boys", "annotation_id": "30590935", "url": "https://genius.com/30590935", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tha Block Is Hot", "line": "Ain’t got on no suits, ‘cause we ain’t tryin’ to be presidents", "vault_lines": ["Ain't got on no suits, 'cause we ain't tryin' to be presidents"], "meaning": "This contradicts what he said a few months prior in Hot Boy$’s song “I Feel”:\n\nI feel like the Hot Boy$ dem should be the president\n\nThis might also be a shot at No Limit Records as they were known to be wearing suites especially on their album covers.\nhttps://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcSKkkFJIH2ewxFCSi6JpSRdNanbYp2-iiq4fQ&s\n\n\n\nFunnily enough, about 6 years later Lil Wayne would become president of Cash money Records. He would also go on to wear a suit in the music video of his song “Lollipop”\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2IH8tNQAzSs&t=33s\n\nAnd in the music video of his song “6 Foot 7 Foot”\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c7tOAGY59uQ\n\nHe would also make a song titled “President Carter”", "annotation_id": "28617049", "url": "https://genius.com/28617049", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Tha Block Is Hot", "line": "Straight off the block, gold nuts in my hand, trustin’ no man", "vault_lines": ["Straight off the block, gold nuts in my hand, trustin' no man"], "meaning": "Gold nuts = bullets", "annotation_id": "27539747", "url": "https://genius.com/27539747", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
