{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Suffix", "line": "Yeah, dear summer, summer breeze: Summer, please, know I live for you Throw on the wife-beater, let back the lid for you A few years I probably hear from you, but now I’m here for you I know niggas that shed a tear for you Behind bars, tryna get to you, and probably never see you", "vault_lines": ["Summer, please, know I live for you"], "meaning": "In Dear Summer Jay-Z referred to rap as summertime, and Wayne similarly at this point in his career had the same passion and desire for summer (rap) as his predecessor. He lives for it and knows in a few years he’ll be able to write the same letter that Jay once wrote to summer.\n\nThe success he is aiming for, however, is something that he knows many people in the hood or in prison will never see. There are probably many talented people who are limited by their situation (in poverty or behind bars) to ever fully experience summer.", "annotation_id": "5989245", "url": "https://genius.com/5989245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Suffix", "line": "The only Hot Boy eating off this plate: Young Weezy Baby, that’s what your bitch say Give her ass a location and a temp plate Show her how to work the interstate", "vault_lines": ["Show your ass how to take four and get eight"], "meaning": "Weezy Baby is one of Wayne’s many nicknames. As a matter of fact you can hear a female saying his name much like this at the beginning of Fireman and many other songs found on The W. Carter Collection mixtape.\n\nWayne is saying he has your girl saying his name and he can make her work the interstate selling his drugs.", "annotation_id": "5952566", "url": "https://genius.com/5952566", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Suffix", "line": "And tell your honor he a bitch to his face", "vault_lines": ["And tell your honor he a bitch to his face"], "meaning": "He’d tell the judge (referred to in court as Your Honor) right to his face that he’s a female dog. It’s obvious Wayne doesn’t have much respect for judges or anyone who puts people in jail.\n\nIn the song It’s Good from Tha Carter IV, Wayne’s protege Drake states “fuck the judge, and the jury, and the litigator” which could reflect Wayne’s attitude here.", "annotation_id": "5918124", "url": "https://genius.com/5918124", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Suffix", "line": "I’m just doing a buck down memory lane If I crash, just pick up my brain", "vault_lines": ["I'm just doing a buck down memory lane"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne uses imagery here in saying he’s doing a buck (100 mph) down Memory Lane to describe how he’s thinking back on all the memories, and if he happens to crash (or loses his mind thinking back) to just pick up his brain.\n\nHe also used this line in Keisha Cole’s remix of “I Love You” by Mary J. Blige.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iWPhdG1-W8w", "annotation_id": "5916128", "url": "https://genius.com/5916128", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Suffix", "line": "Hurricane wiped us out like a earthquake", "vault_lines": ["Hurricane wiped us out like a earthquake"], "meaning": "Hurricane Katrina, and the following flood caused the City of New Orleans damage equivalent to a high powered earthquake.", "annotation_id": "2992649", "url": "https://genius.com/2992649", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Suffix", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-suffix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Suffix", "genius_id": 233019, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "And I’m a sucker for pain, it ain’t nothin’ but pain You just fuckin’ complain, you ain’t tough as you claim", "vault_lines": ["I'm a slave to your games (I'm just a sucker for pain)"], "meaning": "Wayne shifts to rhyme twice within each set of bars, no longer carrying the meaning over from line to line, just having a theme of “do not mess with me.”\n\nWayne’s single “Drop The World” also had a similar theme, with pain dominating his life and his mental state. He “hurts but never cries,” whereas his enemies are weak and cannot handle the pain. Maybe they need pain pills.", "annotation_id": "10415418", "url": "https://genius.com/10415418", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "[Verse 3: Lil Wayne]", "vault_lines": ["Do it for the fam, dog, ten toes down, dog"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne goes full psychopath in his verse, spitting lines about how people should be terrified of him because of how crazy he is.\n\nHe also takes the song title literally, giving multiple examples of how he enjoys pain. Wayne has always said that he is, “not a human being,” and this verse complements that statement well.\n\n\n\nHe uses enjambment and double entendre throughout the first part of the verse (from “I’m devoted to destruction” to “So I don’t fear shit but tomorrow”) in order to display his lyrical mastery. He then switches to rapid fire delivery, rhyming twice as fast for the rest of his verse, highlighting his ability to switch between two very engaging flows.\n\nAlso, note the amount of alliteration he uses in his verse;  Weezy still got it.\n\nIn the video, Lil Wayne is sitting on a throne with the Joker’s Suicide Squad skull symbol on it, heavily implying that is representing the Joker here.", "annotation_id": "10210531", "url": "https://genius.com/10210531", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "[Verse 1: Ty Dolla $ign]", "vault_lines": ["Do it for the fam, dog, ten toes down, dog"], "meaning": "In Ty Dolla $ign’s verse, he raps from the perspective of a member of the Suicide Squad (possibly El Diablo, considering his Ty’s line about walking through fire), saying how he’ll die for his crew even while the world rejects them.\n\nHe could also be talking about himself, as the lines about going against the establishment mirror his musical style, seeing as Ty sings about as much as he raps (that is to say, quite a bit), and by going against the system, he can’t be classified as either a rapper or a singer.", "annotation_id": "10210510", "url": "https://genius.com/10210510", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "I’m a sucker for pain", "vault_lines": ["I'm a sucker for pain"], "meaning": "Ty begins his verse with a nod to the chorus and theme of the song, strengthening his ties to the actual Suicide Squad characters. This features as an introduction to his subject matter: He recognises that his fame and fierce loyalty has made him an outcast, but he wouldn’t change that.", "annotation_id": "9738429", "url": "https://genius.com/9738429", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "This is what we wanted from a young age", "vault_lines": ["This is what we wanted from a young age"], "meaning": "Wiz has wanted success, wealth, and popularity ever since he was a kid. He definitely achieved those goals, since his 2015 net worth was reportedly $55 million.\n\nNow that he has dedicated fans and an extremely successful career in the rap game, there is nothing he can’t do. If he can achieve his childhood dreams, he can do anything else he wants as long as he puts in the work and dedication.", "annotation_id": "9725222", "url": "https://genius.com/9725222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "No emotion, that’s what business is Lord have mercy on the witnesses", "vault_lines": ["No emotion, that's what business is"], "meaning": "The gangster business model classically showcased in films like The Godfather dictates that there is no place for empathy when it comes to business.\n\nWiz is so entrenched in doing bad he’s willing to remove the witnesses in order to get away with his crimes.  These sentiments go hand in hand with a characterization of the Joker, who appears in Suicide Squad. The Joker’s sociopathy strips him of his empathy and allows him to perform morally reprehensible actions to achieve his goals.", "annotation_id": "9724486", "url": "https://genius.com/9724486", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "I know I been bussin’, no discussion for my family", "vault_lines": ["I know I been bussin', no discussion for my family"], "meaning": "“Bustin'” links back with the dual personalities Logic is rapping in this track. He is referring both to bustin' bullets in Suicide Squad fashion, as well as “busting” his “ass,” as he said in “Lord Willin,” meaning he’s putting in work.\n\nHis family refers to both the fellow Squad members, and his RATTPACK crew, who are all on the same wavelength. They’re either bustin' their weapons with him, or bustin' their “ass” in the studio to help him get ahead.", "annotation_id": "9722110", "url": "https://genius.com/9722110", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "Used to doing bad, now we feel like we just now gettin’ it Ain’t got no other way, so we start it and finish it", "vault_lines": ["Used to doing bad, now we feel like we just now gettin' it"], "meaning": "Wiz turned down a deal with Warner Brothers because he didn’t agree with their views, and he stayed true to his pot smoking habits, not because it was cool but because he wanted to remain authentic to himself. These decisions ultimately led to increased record sales, even if they were seen as “bad” at the time.\n\nOn the topic of success, Wiz has said:\n\nThe most daring thing is to be yourself and to do exactly what you want to do at that point in time and not to be worried with what other people are doing or what’s popular.\n\nIt also mimics the movie, in that the Suicide Squad are super villains used to breaking the law, and they’re now in charge of “saving the world.”", "annotation_id": "9721937", "url": "https://genius.com/9721937", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "Do it for the fam, dog, ten toes down, dog Love and the loyalty, that’s what we stand for (Uh-huh)", "vault_lines": ["Do it for the fam, dog, ten toes down, dog"], "meaning": "Ty Dollar $ign’s crew plays a key role in motivating him to reach new levels of success. In turn, he’ll repay his gang’s trustworthiness and support by bringing them into the lavish lifestyle he currently enjoys. ‘Ten toes down’ is a phrase representing unquestionable commitment to a cause, in this case, it’s Ty’s brothers in the game.\n\nHe is also referencing his song, “Stand For.”", "annotation_id": "9721871", "url": "https://genius.com/9721871", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "Won’t hesitate to go straight to your head like a concussion", "vault_lines": ["Won't hesitate to go straight to your head like a concussion"], "meaning": "Logic claims his music is direct and mind-blowing, leaving you with a concussion. His bars go straight to your head, because they’re both emotionally charged, and intelligent. The second half of this lyric is a recycled line from “Growing Pains III,” a track off of Logic’s debut album Under Pressure.\n\nThis could also be a reference to Suicide Squad, which involves a lot of gunfire, and a bullet to the head isn’t going to tickle. Logic may be hinting at a portrayal of the character Deadshot here. Will Smith plays Deadshot, and he was  the lead in a film called Concussion in 2015.", "annotation_id": "9721684", "url": "https://genius.com/9721684", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "[Verse 4: Wiz Khalifa]", "vault_lines": ["Do it for the fam, dog, ten toes down, dog"], "meaning": "Wiz Khalifa is no stranger to film soundtracks. “See You Again” for Furious 7 and “Young, Wild, and Free” for his own film, Mac and Devin Go to High School,  are just two others of many that Wiz has contributed to the soundtrack on.", "annotation_id": "9721611", "url": "https://genius.com/9721611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "Alienated by society All this pressure give me anxiety (Anxiety) Walk slow through the fire Like, who gon’ try us? Feelin’ the world go against us (’Gainst us) So we put the world on our shoulders", "vault_lines": ["All this pressure give me anxiety (Anxiety)"], "meaning": "If it feels like the whole world is against you, rebelling can only go so far. Instead, helping those who oppose you might serve to change their perspective.\n\n\n\nIn Suicide Squad, the government needs help with a secret mission, but instead of recruiting heroes, they recruit a group of villains. The mission will likely end in death for them, but since they are villains they’re already hated by society and are therefore expendable. Their only choice is to go along with the mission and save the world.", "annotation_id": "9721238", "url": "https://genius.com/9721238", "state": "accepted", "votes": 86, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "Pressure from the man got us all in rebellion (Yeah) We gon’ go to war, yeah, without failure", "vault_lines": ["Pressure from the man got us all in rebellion (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Ty may be speaking both about himself, and the perennial “the man” character, a name attributed to anyone oppressing or attempting to control the masses, or minorities.\n\nThe members of Suicide Squad are all super villains, incarcerated but employed by the Government to perform unpleasant operations for shorter sentences. Ty may be referring to the tyranny that they are faced with: Either rot in jail, or perform like a puppet.\n\nHe’s also referred to himself as “The Man” repeatedly, and it could be pressure he’s placed on himself that has caused him to crack, and act in a reckless manner.", "annotation_id": "9721185", "url": "https://genius.com/9721185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "No hesitation, through my scope I see my enemy Like what’s up? Hold up, we finna re-load up Yes, I re-load up, I know what up, I know what up", "vault_lines": ["No hesitation, through my scope I see my enemy"], "meaning": "Logic’s words are his weapons. When he’s confronted, he loads up his weapon and shoots to kill by spitting ill lyrics. After all, he wants to be the greatest.\n\nThese lyrics fit in well with a characterization of Deadshot, a character from Suicide Squad portrayed by Will Smith.", "annotation_id": "9721046", "url": "https://genius.com/9721046", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "No pain, no gain Never stand down, made our own way Never going slow, we pick up the pace", "vault_lines": ["No pain, no gain"], "meaning": "The common expression “no pain, no gain,” suggests that, in order to grow and succeed, we must experience hardship. The back stories of each character in the movie are full of hardship, and their approach to the mission they receive is full throttle,  as the trailer shows.\n\nThis sentiment relates to the Joker’s psyche, in that he will do whatever is necessary to achieve his goals, no matter who it hurts.", "annotation_id": "9720723", "url": "https://genius.com/9720723", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "Hold up, take a step back when we roll up, ’cause I know what We been loyal, we been fam, we the ones you trust in", "vault_lines": ["Hold up, take a step back when we roll up, 'cause I know what"], "meaning": "Logic raps about the RATTPACK’s loyalty and familial connection. In an interview with Hard Knock TV, he spoke on the importance of making these close connections:\n\n[The RATTPACK] means family, in many ways the family I never had growing up, so that’s very special to me. Also, it means family around the world. It means that I can go anywhere and be accepted and know that, if I ever needed anything, I could go to one of these RATTPACK members and they would look out for me just like I do for them through my music.", "annotation_id": "9720584", "url": "https://genius.com/9720584", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "I torture you Take my hand through the flames I torture you I’m a slave to your games (I’m just a sucker for pain) I wanna chain you up, I wanna tie you down Ooh, I’m just a sucker for pain", "vault_lines": ["Take my hand through the flames"], "meaning": "These lines are reminiscent of the relationship between the Joker and Harley Quinn. The Joker, in Suicide Squad, ties down Harley, torturing her and tattooing her. Harley seems to love him, however, as she is a “Sucker For Pain.”\n\n\n\nAs the chorus for the track, it binds the entire song in a lust for agony, in the quest to feel something, anything, whether it be good or bad.\n\nAnother thing to note is the reference in “a slave to your games”. Looking back at Suicide Squad, the members are all pawns to Amanda Waller, who can execute them by the single click of a button.", "annotation_id": "9720283", "url": "https://genius.com/9720283", "state": "accepted", "votes": 309, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "Just stay up in your lane, just don’t fuck with Lil Wayne", "vault_lines": ["Just stay up in your lane, just don't fuck with Lil Wayne"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne doesn’t want you intruding in his personal affairs, or his “lane,” which could represent the path he takes in hip hop. Wayne hasn’t defined a role for himself since his “Best Rapper Alive” phase, so it’s hard to say who he’s speaking to.\n\nWayne is a fan of the “lane” metaphor, and has used it to mean a car lane (“Jazz Cartier”), a basketball lane (“Outstanding”), and even as a bowling lane (“Hawaii 5.0”).\n\nThe results of trying to take over someone else’s lane usually lead to a collision. Combined with the injunction to not fuck with him implies that in any collision, you will be the one who gets totaled. Just don’t. Fuck. With. Lil. Wayne.", "annotation_id": "9720185", "url": "https://genius.com/9720185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "And only Lord knows when I’m coming to the crossroads So I don’t fear shit but tomorrow", "vault_lines": ["And only Lord knows when I'm coming to the crossroads"], "meaning": "“The lost soul” idea hearkens back to the parable of the lost sheep in the gospels like Luke 15:4, which ties neatly into claiming that “only [the] Lord knows…”\n\nWhat man of you, having an hundred sheep, if he lose one of them, doth not leave the ninety and nine in the wilderness, and go after that which is lost, until he find it?\n\nThe crossroads in life is when a person must make a major decision, sometimes between life and death. According to myth, Robert Johnson met the devil at crossroads and was granted complete mastery of the guitar.\n\nWayne often references “the crossroads,” mentioning it explicitly during “After Disaster,” and “Ready For The World”:\n\n\nTo those restin in peace, those dead and deceased, soon again we meet. Or til im high and drunk and my cars swerves off road, haa. I’ll meet you at the crossroads.\n\nAnd theres no window to escape your mental drought\nYou think about the crossroads more than the man that’s conducting the train", "annotation_id": "9719999", "url": "https://genius.com/9719999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "I got the squad tatted on me from my neck to my ankles (My ankles)", "vault_lines": ["I got the squad tatted on me from my neck to my ankles (My ankles)"], "meaning": "The “new” Joker in the Suicide Squad reboot has a multitude of tattoos, and although there’s been some concern aired by fans, director David Ayer explained that the tattoos are essential in The Joker’s backstory:\n\nThe tattoos tell a very specific story. And eventually people will decipher them and understand what’s going on, but obviously they’re contentious, any time you do something new it’s contentious. There’s very specific stories and easter eggs in those tattoos.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, the cast and crew of Suicide Squad tattooed each other, with the word “Skwad”. Some on their biceps and ankles and foot.\n\nTy has many tattoos, each with their own distinct meaning, but at least some having to do with his “squad,” including his brother TC, who Ty claims has been wrongfully imprisoned for murder. During an interview with Hot 97 he said:\n\nFree TC is tatted on my fingers, right there.", "annotation_id": "9719758", "url": "https://genius.com/9719758", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "Naked in the North Pole That’s why my heart cold, full of sorrow, the lost soul", "vault_lines": ["naked in the North Pole"], "meaning": "The juxtaposition of “charcoal” and “North Pole,” as well as the internal rhyme are a subtle allusion to him being on Santa’s “naughty list,” though the reason for this isn’t made clear.\n\nIf you are choking on a chunk of charcoal instead of using it to start a fire to keep warm, then you must really like pain.\n\nBeing naked in the North Pole, where temperatures can reach -40 degrees Fahrenheit in the winter, is pretty much suicidal.\n\nWayne states that these conditions have caused his cold heart and makes him filled with gloom and despair. As a result, he feels like he doesn’t have a place in the world.", "annotation_id": "9719402", "url": "https://genius.com/9719402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "I get stoked, might choke Like I chewed a chunk of charcoal,", "vault_lines": ["I get stoked, might choke"], "meaning": "“If I get stung I get stoked” is basically Wayne saying if he gets ‘stung’ (very drunk off large amounts of alcohol), he gets ‘stoked’, by which he likely means high on marijuana. He then implies that he might be going into anaphylaxis because of the jellyfish sting.\n\nThis is also more tightly packed alliteration:\n “might choke like I chewed a chunk of charcoal.”\n\nCharcoal is black residue mixed with ashes that is often created by burning wood. It’s definitely not something you would want in your mouth in large amounts, the taste and texture alone would be unpalatable. However, people do “chew charcoal” to help with overdoses of various kinds, though the efficacy of doing so is in question.\n\nCharcoal is important when building (stoking) a fire, as it’s these pieces that will hold heat and cook food or provide long periods of warmth.", "annotation_id": "9719158", "url": "https://genius.com/9719158", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "See, I’m a fool for pain, I’m a dummy Might cut my head off right after I slit my throat", "vault_lines": ["See, I'm a fool for pain, I'm a dummy"], "meaning": "This line continues the  idea of the devil trying to rush Wayne to death; he’s a fool, because he’s still rushing in to the pain, even though it might kill him.\n\n‘Fool’ and ‘dummy’ are being used as synonyms for Wayne’s take on being a “sucker for pain.”\n\nWhile ‘fool’ and ‘dummy’ are direct synonyms, ‘dummy’ is another word for ‘pacifier’, a baby’s toy that it can suck on in times of distress. So he’s saying he is a “sucker for pain.”\n\nSlitting his throat will already kill him, so cutting his head off is unnecessary pain that he’s a “fool” for.", "annotation_id": "9718523", "url": "https://genius.com/9718523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "I’m dying slow, but the devil tryna rush me", "vault_lines": ["I'm dying slow, but the devil tryna rush me"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne’s had health issues recently, including two back-to-back seizures that some claim occurred due to Wayne’s consumption of cough syrup (Lean). The devil is tempting him to take bigger and bigger doses since it is insanely addictive.\n\nWayne is only 34, but these seizures have landed him in hospital more than once.\n\n“Dying slow” may refer to the fact that lean causes extreme drowsiness and loss of motor skills, causing the body to slow down.\n\nI’m like a turtle, when I sip the purple", "annotation_id": "9718358", "url": "https://genius.com/9718358", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "I’ma jump from my plane or stand in front of a train ’Cause I’m a sucker for pain", "vault_lines": ["I'ma jump from my plane or stand in front of a train"], "meaning": "Wayne finishes up this verse with a reference to the chorus, emphasising his masochistic tendencies by placing himself in two more situations that are likely to cause death.\n\nNotice that he is saying his plane and not a plane. His vast amounts of money are just one more reason you “don’t fuck with Lil Wayne.”", "annotation_id": "9718168", "url": "https://genius.com/9718168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "I’m devoted to destruction A full dosage of detrimental dysfunction", "vault_lines": ["I'm devoted to destruction"], "meaning": "Wayne’s verse parallels the theme of the track as an anthem for the anti-heroes in Suicide Squad.\n\n“Self Destruction” is a particular speciality of Wayne’s, and anyone who has followed his career know that drugs, criminal activity, and a dangerous work ethic have combined to stall his career at times. The use of alliteration shows that he’s still a force to be reckoned with on the mic:\n\nUh, devoted to destruction\nA full dosage of detrimental dysfunction\nI’m dying slow but the devil\n\n\n\nI wish I could give you this feelin', I feel like buying\nAnd if my dealer don’t have no more, then (I feel like dying)", "annotation_id": "9717847", "url": "https://genius.com/9717847", "state": "accepted", "votes": 111, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "Tongue kiss a shark, got jealous bitches up in the boat Eatin’ peanut butter and jelly fishes on toast And if I get stung,", "vault_lines": ["Tongue kiss a shark, got jealous bitches up in the boat"], "meaning": "Slitting your throat, is an even worse idea if you’re about to get in the water with a shark. You better hope they aren’t hungry, especially in close enough proximity to tongue kiss it. Wayne also ties these lines together by “tongue” instead of the much more common “French” as a modifier for kissing, relating back to “throat.”\n\nSince Wayne at least at one time owned sharks, he’s very likely aware that, to the extent that sharks have ‘tongues’, they are inside their mouths.\n\nKissing sharks has made his girlfriends jealous, so he treats them to a dish of peanut butter and jelly fish. Unfortunately, jelly fish sting, and although they are eaten in China, fitting with the theme of this track it’s only right that Wayne chugs them straight out of the ocean, stinging his esophagus.", "annotation_id": "9717454", "url": "https://genius.com/9717454", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sucker for Pain", "line": "I been at it with my homies, it don’t matter, you don’t know me I been rollin’ with my team, we the illest on the scene I been riding ’round the city with my squad I been riding ’round the city with my squad We just posted, getting crazy, livin’ like this is so amazing", "vault_lines": ["I been at it with my homies, it don't matter, you don't know me"], "meaning": "Logic is signed to Visionary Music Group and Def Jam.\n\nHe is also a member and founder of the RattPack, a loose collective he formed full of people that stuck by his side as he became famous. Members currently include 6ix and Big Lenbo. Logic has also included Jon Bellion, who is signed to Visionary, and his manager, Chris Zarou, in the group. He likens this squad to the Suicide Squad.", "annotation_id": "9715579", "url": "https://genius.com/9715579", "state": "accepted", "votes": 88, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
