{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Life", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-street-life-lyrics", "genius_title": "Street Life", "genius_id": 583493, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I can hijack a Brinks", "vault_lines": ["I can hijack a Brinks"], "meaning": "Brinks is a security company that transports large cash deposits and transfers for companies.\n\n\n\nClearly, it would be no easy feat to hijack a Brinks security truck. So, Lil Wayne uses the boasting standard of rap to deliver this line about doing close to the impossible.", "annotation_id": "23717944", "url": "https://genius.com/23717944", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Stay in ya lane, I remind these lil’ boys This is victory lane, now do I need a horn?", "vault_lines": ["Stay in ya lane, I remind these lil' boys"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne tries to tell others (the “lil' boys”) to stay in their lane, considering Wayne’s is the victory lane. He says he reminds these boys, then asks “do I need a horn,” as this is how you remind someone to stay in their line on the road. He asks the question as a warning, reaffirming the fact that he is unique and special to the point that these other boys cannot follow in his footsteps.", "annotation_id": "23717921", "url": "https://genius.com/23717921", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "No love", "vault_lines": ["Phone ringin' off the hook, bitch, that's the plug"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne was featured on Eminem’s “Recovery” album on a song titled “No Love,” where Lil Wayne delivers hard-hitting, grim lines about his state of being with gangsta imagery and themes.", "annotation_id": "23717903", "url": "https://genius.com/23717903", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Got more sand than Sudan, Lord", "vault_lines": ["Got more sand than Sudan, Lord"], "meaning": "‘To have sand’ is slang for having an over-the-top amount of perseverance or being extremely strong-willed. The phrase has been used in pop culture recently in Quentin Tarantino’s “Django Unchained,” a story about a former slave. In one of the flashback scenes, Jamie Foxx’s character Django is getting punished by a slave master for running away.\n\n\n\nBruce Dern’s slave master character said, “You got sand Django. Boys got sand! I got no use for a n*gger with sand.”\n\nLil Wayne raps about how he has this type of perseverance, perhaps alluding to Django Unchained with the imagery of street chains and getting freedom from those chains as Django did.", "annotation_id": "23717802", "url": "https://genius.com/23717802", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Boy, I’m drownin’ in the syrup like a short stack", "vault_lines": ["Boy, I'm drownin' in the syrup like a short stack"], "meaning": "Here Wayne most likely is referring to a short stack of pancakes and how they are usually drowned in syrup. Wayne is using this clever wordplay to talk about his lean consumption.", "annotation_id": "20447665", "url": "https://genius.com/20447665", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Yeah", "vault_lines": ["Phone ringin' off the hook, bitch, that's the plug"], "meaning": "Wayne’s “Yeah” here sounds more like an “ehh” as if Wayne was unimpressed with his last line on this song. However, he doesn’t care to change the line because he understands that he demolished the rest of the song. Wayne is notorious for being hard on himself lyrically; in an interview with Genius, he talked about how he almost changed arguably the best line on 2 Chainz' “Rolls Royce Weather Everyday,” because he thought it was “too plain.”\n\nI just got a text message from wifey, nigga\nI scroll down, the next message from Nike, nigga", "annotation_id": "10010965", "url": "https://genius.com/10010965", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I’m spitting these bars, hope my bar tab is cheap", "vault_lines": ["I'm spitting these bars, hope my bar tab is cheap"], "meaning": "To spit bars means to rap or freestlye, usually in a continuous and fast-paced method (something Wayne definitely did on this track). In addition, a bar tab is essentially your open check at a bar when you order your drinks; You pay for it all when you’re done drinking and “close” the check.\n\nWith all that said, Wayne is saying he’s spitting so many bars that he’s worried his bar tab is getting expensive.", "annotation_id": "10010800", "url": "https://genius.com/10010800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "You know that haters come in all sizes, all shapes, ’kay That’s why I had to get a little more trunk space", "vault_lines": ["You know that haters come in all sizes, all shapes, 'kay"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne indicates that whatever you do, there will always be haters trying to bring you down. Because of the various shapes and sizes that haters are coming in, just to be safe, he got a little more trunk space, so in case a body needs to be placed in the trunk, it would fit.\n\nThis could also refer to the murder scene and subsequent trunk-stuffing of mobster Billy Batts in the movie Goodfellas.", "annotation_id": "7337775", "url": "https://genius.com/7337775", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Got insurance on the trap house, Allstate", "vault_lines": ["Got insurance on the trap house, Allstate"], "meaning": "Wayne is letting us know that the trap house is insured. His drugs are covered by an insurance policy, then adds a pun by referring to Allstate, which is one of the largest personal lines insurers.", "annotation_id": "7337767", "url": "https://genius.com/7337767", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "We trap out the ’telly when we outta town", "vault_lines": ["We trap out the 'telly when we outta town"], "meaning": "Even when he isn’t in town, Lil Wayne’s still making drug deals and running shit through the phone. This is similar to the hook in “9 Piece (Remix)” implies:\n\nI’m selling dope, straight off the iPhone\n\nWayne also could be talking about making drug deals out of his hotel room when he’s out of town.", "annotation_id": "7337754", "url": "https://genius.com/7337754", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "So my sweet soda pink", "vault_lines": [", so my sweet soda pink"], "meaning": "Although Lean is usually known for being purple, it actually can be different colors depending on what specific syrup you use, as well as the types of mixers you use.  So sometimes it comes out more pink than purple.\n\nTwo white cups and I got that drink\nCould be purple, it could be pink", "annotation_id": "7336022", "url": "https://genius.com/7336022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I gotta get the raw back, I need a format", "vault_lines": ["I gotta get the raw back, I need a format"], "meaning": "When you format an electronic device, you completely erase it and start back over at the original, “raw” state.\n\nNow that Wayne is striking out on his own, he realizes he has to get back to that zone where it was hard to argue against Weezy’s claim of being The Best Rapper Alive.\n\nWeezy believes he can start with a fresh slate, and get his original fire and passion (raw emotion/talent) back; which can be taken as a hint that Wayne hasn’t totally committed to retirement yet – if he feels like he’s “gotta get the raw back” then he must intend to stay in the game a little longer.  It’s always best to go out on top.", "annotation_id": "7335995", "url": "https://genius.com/7335995", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I ain’t playin’ Guns in my hands, I ain’t prayin’", "vault_lines": ["Hold up, I ain't playin'"], "meaning": "Throughout Wayne’s work there is a constant tension between the traditional ideas of good and evil / religion vs. sin / etc.\n\nWayne seems to have some sort of belief in a  higher power and/or afterlife; however he is often equally irreverent and dismissive of religious ideas.\n\nIn this image, he places his trust in his own ability to protect himself, rather than placing it in God.  Wayne can’t pray (in the traditional Protestant sense) without being able to fold his hands – and he can’t fold his hands, because both of them are holding onto weaponry, and Wayne has no intentions of letting his grip loose on the tools he trusts to protect him in this life.", "annotation_id": "7335967", "url": "https://genius.com/7335967", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I’m too hard for this beat I’m the heart and the beat", "vault_lines": ["I'm too hard for this beat"], "meaning": "Wayne claims he is too hard for the beat made by StreetRunner and Tarik Azzouz. The hard and meldolistic production is no match for Wayne’s flow. Wayne follows up with a great play on words that claims, he is the heart and the beat, which simply means he is the cause and the effect. Heartbeats are a complementary motion, the heart pumps blood into and out the system and makes a beat, Wayne says he is both.", "annotation_id": "7328016", "url": "https://genius.com/7328016", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Got the trap house pumpin’ like a heart rate Got the trap house pumpin’ like a 808 Boom, like a 808", "vault_lines": ["Got the trap house pumpin' like a heart rate"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne describes the resemblance of heart rate, and trap house. Heart rate is the speed of the heartbeat measured by the number of poundings of the heart, while his crack house depends on the number of addicts. Similarly to how heart is pumping blood, Lil Wayne got a crack house that is pumpin', or slanging drugs.\n\n808 refers to the Roland TR-808 Sequencer and, to the sound of bass from stereos. Since pumpin' could refer to loud treble/bass, he depicts it to his trap house. 808 is also the penal code for disturbing the peace.\n\nAlso a reference to Blaque’s gold-selling single, “808”.", "annotation_id": "7327864", "url": "https://genius.com/7327864", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Had a date with the Devil Then I changed schedules", "vault_lines": ["Had a date with the Devil"], "meaning": "Tunechi saw that he was going down the wrong path, so he switched up his life to better himself.\n\nI’d say he’s doing okay for himself…", "annotation_id": "7274195", "url": "https://genius.com/7274195", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I could fly around that bitch, need a tarmac", "vault_lines": ["I could fly around that bitch, need a tarmac"], "meaning": "A tarmac is a material used for surfacing roads or other outdoor areas, consisting of crushed rock mixed with tar. This is what airport runways are consisted of.\n\nAs long as there is helipads, Lil Wayne could travel anywhere with his private jet. However, if he find no tarmacs, or helipads to land his private jet on, he’s going elsewhere.\n\nAlso, Wayne could be saying that he is so fly, he needs a tarmac to land on. “Fly” means cool and stylish in this interpretation.", "annotation_id": "7274187", "url": "https://genius.com/7274187", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Ocean view bedroom, baby See through showers and I just put some fish in my tub", "vault_lines": ["Ocean view bedroom, baby"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is referring to his luxury mansion, and how big is it. He earlier mentioned in the song “No Ceilings (Pop That)” that his house in Miami is on the seaside. He also mentioned tons of times that he has sharks in his house, for its immense size.\n\nI’ll be waiting in my mansion, seaside nigga", "annotation_id": "7274030", "url": "https://genius.com/7274030", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Straight from the East side Blood gang, we heavy", "vault_lines": ["Straight from the East side"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne was born and and raised in New Orleans, Eagle Street, uptown, which is the East Side. He’s also known for referring to his affiliation with blood gang, and blood gang references in a lot of his songs.", "annotation_id": "7274024", "url": "https://genius.com/7274024", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I’m newborn and deceased", "vault_lines": ["I'm newborn and deceased"], "meaning": "Although he’s a new born, started working independently, he has already killed the old Weezy.\n\nAfter ranting on twitter about his label, then saying “Fuck Cash Money”, then making a song talking about how the old him Is Dead, I think you can safely say that this is a new Wayne.\n\nRest In Peace to the Cash Money Weezy\nGone but not forgotten, no\n\n\n\nWho would’ve ever thought that would happen?", "annotation_id": "7271692", "url": "https://genius.com/7271692", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I’m a trained killer Like a paid killer Better yet, Saddam Hussein nephew", "vault_lines": ["I'm a trained killer"], "meaning": "Weezy is saying he is trained to kill much like someone who is paid to kill; you wouldn’t pay someone to kill if they’re not trained.\n\nSaddam Hussein, former Iraq President, was very active with his military and their attacks. He was considered as a killer, for the murder of 148 Iraqi Shi'ites.\n\nSince one of Hussein’s nicknames was “Great Uncle”, Lil Wayne considers himself as his nephew, who would probably be a killer following his uncle.", "annotation_id": "7270758", "url": "https://genius.com/7270758", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I just got another speeding ticket on the Bugatti While it was parked", "vault_lines": ["I just got another speeding ticket on the Bugatti"], "meaning": "Weezy loves his Bugatti and here he uses a hyperbole to describe how fast it goes. He says it’s so fast that he is speeding even when the car is parked and not moving.\n\nAs a matter of fact, Bugatti Veyron has a top speed of 408.84 km/h, and is recognized by Guinness World Records as the fastest street-legal production car in the world.", "annotation_id": "7270741", "url": "https://genius.com/7270741", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Train smoker, smoke plain, never", "vault_lines": ["Train smoker, smoke plain, never"], "meaning": "The A-Train strain is in fact a cross between two of the most widely used strains in the medical cannabis community. Professionals consider A-Train to be an extremely effective strain that delivers a relaxing and balanced high.\n\nTunechi never smokes mids or any generic strain of weed. He’s smoking the best out there.", "annotation_id": "7270002", "url": "https://genius.com/7270002", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Hold up, I ain’t playin’ Niggas say they rich, I say “And?”", "vault_lines": ["Hold up, I ain't playin'"], "meaning": "Wayne isn’t joking when he usually brags about having a lot of money in his music, his net worth is $140 million and he is #9 on the list of the richest 10 rappers in the world (as of 2015).\n\nWhen people say that they are rich Wayne doesn’t care, because the money they have is nothing compared to his.", "annotation_id": "7269028", "url": "https://genius.com/7269028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Fuck nigga, dare me I turn into Freddy My fingers machetes", "vault_lines": ["Fuck nigga, dare me"], "meaning": "Freddy Krueger is the main antagonist of the A Nightmare on Elm Street film series. Freddy was a serial killer, and upon his death, he started murdering people in their dreams, only to keep resurrecting himself.\n\nAlso could be a reference to Jason Vorhees, which is also referred to in the second verse,  who used a machete to kill his victims in the Friday The 13th movies.\n\nSo Wayne is Freddy with Jason weapons for fingers, and he is turning to a serial killer, if you have the courage to challenge him.", "annotation_id": "7269019", "url": "https://genius.com/7269019", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Like my bitch mixed, like Long Island Tea She don’t hide her figure, she don’t hide her feet", "vault_lines": ["Like my bitch mixed, like Long Island Tea"], "meaning": "A Long Island Tea is a type of alcoholic mixed drink typically made with, among other ingredients, tequila, vodka, light rum, triple sec, and gin.\n\nLil Wayne likes his girl to be from various races – has more than one racial background – similarly to a mixed alcoholic drink. He also admire this girl who’s not shy to show off her body.", "annotation_id": "7268629", "url": "https://genius.com/7268629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "The Chong and the Cheech My blunt long as a speech", "vault_lines": ["The Chong and the Cheech"], "meaning": "Reference to Cheech and Chong, one of the greatest stoner comedians, and most famous stoner duo that ever lived.\n\nLil Wayne depicts his love to marijuana, similarly to Cheech and Chong’s, who were most notably for their love of cannabis.\n\nThis could also refer to “How Long, Not Long”, a popular name given to the public speech delivered by Martin Luther King, Jr.", "annotation_id": "7268588", "url": "https://genius.com/7268588", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I dropped out of class so I’m zero degrees", "vault_lines": ["I dropped out of class so I'm zero degrees"], "meaning": "Play on words here by comparing zero degrees to academic titles. Contrary to how somebody dropping out of class gets zero degrees, Lil Wayne has zero degrees, or has a cold heart, and a living care-free attitude.\n\nAs a matter of fact, Lil Wayne attended the University of Houston for part of 2005 until schedule conflicts forced him to drop out.", "annotation_id": "7268513", "url": "https://genius.com/7268513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I’m the head nigga, like Prodigy", "vault_lines": ["I'm the head nigga, like Prodigy"], "meaning": "Reference to H.N.I.C., which was the debut solo album by Prodigy.\n\nSince “H.N.I.C” is an acronym for “Head Nigga In Charge”, Lil Wayne is describing himself as his boss’s boss.", "annotation_id": "7268487", "url": "https://genius.com/7268487", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "My bitch from Atlanta got eyes like a hawk She see why I’m a boss", "vault_lines": ["My bitch from Atlanta got eyes like a hawk"], "meaning": "He is making a clever juxtaposition to the Atlanta Hawks, a professional basketball team. “Got eyes like a hawk” is a famous idiom, which is referring to how accurate a person can judge. If someone has eyes like a hawk, they notice everything, and misses nothing.\n\nLil Wayne’s girl can notice why he’s a boss. She watched him carefully, and noticed how hard he has worked.\n\nNotice also the wordplay on “why I’m” and “YM”, since he is the boss of Young Money.", "annotation_id": "7268452", "url": "https://genius.com/7268452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "And bitch, I’ma shine, like Connery", "vault_lines": ["And bitch, I'ma shine, like Connery"], "meaning": "Reference to Sean Connery, a Scottish actor known for playing “007” in the first James Bond spy movies.\n\nThe homophone used here is “shine”, and “Sean”. Lil Wayne describes how he’s shining, and got the public attention, as if a spotlight is shining on him and the world’s eyes are on him. Tunechi is grabbing the public eye, similarly to how the multiple award winner did.", "annotation_id": "7268416", "url": "https://genius.com/7268416", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Same old song, I ain’t dance", "vault_lines": ["Same old song, I ain't dance"], "meaning": "“Same old song and dance” is another way of saying same as always, or nothing out of the ordinary.\n\nSince he doesn’t dance to the old songs, Lil Wayne refers to how he is not the same as the old Weezy. He is referring to the latest problems he has been involved in with Cash Money label. These problems have changed him, and have changed his story.", "annotation_id": "7268399", "url": "https://genius.com/7268399", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Now I don’t wan’ talk, bitch, I don’t wan’ talk Lean in my punch,", "vault_lines": ["Now I don't wan' talk, bitch, I don't wan' talk"], "meaning": "Punch is the term for a wide assortment of drinks, both non-alcoholic and alcoholic, generally containing fruit or fruit juice. Lil Wayne is known for his usage of\ncough syrup, which is casually mixed with a tasteful drink to mask off its flavor.\n\nLil Wayne has a similar line in “CivilTV Next Up Cypher”, where he freestyled with other Young Money members.\n\nAll this lean in my punch got me lickin' a mothafuckin' fist", "annotation_id": "7268393", "url": "https://genius.com/7268393", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I just landed in Cuba, need a straw hat", "vault_lines": ["I just landed in Cuba, need a straw hat"], "meaning": "Cuban men usually wear straw hats, for being more representative of Cuba than other hats. The wearing of the straw hat is a statement to a judgmental world, for giving a careless look.\n\nLil Wayne might travel all around the world to make money or for vacations, and not care about critics' views.", "annotation_id": "7268388", "url": "https://genius.com/7268388", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Heater on my waist, hotter than a frying pan", "vault_lines": ["Heater on my waist, hotter than a frying pan"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne plays on words “heater” and “frying pan”. Since heater is a slang for a gun, and frying pan is a flat-bottomed pan used for frying foods, Lil Wayne has a hotter heater than a pan, which is used primarily when on fire.\n\nTunechi has a similar line on “Amazing Amy”, where he depicts his girl as hot as frying pans.\n\nI love her she came with a frying pan", "annotation_id": "7268384", "url": "https://genius.com/7268384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Trap house jumpin’ like Monta Ellis", "vault_lines": ["Trap house jumpin' like Monta Ellis"], "meaning": "Monta Ellis is an NBA star known for jumping highly to do dunks, or shots. Lil Wayne plays on the word “jumping”, and similarly to how Monta Ellis jumps to score, the crack house is jumping, or popping with addicts to score.\n\nLil Wayne has a similar line of this in multiple tracks, recently in “White Girl”, which is the 14th track of the current album.\n\nTrap house jump out the gym, trap house jump like LeBron", "annotation_id": "7268377", "url": "https://genius.com/7268377", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "Ooh, 187, 211 Hockey mask on, Wayne Gretzky", "vault_lines": ["Hockey mask on, Wayne Gretzky"], "meaning": "211 is a code used by police to identify a robbery, and 187 is a code used to identify murder.\n\nLil Wayne is going to rob, and murder you with his mask on. Similarly to Wayne Gretzky, a Canadian former professional ice hockey player, Lil Wayne would put on a hockey mask, so nobody can figure out his physical features during his criminal activities. Also, notice the name similarity.\n\nTunechi also refers to Jason Voorhees, a fictional character from the Friday the 13th series, who’s widely recognized for murdering with a hockey mask on.", "annotation_id": "7268368", "url": "https://genius.com/7268368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Street Chains", "line": "I’m excited to start Get indicted tomorrow I be out by the morn Before I even yawn", "vault_lines": ["I'm excited to start"], "meaning": "Wayne is known as one of the hardest working artists of all time, he gets up in the morning and starts working, he has crazy work ethic. As recently stated by one of his producers, Infamous:\n\nHis work ethic is crazier than anybody. He’s literally in the studio at least six days a week. And those six days, it’s a minimum of eight to 10 hours a day.\n\nI think it’s obvious too since he has 1,600+ songs.", "annotation_id": "7267123", "url": "https://genius.com/7267123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
