{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Yeah, bitch, I’m outside with it (Mm, uh), tell them niggas I did it", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, bitch, I'm outside with it (Mm, uh), tell them niggas I did it"], "meaning": "Tyler stated that he fucks people outside of the pussy and leaves marks. Telling the woman to tell the other men she fucks he left the marks.\n\nSomething like that", "annotation_id": "36372412", "url": "https://genius.com/36372412", "state": "pending", "votes": -4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Fuck that aiming ’bout? Hittin’ everything that’s in my way, fuck what they talkin’ ’bout", "vault_lines": ["Fuck that aiming 'bout?"], "meaning": "The lyric “Fuck that aiming bout'?” means “What that mean”. So in the line after that, he clarifies himself. Now, in the second line that explains what he meant previously means that he doesn’t care what’s in his path. He will still do what he needs to do. In the song, RISE! he talks about his haters how they do too much and talk on him and hate on him. “fuck what they talkin' ‘bout” means he doesn’t care what the haters have to say for them.", "annotation_id": "34891302", "url": "https://genius.com/34891302", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Ahead of ya, I’m better, baby, check the vehicle LaFerrari (Mhm), niggas sour (Mhm)", "vault_lines": ["Ahead of ya, I'm better, baby, check the vehicle"], "meaning": "Tyler is saying that he has the privilege to buy expensive cars, like the LaFerrari, while people are “sour” that they cannot buy one for themselves.", "annotation_id": "34427597", "url": "https://genius.com/34427597", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Louis V, say my name, we ain’t worth the same amount, boy", "vault_lines": ["Louis V, say my name, we ain't worth the same amount, boy"], "meaning": "The lyric show Tyler’s wealth by implying that even Louis Vuitton mentions him. Louis Vuitton is considered one of the most well-respected and luxurious fashion brands in the world and a major status symbol due to their expensive items, so Tyler is implying that he is so successful that even such a brand that many desire mentions his name. That  may be referring to the numerous collaborations between Louis Vuitton and Tyler the Creator, such as the Louis Vuitton x Tyler the Creator Trainers, and the monogram tribute line of items. And he may be referencing Louis V of France, the king of France from 979 to 987 AD.", "annotation_id": "34420538", "url": "https://genius.com/34420538", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "All the bi bitches know the fuck is goin’ down", "vault_lines": ["All the bi bitches know the fuck is goin' down"], "meaning": "Tyler switched this to “gay girls” in his November 2024 performance at Camp Flog Gnaw. Note that this shift builds upon the overall alliteration of the line, with “gay girls” now connecting with “goin'.”\n\nhttps://youtu.be/XutD0u4W2p8?t=18m1s", "annotation_id": "34280844", "url": "https://genius.com/34280844", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "(Keep your fuckin’ kids off the street)", "vault_lines": ["Poppin' that shit get you hit quick fast (Mink, mink, mink)"], "meaning": "This ad-lib references a comment from the 1995 movie Friday:\n\nFelicia, get your fuckin kids off the street\n\nSexyy is comparing herself to Felicia, who was more likely to fight for dick than to fight for respect. At the same time, using Felicia here also recalls the popular internet meme “Bye, Felicia,” which allows Sexyy to invoke Felicia both as inspiration and also as a dismissal towards those who are trying to criticize her.", "annotation_id": "33704860", "url": "https://genius.com/33704860", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "[Chorus: Tyler, The Creator]", "vault_lines": ["Poppin' that shit get you hit quick fast (Mink, mink, mink)"], "meaning": "A TikTok account called jazmineeeee___ posted a video showing listeners that they were the girls singing Sticky, Sticky, Sticky in the background.\n\nAnother account, looks like one of the other girls in the Studio posted a video showing photos of them all in the studio with Tyler recording the adlibs.\n\nTyler said he needed some gworlzzz from Atlanta on the track and we showed upp 💚🤪", "annotation_id": "33691903", "url": "https://genius.com/33691903", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Fuck now, mood swingin’ like Chet", "vault_lines": ["Fuck now, mood swingin' like Chet"], "meaning": "Chet, a reference to Chet Baker, who himself was a mercurial figure with a ““well-publicized drug habit\" [that] also drove his notoriety and fame; Baker was in and out of jail frequently before enjoying a career resurgence in the late 1970s and ‘80s.”. Baker was known as the “prince of cool” and took elements of mainstream jazz genres like swing and big band to envision and help create entirely new  genres: perhaps representing an aspirational figure for Tyler or his legacy.", "annotation_id": "33551612", "url": "https://genius.com/33551612", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "I be gettin’ off like holiday, man, pull some paper out", "vault_lines": ["I be gettin' off like holiday, man, pull some paper out"], "meaning": "Punning simile that uses the phrase “gettin' off” to imply both leaving the country to go on vacation while also referencing having an orgasm. Similarly, “pull some paper out” refers to the ability to spend money as a means to signify one’s financial standing while simultaneously referencing the paper that will be used to catch said ejaculation.", "annotation_id": "33474194", "url": "https://genius.com/33474194", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Fuck comparin’ bank accounts (Uh)", "vault_lines": ["Fuck comparin' bank accounts (Uh)"], "meaning": "Here, he’s rejecting the idea of comparing his wealth to others. He’s confident in his own financial status and uninterested in measuring his success against anyone else’s.", "annotation_id": "33474164", "url": "https://genius.com/33474164", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "I can fit ten-hundred thousand in these trousers (Mhm)", "vault_lines": ["I can fit ten-hundred thousand in these trousers (Mhm)"], "meaning": "He’s flexing his wealth by saying he can carry a significant amount of cash casually, symbolizing his financial success.", "annotation_id": "33474082", "url": "https://genius.com/33474082", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "This shit regular, regular, all that shit be regular", "vault_lines": ["This shit regular, regular, all that shit be regular"], "meaning": "Tyler is emphasizing that his high standards and lifestyle are “regular” for him, indicating he’s used to a level of luxury that others might find extraordinary.", "annotation_id": "33474054", "url": "https://genius.com/33474054", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Okay, big-dick nigga big stompin’ through your town", "vault_lines": ["Okay, big-dick nigga big stompin' through your town"], "meaning": "Tyler likens himself to Big Dick Randy, a non-fictional character who comes out on Halloween and Christmas to take people’s booty and perform devious acts on them.\n\nMore specifically, he references the second installation of the series. In BIG DICK RANDY 2 by DigBar, Big Dick Randy raps:\n\n“Halloween I’m on the US tour and I’ll be in yo city”\n-Big Dick Randy, “BIG DICK RANDY 2”", "annotation_id": "33463832", "url": "https://genius.com/33463832", "state": "pending", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Must be God instead of me", "vault_lines": ["Must be God instead of me"], "meaning": "Tyler ends this part with a humble yet powerful statement. The only one Tyler acknowledges as “ahead” is God, placing himself just under divine power. This mix of confidence and humility has become part of Tyler’s persona as he’s grown in fame—he recognizes his own success but keeps perspective, a shift from his younger days when he was known for an unfiltered ego.\n\nTyler may also be suggesting he is a conduit for Divinity, so the work he’s done isn’t his own but rather credited to “God”.\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: all he saying is no one better than him (contradicting what he said in rah tah tah)", "annotation_id": "33461185", "url": "https://genius.com/33461185", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Knock, knock, knock, knock, knockin’ at the door (I’m knockin’ at the door)", "vault_lines": ["Knock, knock, knock, knock, knockin' at the door (I'm knockin' at the door)"], "meaning": "The repetition of “knock” could symbolize persistence and his determination to make his presence known, both in the music industry and within his creative circles. Tyler’s come a long way from his Odd Future days, where he was constantly “knocking” for attention with his raw and controversial style. Now, he’s in a position where people know he’s a force to be reckoned with, yet he still plays with that idea of entering spaces on his own terms.\n\nTyler also uses this line in an unreleased verse on “Like Him” which is featured on the Chromakopia test pressing version of the vinyl.\n“Knock, knock, knock (At the door)”\n\nIn GONE GONE / THANK YOU from IGOR, Tyler says “That bitch walked outside the front door knock knock knock knock”. This line is very similar and likely self-referential.", "annotation_id": "33460955", "url": "https://genius.com/33460955", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Pop that shit like I’m poppin’ some gum Who the fuck you talkin’ to, ho? I ain’t the one", "vault_lines": ["Pop that shit like I'm poppin' some gum"], "meaning": "The punning simile in the first line compares Tyler’s confrontational attitude to the casual act of popping gum by drawing upon two very different definitions of pop. In making this comparison, Tyler’s confidence is revealed—it is as easy for him to pop off or put someone in their place as it is to pop gum. Tyler has long been known for his carefree attitude and often treats serious topics with a lighthearted, almost dismissive tone.\n\nIt also continues the “poppin'” seen in the two previous lines, with the simile being used as a set up for the last line where Tyler exerts his authority by silencing those who are talking to him, verbally popping their challenges.", "annotation_id": "33460909", "url": "https://genius.com/33460909", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Poppin’ that shit get you hit quick fast (Mink, mink, mink)", "vault_lines": ["Poppin' that shit get you hit quick fast (Mink, mink, mink)"], "meaning": "“Mink” is a slang term for a hard punch, popularized by clips of CoryxKenshin saying the word in rapid succession. Tyler even mimics this cadence as an adlib in the intro.\n\nKeloids are a form of raised scarring on the skin that form after previous injury. They specifically refer to scars that grow bigger than the original wound.\n\n\n\nIn other words, Tyler has learned to hold back physical aggression when angry because he’s already been in too many fights. The reference of keloid scars could refer to the knowledge that problems tend to get worse when violence comes up.", "annotation_id": "33460705", "url": "https://genius.com/33460705", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Buddy pillow talkin’, “Stay away from ’Konma” (Mhm) A homewrecker, I be fuckin’ who I wanna (Mhm)", "vault_lines": ["Buddy pillow talkin', \"Stay away from 'Konma\" (Mhm)"], "meaning": "Pillow talks are conversations which occur between romantic partners, usually in bed. One partner might be telling the other that they should stay away from Tyler because he is, as demonstrated by the next line, a homewrecker.\n\nThis bar also plays on Tyler’s last name (Okonma) and the word coma to link with the theme of the bed.", "annotation_id": "33456205", "url": "https://genius.com/33456205", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Fly as fuck, niggas want that motion, I be in the cut", "vault_lines": ["Fly as fuck, niggas want that motion, I be in the cut"], "meaning": "Big Glo dropping some basketball knowledge, “motion” is a play learned in fundamental basketball where all players should be passing and cutting to the hoop to get open. “want that motion” Call the play “I be in the cut” she’s gonna be there\n\nAlso “in the cut” is known as chillin and hanging somewhere", "annotation_id": "33453905", "url": "https://genius.com/33453905", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Allergies to bum niggas, I see you, my eyes itchin’", "vault_lines": ["Allergies to bum niggas, I see you, my eyes itchin'"], "meaning": "This could be a double entendre, referencing the “ICU”, or Intensive Care Unit, a section in hospitals that provides specialized care for very ill or critically injured patients.\n\nThis idea is reinforced by the mentions of allergies and itchy eyes.", "annotation_id": "33452707", "url": "https://genius.com/33452707", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Standin’ like ovation on business occupation", "vault_lines": ["Standin' like ovation on business occupation"], "meaning": "Wayne uses a double entendre to display his character and elegance.\n\nHe uses the popularized slang term “standing on business” to convey his sense of personal values and principles. Wayne does this so consistently and extraordinarily, that it would be likened to his “occupation”, and deserving of a “standing ovation”.", "annotation_id": "33447380", "url": "https://genius.com/33447380", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Sticky situation (Rock ’em), discombobulation", "vault_lines": ["Sticky situation (Rock 'em), discombobulation"], "meaning": "Wayne contributes to the overall theme of the song by reflecting his own experiences being  involved in sticky situations. This is proven by his criminal history, including his 2021 federal gun possession charge.\n\nDiscombobulation is a state of embarrassment, confusion, and discomfort. The word is essentially used as a threat of violence by Wayne, following the rising tension within the song. The line also echoes with his 2023 collaboration with Dame D.O.L.L.A. on “My Daddy’s Son”:\n\nI will pistol whip ya, I will bobblehead ya\nI will discontinue, discombobulate ya", "annotation_id": "33447179", "url": "https://genius.com/33447179", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "These bitches tryna scrap, but I’m knuckin’ if you buckin’, ho", "vault_lines": ["These bitches tryna scrap, but I'm knuckin' if you buckin', ho"], "meaning": "To “knuck if you buck” is a slang phrase that indicates that you are ready to fight someone when they are provoking you and “tryna scrap.” The “ho” at the end is intended to further indicate that GloRilla is more than ready for these fools.\n\nThe phrase to “knuck if you buck” was popularized by Atlanta hip-hop group Crime Mob, in their 2004 song “Knuck if You Buck.”", "annotation_id": "33439437", "url": "https://genius.com/33439437", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Drippin’ condensation, gotta whisper conversations", "vault_lines": ["Drippin' condensation, gotta whisper conversations"], "meaning": "“Drippin’ condensation” suggests a humid, close setting like a room where the air feels thick, possibly from tension or emotion. “Gotta whisper conversations” implies that wayne feels the need to be quiet, perhaps to maintain privacy or secrecy. It paints a picture of a hushed, almost steamy moment where people are sharing words softly, adding a layer of mystery or intensity to the scene.", "annotation_id": "33434124", "url": "https://genius.com/33434124", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "It’s gettin’ sticky, sticky, sticky (Ooh) Bitch, it’s gettin’ sticky, sticky, sticky Bro, it’s gettin’ sticky, sticky, sticky Bitch, it’s gettin’ sticky, sticky, sticky", "vault_lines": ["It's gettin'— sing that shit, sticky, sticky, sticky, bitch"], "meaning": "“It’s gettin' sticky” is likely a slang phrase meaning that a fight is about to happen. Both GloRilla and Sexyy Red’s verses allude to this in their final line:\n\nThese bitches tryna scrap, but I’m knuckin' if you buckin', ho\n[…]\nFah-fah-fah-fah, baby girl, I’m finna rock yo' shit\n\nIt could also mean that guns are about to get pulled out, playing on the firearm slang “stick.” While “fah-fah-fah-fah” can be interpreted as gunshots, the intro describes “popping off,” a slang for shooting:\n\nPoppin' that shit, get you hit quick fast\nNiggas poppin' off and niggas steppin' on the gas\n\nTyler mentions having a “cannon underneath the bed” on “Noid,” being familiar with the gun and gang culture of Los Angeles on “MOTHER,” and later mentions shooting without aim in his verse:\n\nFuck that aiming bout? Hittin' everything that’s in my way\n\nThere’s also the sexual interpretation, as the phrase may refer to the “stickiness” of sexual fluids, which Tyler alludes to in the refrain when he references his 2023 oral sex anthem “DOGTOOTH.” Sex is explicitly part of everyone’s verses minus Lil Wayne:\n\nSneaky link, she say that’s her nigga, I don’t give a fuck\n[…]\nI don’t fight for my respect, bitch, I fight for dick\n[…]\nThat’s a bet, skip the sex, ride my face\n\nWhatever the meaning, Tyler plays on this in the refrain where he says he “better find a mop,” as they’re used to clean literally sticky floors. “Mop” is also slang for a gun with a large magazine.", "annotation_id": "33432535", "url": "https://genius.com/33432535", "state": "accepted", "votes": 140, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Niggas poppin’ off and niggas steppin’ on the gas", "vault_lines": ["Niggas poppin' off and niggas steppin' on the gas"], "meaning": "This line could be interpreted as a jab at artists who suddenly appear and disappear with the same, or even faster, momentum.\n\nTyler is possibly referring to the phenomenon where some artists arrive on the scene suddenly and “pop off” by making bold statements or creating a buzz, but they lack the substance or longevity to maintain their position. These artists often experience fleeting fame and fail to establish themselves with the consistency, creativity, or resilience needed to stay relevant over time.", "annotation_id": "33431816", "url": "https://genius.com/33431816", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "See, I’m a Westside nigga from the zone (What’s goin’ on, nigga?)", "vault_lines": ["See, I'm a Westside nigga from the zone (What's goin' on, nigga?)"], "meaning": "This could be referring to The Zone, a sex club in West LA that catered to gay and bisexual men.\n\nAdditionally, it may also because he’s from Hawthorne and the area or zone around LA, Compton, Inglewood where there’s a lot of gangs and a lot of affiliated rappers are from.", "annotation_id": "33428731", "url": "https://genius.com/33428731", "state": "pending", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Bitch, let it be Niggas say I ain’t that guy, they must be smoking ketamine Always talkin’ ’bout potential, bitch, I am the better me", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, lеt it be"], "meaning": "Tyler’s reference to “ketamine” highlights the surreal nature of underestimating him, comparing such thoughts to the dissociative effects of the drug. By addressing his own “potential,” Tyler rejects external judgments, asserting that he’s already reached his “better me.” This confidence aligns with his thematic evolution, where he emphasizes self-validation over others’ expectations.", "annotation_id": "33428369", "url": "https://genius.com/33428369", "state": "pending", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Caught me red-handed, palms itchin’ like rosacea", "vault_lines": ["Caught me red-handed, palms itchin' like rosacea"], "meaning": "Rosacea is a skin condition that causes persistent redness on the face, often affecting the cheeks, nose, chin, and forehead. It primarily impacts middle-aged women with fair skin and can sometimes include visible blood vessels, swelling, and sensitivity.", "annotation_id": "33427367", "url": "https://genius.com/33427367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Jack of all trades, name a nigga who ahead of me", "vault_lines": ["Jack of all trades, name a nigga who ahead of me"], "meaning": "The phrase “jack of all trades” is a Medieval idiom, simply meaning “man of all trades”,  referring to someone who is skilled in many areas.\n\nTyler calling himself a “jack of all trades” could imply how he considers himself the best at all styles of music, proven in his wide versatility through his discography, with widly different songs ranging from the calm, vocally impressive See You Again to more hip-hop oriented songs like this track, Sticky.", "annotation_id": "33427265", "url": "https://genius.com/33427265", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "line": "Nigga, give a fuck ’bout pronouns, I’m that nigga and that bitch", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, give a fuck 'bout pronouns, I'm that nigga and that bitch"], "meaning": "Tyler previously addressed the LGBTQ+ movement to encourage the usage of pronouns. T unapologetically rejects what other people think of him on “SORRY NOT SORRY,” the final track of The Estate Sale:\n\nSorry I don’t know your pronouns\nI don’t mean no disrespect\nBut, damn, we just met, calm the fuck down\nOh, I’m out of touch and I’m a jerk?\n\nTyler himself has a complex history with his sexuality, one could interpret this as being a statement of uncaring bisexuality. He doesn’t care about a person’s gender identity, he’ll get sticky with all of them. This is also expressed by the dichotomy of “bro” and “bitch” in the chorus.", "annotation_id": "33426574", "url": "https://genius.com/33426574", "state": "accepted", "votes": 218, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sticky", "status": "done", "targets": 37, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Tyler-the-creator-sticky-lyrics", "genius_title": "Sticky", "genius_id": 11044856, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Stick ’Em", "line": "I’ma let my nigga shine, I’ma let my nigga shine And if you ain’t my nigga, you a cloud to a nigga", "vault_lines": ["I'ma let my nigga shine, I'ma let my nigga shine"], "meaning": "In the first line, Wayne states that he wants those around him who have his best interest in mind to shine like a star, meaning he wants to see them successful. However, in the second line, Wayne states that if you’re around him but perhaps have an ulterior motive that isn’t in his best interests, you’re a hinderance, blocking his shine like a cloud does to the Sun.", "annotation_id": "20888887", "url": "https://genius.com/20888887", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
