{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Steppers", "line": "Tunechi", "vault_lines": ["That's why Tunechi went first and I'm second on niggas"], "meaning": "“Tunechi” is a widely used nickname and alter-ego of rapper Lil Wayne (Dwayne Michael Carter, Jr.). The name builds on “Tune” a nickname from his childhood, short for Tune/Tunafish — dressed up with a stylized ending.", "annotation_id": "41171026", "url": "https://genius.com/41171026", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Steppers", "line": "Cincinnati hat and I got stripes, but I don’t play for the Bengals, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Cincinnati hat and I got stripes, but I don't play for the Bengals, nigga"], "meaning": "Both Game and Lil Wayne are affiliated with the Bloods street gang, whose members often wear red clothing. It’s common for these gang members to wear Cincinnati Reds baseball hats due to its color. Weezy even wore one in the “Lollipop” music video. Game has referenced rocking a Reds hat multiple times in his music, most notably in “Red Nation” which also featured Weezy:\n\nThrow your muthafucking Cincinnati hats in the sky, nigga don’t ask why\n\n\n\nHe then references the NFL team the Cincinnati Bengals whose helmets include an iconic tiger-stripe pattern. The “stripes” that Game is actually referring to is the hard work and hustle he’s put into his career.", "annotation_id": "41170617", "url": "https://genius.com/41170617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
