{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Started", "line": "Tunechi, everybody know your name like Cheers, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Tunechi, everybody know your name like Cheers, nigga"], "meaning": "Reference to the theme song for the critically acclaimed 80’s sitcom Cheers, used to further explain Wayne’s raging popularity in modern day media.\n\n“Sometimes you wanna go, where everybody know your name!”\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rS0VQOHX7lM\n\nBUT WAIT!\n\nTunechi isn’t the only one to make a Cheers reference in the last few months:\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=csl-RW41Miw", "annotation_id": "2162327", "url": "https://genius.com/2162327", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Started", "line": "Tell my bitch I could do bad on my own", "vault_lines": ["Tell my bitch I could do bad on my own"], "meaning": "References a popular African American phrase “I can do bad all by myself”, which basically means that you can mess up without anyone and that you don’t need any help from the ones you hate/dislike.\n\nThe phrase came into a peak of popularity when Tyler Perry used it for the title of one of his first plays, I Can Do Bad All By Myself and, much later, used the same title for the film alternative in late 2009.\n\nThe Play:\n\n\n\nThe Film:", "annotation_id": "2162312", "url": "https://genius.com/2162312", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
