{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "(Yeah, Mickey and Mallory)", "vault_lines": ["Let's start a fire"], "meaning": "Mickey and Mallory Knox are the two main characters in the 1994 drama film Natural Born Killers. Wayne uses this to make the first of three well known couple comparisons.", "annotation_id": "4456517", "url": "https://genius.com/4456517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Make her perspire, sweat, blood, and crying", "vault_lines": ["Make her perspire, sweat, blood, and crying"], "meaning": "Wayne uses the idiom “blood, sweat, and tears” to explain how much effort and – er – hard work goes into their sex.\n\nIt doesn’t paint the prettiest picture, but hey, it gets the point across.", "annotation_id": "4430517", "url": "https://genius.com/4430517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Thelma and Lil Weezy", "vault_lines": ["Thelma and Lil Weezy"], "meaning": "Playing off famed duo ‘Thelma and Louise’, Two friends who become outlaws. Wayne is instead assuming a male role as “Lil Weezy” instead of “Louise”.", "annotation_id": "4429234", "url": "https://genius.com/4429234", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Oh, I ran into my old thing I say, “Bitch, you broke my heart, but that’s just growin’ pains”", "vault_lines": ["Oh, I ran into my old thing"], "meaning": "At one time, Wayne was in love with a girl who ultimately broke his heart. It hurt back then, but now he looks back at the painful situation as a learning experience.", "annotation_id": "4429217", "url": "https://genius.com/4429217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Young Mula baby", "vault_lines": ["Let's start a fire"], "meaning": "Among Lil Waynes many ad-libs, Tunechi is famous for yelling\n\nYoung Mula Baby\n\nAfter he has finished rapping his verse. You can find all of his other ad libs, among the ad libs of other rappers here.", "annotation_id": "4428534", "url": "https://genius.com/4428534", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "I’m like goodness gracious, great wall of China All we do is fuck all day, all mañana", "vault_lines": ["I'm like goodness gracious, great wall of China"], "meaning": "To begin the verse, Wayne plays off the 1957 hit “Great Balls of Fire” by Jerry Lee Lewis – he references the track again in the second verse. The Great Wall of China is one of the world’s most famous landmarks and could be used to express Wayne’s surprise.\n\nC'mon baby, you drive me crazy\nGoodness gracious great balls of fire!\n\n\n\nIn Spanish, “mañana” means “tomorrow”, so Wayne was kind enough to give us a Spanish lesson while describing how often he and this girl have sex.", "annotation_id": "4428492", "url": "https://genius.com/4428492", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Stop, drop, roll, and shake what her mama gave her", "vault_lines": ["Stop, drop, roll, and shake what her mama gave her"], "meaning": "Following up on the concept of a fire being started, Wayne wants Christina to stop, drop & roll since she is on fire.\n\nShe may want to shake what her mama gave her, which is a popular phrase used when going to dance. However, they may be doing something more intimate than dancing.", "annotation_id": "4428491", "url": "https://genius.com/4428491", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Yeah, Bonnie and Clyde,", "vault_lines": ["Let's start a fire"], "meaning": "Is Wayne taking a page out of Jay Z and Beyonce’s book by comparing his bad girl and himself to one of the most notorious couples in history… Bonnie and Clyde?", "annotation_id": "4428488", "url": "https://genius.com/4428488", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Now that pussy is my domain", "vault_lines": ["Now that pussy is my domain"], "meaning": "A domain is someones home. Wayne is basically saying that he has sex with this girl so much, that he practically lives in her vagina.\n\nThis isn’t the first time Weezy has said something like this, in the song Tapout he says:\n\nThat’s because I sleep in that ho", "annotation_id": "4428479", "url": "https://genius.com/4428479", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "It’s a cold world, I stay warm inside her", "vault_lines": ["It's a cold world, I stay warm inside her"], "meaning": "It’s a cold world, this is a phrase used to describe the cruelness of the world. However Lil Wayne doesn’t care about anything because he stays warm when he is inside of her, or inside the pussy.\n\nJust another one of Wayne’s really sexual lines.", "annotation_id": "4428461", "url": "https://genius.com/4428461", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "She ridin’ me like a street car named Desire", "vault_lines": ["She ridin' me like a street car named Desire"], "meaning": "Referencing the legendary play by Tennessee Williams, this line has more meaning to Lil Wayne than it might seem at first. The sexual wordplay is impressive, but the locale is just as important. Set in New Orleans and inspired by Desire Street in the city, Weezy gets to proclaim his two biggest loves in one line: sex, and hometown pride.", "annotation_id": "4428449", "url": "https://genius.com/4428449", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "’Cause now I got more time to burn in that pussy like some propane", "vault_lines": ["'Cause now I got more time to burn in that pussy like some propane"], "meaning": "Less than a year after Wayne’s admirer Young Thug dropped questionable bars possibly asking for an STD, it appears the student has taught the master.\n\nIt’s a play on burn/kill some time. He’s burning time by having sex and “like propane” is playing on the word burn.", "annotation_id": "4428430", "url": "https://genius.com/4428430", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "We break all of monotony, baby, we high commodity", "vault_lines": ["We break all of monotony, baby, we high commodity"], "meaning": "Monotony is meaning that something is repetitive. Implying that Wayne and Christina don’t get tired of having the same sexual intercourse over and over.\n\nHigh Commodity is something that is very valuable. Wayne is very wealthy, along with the wealth of Christina Milian, so clearly, both the sex they have, and the money is all a big value to Wayne.", "annotation_id": "4428428", "url": "https://genius.com/4428428", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "I’m so glad I’m out the dope game", "vault_lines": ["I'm so glad I'm out the dope game"], "meaning": "Wayne no longer has to sell drugs to make a living – he has a more stable and honest way of living now. It also probably pays a lot more. He has always mentioned his drug dealing past like in the song “Misunderstood”:\n\nWe probably only selling the crack cocaine because we in the hood\nAnd it’s not like in the suburbs, we don’t have what you have\n\nNow he is the rapper and he doesn’t sell drugs but if you need work his homies still.", "annotation_id": "4428426", "url": "https://genius.com/4428426", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "And I threw water on that old flame, no more flames", "vault_lines": ["And I threw water on that old flame, no more flames"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne has had too many relationships before, and now he has gotten over all of his ex girlfriends, he doesn’t have feelings for them anymore.\n\n\n\nHe is currently dating Christina Milian and sparking flames with her.", "annotation_id": "4428422", "url": "https://genius.com/4428422", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Clap that ass like the choir", "vault_lines": ["Clap that ass like the choir"], "meaning": "One of Wayne’s many ass similes – comparing this girl’s to the literal clapping a church choir does when singing together.\n\nYou can almost hear the singing now…", "annotation_id": "4428413", "url": "https://genius.com/4428413", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "But trust me, she ain’t nothing but an old flame", "vault_lines": ["But trust me, she ain't nothing but an old flame"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne had some previous feelings for this girl, she used to spark some excitement to him, but now she is just somebody that he used to know. Know he has a new, hotter girl who he he is willing to spark a new flame with.\n\nWayne compares this girl to a flame because like flame, their love had died.", "annotation_id": "4428412", "url": "https://genius.com/4428412", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Mama, I’m in love with a hot girl, and they just don’t get it They just don’t get it Temperature’s risin’, her body yearnin’, yeah, we on that R. Kelly And then she tell me", "vault_lines": ["Mama, I'm in love with a hot girl, and they just don't get it"], "meaning": "Weezy and Christina finally confirm their rumored fling between a Hot Boy and a hot girl. Their explosive chemistry will spark a flame that sets the whole world on fire – in other words, everyone will know.\n\nWeezy name-drops R. Kelly and quotes his 1993 song “It Seems Like You’re Ready”.\n\nTemperature’s rising\nAnd your body’s yearnin' for me\n\nMany have publicly admitted they “just don’t get” this relationship. Nivea (one of Weezy’s five baby mamas and ex-wife/baby mama of Christina’s ex-husband The-Dream…got that?) slammed the couple for not putting their children first. Toya Carter, Weezy’s first wife, called the relationship a “hot mess”.", "annotation_id": "4428401", "url": "https://genius.com/4428401", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "line": "Goodness gracious, great God almighty She fuck me like a king, Stephen, Martin, Rodney", "vault_lines": ["Goodness gracious, great God almighty"], "meaning": "Thank God almighty, we are free at last\n\nFirst of all, Weezy’s new girl treats him as if he were a king. She gets to a level of freaky that most girls wouldn’t go to unless the guy was royalty. Kanye West also used a similar line.\n\nSecondly, Wayne , once again, is comparing three dissimilar people, who have a similar names or surnames. In this case he is comparing:\n\n\nStephen King – A popular American author who has sold over 350 million books worldwide.\nMartin Luther King – A civil rights activist, humanitarian, and a prominent leader in the African-American Civil Rights Movement.\nRodney King – Rodney Glen King III was an African American man who became a symbol of police brutality after being horrendously beaten by Los Angeles police officers.\n\n\n\nIs it possible that Wayne is saying that he sells records like Stephen King sells books, preaches like Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. and he is a symbol of rap much like how Rodney king is a symbol of police brutality?", "annotation_id": "4428398", "url": "https://genius.com/4428398", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start a Fire", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-start-a-fire-lyrics", "genius_title": "Start a Fire", "genius_id": 591887, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "Smiled at my first judge like I’ll be out tomorrow Keep the Glock on ’em", "vault_lines": ["Smiled at my first judge like I'll be out tomorrow"], "meaning": "Wayne has famously stood his ground when faced with legal consequences for his actions.\n\nEven after serving eight months on Riker’s Island in 2010 for illegal possession of a firearm, Wayne assures the listener that he “Keep[s] the Glock on [him].”\n\nUnfortunately, Wayne was later caught with illegal drugs and an illegal firearm on his private jet, for which he faced federal criminal charges (crimes for which Wayne was eventually pardoned by President Donald Trump in January 2021).", "annotation_id": "33187375", "url": "https://genius.com/33187375", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "Now I feel like found money", "vault_lines": ["Now I feel like found money"], "meaning": "As stated in the next line, he feels like he’s awesome. What’s more awesome than free money that you found out and about?", "annotation_id": "22045217", "url": "https://genius.com/22045217", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "We gon’ start this shit off right (Off right) We got bad-ass bitches in the house tonight We gon’ start this shit off right (Off right) We got some bad-ass bitches in the house tonight We gon’ start this shit off right (Off right) We got some bad-ass bitches in the house tonight We gon’ start this shit off right (Off right) We got some bad-ass bitches in the house tonight", "vault_lines": ["We gon' start this shit off right (Off right)"], "meaning": "This samples DJ Jimi’s intro to his 1992 hit “Where They At.” The original lyrics were:\n\nWe gon' start this shit off right\nWe got DJ Jimi in the house tonight\nWe gon' start this shit off right\nWe got Mellow Fellow in the house tonight\nWe gon' start this shit off right\nWe got Devious in the house tonight\nWe gon' start this shit off right, right\nWe got New Orleans in the house tonight, tonight", "annotation_id": "21538050", "url": "https://genius.com/21538050", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "I got a white girl she call me names like bastard and jerk That’s when I send her ass back to the ’burbs", "vault_lines": ["I got a white girl she call me names like bastard and jerk"], "meaning": "I wonder if this is a reference to the show, “The Burbs”.. all white cast, use of the word “bastard” throughout it.", "annotation_id": "21486702", "url": "https://genius.com/21486702", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "(Lu-uh) Fresh, can you please tell these niggas I’ve been rich since the Hot Boys?", "vault_lines": ["(Lu-uh) Fresh, can you please tell these niggas I’ve been rich since the Hot Boys?"], "meaning": "Weezy has been wealthy since he was a part of the New Orleans-based group the “Hot Boys”, which also included Juvenile, B.G., and Turk. The group formed in 1997.", "annotation_id": "16044823", "url": "https://genius.com/16044823", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "(Luh-uh-uh-uh-uh-uh-huh) And since I’m on my Spanish shit, them other boys finito", "vault_lines": ["(Luh-uh-uh-uh-uh-uh-huh) And since I’m on my Spanish shit, them other boys finito"], "meaning": "Finito is Spanish for finished. Ergo, “them other boys finished.”", "annotation_id": "15481729", "url": "https://genius.com/15481729", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "Fucked her right good night, die wit’ em open", "vault_lines": ["Fucked her right good night, die wit' em open"], "meaning": "“Die” refers to la petite mort, the French term for the little death, an expression meaning “the brief loss or weakening of consciousness” and in modern usage refers specifically to “the sensation of orgasm as likened to death.”", "annotation_id": "15481542", "url": "https://genius.com/15481542", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "This a Mannie Fresh beat and I go Pac on it", "vault_lines": ["This a Mannie Fresh beat and I go Pac on it"], "meaning": "Weezy finds himself, once again, rhyming over a Mannie Fresh beat which, evidently, he feels brings out the best in him, inspiring a performance that he feels would worthy of deceased rap legend Tupac Shakur.\n\nWayne and Mannie Fresh have a long and very prolific collaborative history, which stems from both mens former association with New Orleans label Cash Money Records.\n\nMannie served almost exclusively as in-house producer for all the labels acts during his tenure with them which included, amongst other things, producing, in their entirety, the first two albums from Wayne’s breakout group the Hot Boys and all three of his first solo albums.\n\nHe would also helm the vast majority of the first instalment of Tha Carter series but, upon his 2005 departure from the label, found himself absent completely from it’s acclaimed follow-up, Tha Carter II, making it the first Weezy album to lack any sort of input from Mannie.\n\nThe two men have worked together much more sporadically since then but remain on good terms, with Wayne evidently still feeling that his old friend brings out the best in him.", "annotation_id": "15479284", "url": "https://genius.com/15479284", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "Cocaine, bakin’ soda, and some hot water Kept the fiends comin’ back to me, not karma", "vault_lines": ["Cocaine, bakin' soda, and some hot water"], "meaning": "Karma is an old concept in Hinduism and Buddhism, with its most simplistic explanation being “what goes around comes around”, as in your good deeds.\n\nIn this sense, Weezy uses the ideology of Karma as a metaphor for the way drug addicts are reacting to the quality of his crack.", "annotation_id": "15475001", "url": "https://genius.com/15475001", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "With Mannie Fresh the O.G., now it’s a G.O. (Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["With Mannie Fresh the O.G., now it's a G.O. (Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah)"], "meaning": "Mannie Fresh was at one time the in-house producer for Cash Money, producing many records for Wayne, especially during his time with the Hot Boys and the Big Tymers.\n\nHowever, since Mannie left Cash Money in 2005, he and Wayne hadn’t worked together for a long time until 2016 when Wayne was featured on Hate Mannie’s first single in years.\n\nMannie Fresh is 13 years older than Wayne, making him his O.G., and now that they are both free from Cash Money Records and can finally collaborate again.\n\n\n\nWayne, Mannie and Slim circa. 1999", "annotation_id": "15474648", "url": "https://genius.com/15474648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "Muscle car look like I just got out the bing, whoa", "vault_lines": ["Muscle car look like I just got out the bing, whoa"], "meaning": "When in the bing, or jail, there isn’t much to do besides work out, which is why many inmates look ripped in pictures and come out huge.\n\n\n\nWayne’s muscle car looks like it just got out of jail because it’s “so muscley”, probably with a supercharged engine and other custom aspects.", "annotation_id": "15474555", "url": "https://genius.com/15474555", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "Met the devil, came back feelin’ frío Gang bangin’, tatted up like Latinos (Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah) And since I’m on my Spanish shit, it’s Carter Cinco", "vault_lines": ["Met the devil, came back feelin' frío"], "meaning": "‘Frio’ is the Spanish word for cold. The Devil lives in Hell where it is very hot, however Weezy must be much hotter, because he came back cold.\n\nWayne is a proud member of the Bloods, often talking about his affiliation. While they rep different gangs, his full body of tattoos reflect the tattoos that Latino gang members typically get.\n\n\n\nWayne’s plethora of tats\n\n\n\nLatino gang members tats\n\nContinuing with the Spanish theme of the previous two lines, Wayne refers to his album as ‘Carter Cinco’, cinco of course meaning 5 in Spanish​.", "annotation_id": "15474535", "url": "https://genius.com/15474535", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "And I’m gettin’ more checks than a peep hole (Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["And I’m gettin' more checks than a peep hole (Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah)"], "meaning": "Great wordplay here. When someone knocks at your door, what do you do?\n\nCheck the peephole!\n\nThe checks Wayne’s getting are a little different though, ones with large numbers on them.", "annotation_id": "15474528", "url": "https://genius.com/15474528", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "Leave ya house haunted I ain’t kiddin’, I ain’t playin’, fuck up your house party", "vault_lines": ["Leave ya house haunted"], "meaning": "When a house is considered haunted, it’s typically because someone has died in there. Weezy will run up in your house during a house party and kill you, effectively making your house haunted.\n\nAlso some great wordplay by Wayne.\nKid N Play was a rap duo, and the two members, Kid and Play, starred together in the movie House Party).\n\nI aint Kiddin I aint Playin, fuck up your House Party\n\nhttps://resizing.flixster.com/e0KrLh-Ye3Ui4UyRxID-SYFtrik=/206x305/v1.bTsxMTE2OTY5NTtqOzE3OTA0OzEyMDA7ODAwOzEyMDA", "annotation_id": "15474512", "url": "https://genius.com/15474512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "My homeboys proud of me like Barack homies", "vault_lines": ["My homeboys proud of me like Barack homies"], "meaning": "Barack Obama, being the first black president, has received many misconceptions about his character, however, he has gotten a high level of respect from African Americans and people in lower-income communities because he came from the same place as them.\n\nWayne’s homies are proud of him for being one of the most successful rappers of all time, so he can’t even imagine how Obama’s friends must feel.", "annotation_id": "15474486", "url": "https://genius.com/15474486", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "You drive me crazy you gon’ crash the ’vert", "vault_lines": ["You drive me crazy you gon' crash the 'vert"], "meaning": "“Driving [someone] crazy” is an idiom that means that someone or something is extremely annoying.\n\nWayne uses the idiom to make a double entendre. He is presumably telling a girl that she is driving him crazy, as well as literally driving recklessly. A “‘vert” is a convertible car.", "annotation_id": "15474474", "url": "https://genius.com/15474474", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "Its Lil Tunechi, word around on the streets That everythin’ is workin’ out like obliques, they wanna see it obese", "vault_lines": ["Its Lil Tunechi, word around on the streets"], "meaning": "Obliques are muscles in your stomach that are on the side of your abs, and many people heavily exercise these muscles to look good, using a double entendre on “working out”. With the success of his career, Weezy believes everything is working out for him.\n\n\n\nWeezy addresses haters and people who would like to see him fail by stating they would rather see him “obese”, which results in the opposite effect of working out.", "annotation_id": "15474450", "url": "https://genius.com/15474450", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "I fucked around and made a buncha fuck around money", "vault_lines": ["I fucked around and made a buncha fuck around money"], "meaning": "Wayne’s net worth is a reported $150 million. Wayne claims that he acquired his wealth by “fucking around;” doing something that he enjoyed and found easy.\n\nNow he is in a position where he can “fuck around” with his money and it wouldn’t make much difference.", "annotation_id": "15474438", "url": "https://genius.com/15474438", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "I’m a blood with some C-notes (Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I’m a blood with some C-notes (Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah)"], "meaning": "A C-note is a $100 bill. The nickname is derived from Latin: the Latin word for 100 is “centum,” and the Roman numeral for 100 is C.\n\nWayne points out the irony here, as Bloods are stereotyped to avoid words starting with C or the hard C/K sound (cat, kite) due to their enmity towards the Crips, and replacing the starting sound letter with a B.\n\nNew $100 bills also have a blue stripe running down the middle. The Crips are associated with the color blue while the Bloods are associated with red, which adds to the irony Wayne points out. Wayne actually got in minor trouble for rapping about blue C-notes before.", "annotation_id": "15473061", "url": "https://genius.com/15473061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "I’m fuckin’ awesome, ’til awesome have a child for me", "vault_lines": ["I'm fuckin' awesome, 'til awesome have a child for me"], "meaning": "Wayne describes himself as “fucking awesome,” which he uses to mean really awesome as well as literally having sex with, or “fucking,” a girl named Awesome. In this hypothetical, he gets Awesome pregnant and she gives birth to his child.", "annotation_id": "15473034", "url": "https://genius.com/15473034", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "Got homies who ain’t comin’ home ‘til the cows comin’ Niggas with power, they tryna press our power buttons", "vault_lines": ["Got homies who ain't comin' home 'til the cows comin'"], "meaning": "Doing something “until the cows come home” is an English idiom meaning that what you are doing is futile and useless. The implication of the phrase is that the cows are not coming home for a very long time. Wayne talks about his incarcerated homies in this predicament.\n\nHe also touches on the idea that powerful people are trying to silence and/or kill him, as pressing his “power button” would turn him off.", "annotation_id": "15470999", "url": "https://genius.com/15470999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "She said, “I will,” like ill with an apostrophe", "vault_lines": ["She said, \"I will,\" like ill with an apostrophe"], "meaning": "“I’ll” is the contraction for “I will.” It has the same spelling as the word “ill” but has an apostrophe immediately after the “i.”", "annotation_id": "15470987", "url": "https://genius.com/15470987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Start This Shit Off Right", "line": "All eyes on me, I had a Pac moment", "vault_lines": ["All eyes on me, I had a Pac moment"], "meaning": "All Eyez On Me is the 1996 fourth studio album released by rapper 2pac. It is also the name of the panned 2017 biopic film about Pac’s life.\n\n\n\nAt the time the album was released, the eyes of the music world were all centered on Pac, as a result of his recent release from prison, his primary role in the East Coast vs. West Coast gangsta rap rivalry, and his growing rap career. Lil Wayne is comparing Pac’s media attention to the anticipation around his long-awaited Tha Carter V album.", "annotation_id": "15470721", "url": "https://genius.com/15470721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
