{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Stand Tall (Bitch, Get Off Me)", "line": "And tell ya man I beat up niggas like Harpo (Ha)", "vault_lines": ["And tell ya man I beat up niggas like Harpo (Ha)"], "meaning": "And tell ya man I beat up niggas like “Harpo”\n\n(“Harpo Johnson”, as portrayed by “William E. Pugh” on “The Color Purple”, which was released in theaters nationwide on February 7 in 1986.\n\nThere is a disturbing scene which served as a social commentary for a growing issue of commonality pertaining to domestic abuse, which was becoming a nuance of a greater issue having to do with imperialistically misogynistic and chauvinistic mindsets which were propagated as though it could never be perceived as the aberration and antithesis of common courtesy, as well as compassion for entities who inhabit the same indigenous territory as others do, that it is and always was.  “Sophia Johnson”, as portrayed by “Oprah Winfrey” can be discerned being beaten by “Harpo Johnson” in this particularly aforementioned scene in discussion.)", "annotation_id": "11102447", "url": "https://genius.com/11102447", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
