{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Outside the Lines", "vault_lines": [", um, Outside the Lines"], "meaning": "Award-winning sports news program run on ESPN from 1990 to 2022, covering more serious topics from an investigative journalist perspective. Stories covered include the NFL’s concussion crisis and the USA gymnastics sexual abuse scandal.", "annotation_id": "33163551", "url": "https://genius.com/33163551", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Quite Frankly", "vault_lines": ["My favorite TV shows, man, is SportsCenter"], "meaning": "Sports talk show hosted by Stephen A. Smith that ran on ESPN from 2005 to 2007.", "annotation_id": "33163540", "url": "https://genius.com/33163540", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Around the Horn", "vault_lines": [", um, Around the Horn"], "meaning": "Premiering in 2002, ESPN’s Around the Horn turned the “arguing about sports” format into a game in and of itself, with contestants (usually sports journos or ESPN personalities) gaining and losing points based on the quality of their hot takes. Since 2004 the program has been hosted by former PTI “stat boy” Tony Reali.\n\nLil Wayne won his lone appearance on Around the Horn back in 2009.", "annotation_id": "33163504", "url": "https://genius.com/33163504", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Pardon the Interruption", "vault_lines": [", Pardon the Interruption"], "meaning": "Since 2001, sports writers Tony Kornheiser and Michael Wilbon have hosted Pardon the Interruption (PTI) on ESPN. With its snappy banter and trademark on-screen lineup of topics, PTI is considered a pioneer of the lucrative “yelling about sports” format.", "annotation_id": "33163446", "url": "https://genius.com/33163446", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "ESPN News", "vault_lines": ["My favorite TV shows, man, is SportsCenter"], "meaning": "ESPNews is a 24 hour channel mainly playing SportsCenter and simulcasts of ESPN Radio programs.", "annotation_id": "33163437", "url": "https://genius.com/33163437", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "SportsCenter", "vault_lines": ["My favorite TV shows, man, is SportsCenter"], "meaning": "Flagship program on ESPN, daily news programming covering the world of sports.", "annotation_id": "33163431", "url": "https://genius.com/33163431", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Catch up, bitch, I’m in gear three Zoom, gone, see you, peace! Drop one finger (Gangsta!)", "vault_lines": ["Catch up, bitch, I'm in gear three"], "meaning": "Wayne may be painting the image of him showing you three fingers to indicate the gear he is on. As he drives away, he will give you the peace sign, needing two fingers. However, after some time, he will drop one finger, which implies he is flipping you “the bird”.", "annotation_id": "31680396", "url": "https://genius.com/31680396", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "I keep them away like I got leprosy", "vault_lines": ["I keep them away like I got leprosy"], "meaning": "Leprosy is an infection that causes rashes, lesions, and deformities. The perception of leprosy is that it is highly contagious, so Wayne’s enemies would be apprehensive in engaging him.", "annotation_id": "31678295", "url": "https://genius.com/31678295", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Y’all ballers; I’m a sports agent", "vault_lines": ["Y'all ballers; I'm a sports agent"], "meaning": "“Baller” can often be slang for “a successful human”, but it can also be slang for “a player of basketball”. Here, Wayne is saying he’s in charge of even the best of the best (ballers), almost like a sports agent is in charge of basketball players (also ballers).", "annotation_id": "27637316", "url": "https://genius.com/27637316", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "I get money like a Caucasian The car red, so the car Cajun", "vault_lines": ["I get money like a Caucasian"], "meaning": "Wayne compares his money to a Caucasian’s due to there being lots of it. He backs this up by broadly describing a car (assumably his), saying the car’s Cajun. Because of Wayne’s pronunciation, “Caucasian” and “car: Cajun” are homonyms.", "annotation_id": "27637272", "url": "https://genius.com/27637272", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "I see beef as dead meat", "vault_lines": ["I see beef as dead meat"], "meaning": "Wayne continues the food-related wordplay and sight-relating scheme with “I SEE BEEF as DEAD MEAT”. Here, he’s comparing rap beef  (arguments taken to rap lyrics) to edible beef, which he details to be dead. Dead meat is a term that means something in significant trouble. This dead meat could turn into a different type of dead meat if Wayne really wants to destroy his opponent.", "annotation_id": "27637224", "url": "https://genius.com/27637224", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Cover my tracks like butter, so where the bread be?", "vault_lines": ["Cover my tracks like butter, so where the bread be?"], "meaning": "Wayne compares his tracks to butter, due to their respective definitions of smooth, wondering where he can find his bread (slang for money), which can be accompanied by butter.", "annotation_id": "27637213", "url": "https://genius.com/27637213", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "I’m serving this track, like Steffi Graf, yeah Roger Federer;", "vault_lines": ["I'm serving this track, like Steffi Graf, yeah"], "meaning": "“Serving” is, in general, a way to express total dominance.  Phrases like “You got served” were common in the 2000s and even parodied in South Park after being appropriated by dance competitions.\n\nWayne, however, uses it to reference two of the most dominant tennis players of all time.  In tennis, a serve starts the point, and is often the most impressive shot.\n\nSteffi Graf is one of the most dominant atheletes of all time.\n\nEvery one knows about Serena Williams, which would’ve been the obvious reference, but Serena only tied Graf’s astounding 22 Grand Slams in 2016, well after the release of Dedication 2.  Graf also held the World Number One ranking for a record 377 total weeks, and also holds the record for consecutive weeks at number one with 186.  (Serena, as of July 18, 2016, has been ranked world No. 1 for 179 consecutive weeks, and 302 weeks overall – 7 weeks shy of Graf’s consecutive record.)\n\nRoger Federer is still a top player in 2016, and was totally dominant when Wayne dropped this track.  While his stats are less impressive than Graf’s or Serena’s, he has cemented his status as one of the greatest male tennis players of all time, and his longevity proves that.  Many believe men’s tennis has more talent in 2016 than ever, and Federer began his domination well before that but continues to pose a threat despite his age.", "annotation_id": "10120043", "url": "https://genius.com/10120043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Sittin’ in my big house, surrounded by my weaponries", "vault_lines": ["Sittin' in my big house, surrounded by my weaponries"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne owns a couple of “big houses” indeed. Take a look at his Miami Beach House below:\n\n\n\nIn most of his songs, Wayne mentions guns or other types of weapons, showing that if you mess with him, he has got the right stuff to fight you back, and he won’t hesitate to pull the trigger.", "annotation_id": "3369118", "url": "https://genius.com/3369118", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "“Who that?” The president—yeah, me;", "vault_lines": ["\"Who that?\" The president—yeah, me;"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is the president and owner/founder of the label Young Money. Artists such as Tyga and Nicki Minaj are signed to this label.\n\n\n\nLil Wayne mentions being president at other times too, such as in the song he made titled, “President Carter”", "annotation_id": "3369098", "url": "https://genius.com/3369098", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Yeah! Fuck ’em and whoever made ’em", "vault_lines": ["Yeah! Fuck 'em and whoever made 'em"], "meaning": "To a lot of people this is the ultimate disrespect. Wayne expresses his ultimate repulsion to his enemies and even their parents.\n\nWeezy would take similar shots at Pusha T 6 years later.\n\nFuck Pusha T and anybody that love him – Goulish", "annotation_id": "2625087", "url": "https://genius.com/2625087", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "Young God, baby—all them other niggas reverends", "vault_lines": ["Young God, baby—all them other niggas reverends"], "meaning": "A common rap trope of “doing you one better”. Wayne, who often refers to himself as a “God” on this mixtape compares other rappers to reverends, who clearly occupy a lower position on the totem pole of religious stature.\n\nFurther, he may be implying that the rest of the rap game praises and worships him, making the metaphor even more apt.", "annotation_id": "66931", "url": "https://genius.com/66931", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "SportsCenter", "line": "She gave me relations, so, now, we related The morning comes; the picture faded", "vault_lines": ["She gave me relations, so, now, we related"], "meaning": "He slept with her, so now she thinks they’re in a relationship.  But in the morning, he doesn’t care about her so that idea of a relationship fades away.  He also cant remember last night given his state, so the visual memory (picture) is faded as well.\n\nA similar interpretation would be that the first line is just word play – to “give relations” means to have sex, so Wayne jokes that now they are “related” (though it’s likely that the lucky lady would have jumped at the chance to marry him).", "annotation_id": "66926", "url": "https://genius.com/66926", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
