{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sharks", "line": "Thieves ain’t always in the night", "vault_lines": ["Thieves ain't always in the night"], "meaning": "This alludes to 1 Thessalonians 5:2, where the “day of the Lord” is said to come “like a thief in the night” — a metaphor for sudden divine judgment. They flip this, rejecting the assumption that danger comes predictably or under cover of darkness. In their world, threat is constant and unbound by moral or temporal expectation.", "annotation_id": "35815518", "url": "https://genius.com/35815518", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Sharks", "line": "Snakes ain’t always in the grass Sharks ain’t always in the water", "vault_lines": ["Snakes ain't always in the grass"], "meaning": "The phrase “snakes in the grass” usually refers to deceitful people who plot against you while pretending to be on good terms with you (blending in with the grass). The fact that these snakes ain’t always in the grass suggests that they’re not even trying to hide their intentions from you, their hate is open for you to spot rather than in the places you would expect them to be.\n\n“Sharks ain’t always in the water” shows that Wayne is still in the ocean trying to get shark pussy like he said in IANAHB, but can’t find them because they’re not in the water. Poor Wayne.", "annotation_id": "35813301", "url": "https://genius.com/35813301", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
