{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "I’m a goat, nigga, you a sacrificial lamb You a teddy bear, nigga, you a Teddy Graham", "vault_lines": ["I'm a goat, nigga, you a sacrificial lamb"], "meaning": "Possibly another shot at Drake, as Wayne proclaims his status as the Greatest of All Time (GOAT). Metaphorically, Wayne refers to Drake as an inferior sacrifice by calling him a “lamb”. He then draws the connection to Drake’s last name, Graham, and Teddy Graham snack crackers. Wayne highlights how sweet and harmless they truly are, alluding to Drake’s true character.", "annotation_id": "32463143", "url": "https://genius.com/32463143", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "I’ma need more real prayers and less praying hands", "vault_lines": ["I'ma need more real prayers and less praying hands"], "meaning": "Wayne is referencing how people often offer “thoughts and prayers” in a passive way, which could be seen as pretending to care about someone. Wayne is possibly referring to his biggest artist and “No New Friends” co-star, Drake. There may be underlying tension between them, considering the two were at the Michael Rubin 2024 Fourth of July party and shared no interactions. Wayne continues in the second half with a reference to Drake’s popular praying hands logo and stated he needs less of those.", "annotation_id": "32463000", "url": "https://genius.com/32463000", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "Never wore a Jheri curl like Mike Jack in the 80s", "vault_lines": ["Never wore a Jheri curl like Mike Jack in the 80s"], "meaning": "A Jerry curl, commonly spelled as “Jheri curl”, was a perm hairstyle popular in the late 80’s that consisted of tight curls and a glossy look.\n\nThe haircut was famously worn by American pop singer Michael Jackson, often nicknamed Mike Jack.", "annotation_id": "32460316", "url": "https://genius.com/32460316", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "(Now put your hands in the sky if you feel the vibe) Yeah", "vault_lines": ["I rеally got my own sneaker, used to shop at Sears"], "meaning": "This is an interpolation from Kanye West’s “Diamonds From Sierra Leone,” where he says:\n\nThrow your diamonds in the sky if you feel the vibe\n\nhttps://m.youtube.com/watch?v=92FCRmggNqQ\n\nCordae has noted Ye as one of his favorite artists previously, and even joins with Ye on the following Crossroads track, “No Bad News.”", "annotation_id": "32459829", "url": "https://genius.com/32459829", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "You standing on business, I’m a businessman", "vault_lines": ["You standing on business, I'm a businessman"], "meaning": "“standing on business” is a term that means to take responsibility. Wayne compares himself to a businessman, who you can’t take down, and would have to take responsibility for trying to.\n\nThis could also very well be a reference to the famous Jay-Z lyric (who Cordae references earlier in the song) on Kanye’s Diamonds from Sierra Leone (Remix).\n\n“I’m not a businessman, I’m a business, man”", "annotation_id": "32459804", "url": "https://genius.com/32459804", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "I wear this mean mug like a face mask", "vault_lines": ["I wear this mean mug like a face mask"], "meaning": "Mean mugging is when you stare at someone intimidatingly. Some people naturally have this look without trying.\n\n\n\nLil Wayne describes having this constant expression, comparing it to a face mask, which is typically a creamy mask used for skin care.", "annotation_id": "32459754", "url": "https://genius.com/32459754", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "I get more camel toe than them Arabs", "vault_lines": ["I get more camel toe than them Arabs"], "meaning": "Camel toe is slang for the visible outline of a woman’s labia (genitilia) through tight clothing.\n\nCamels are desert mammals often associated with Arab cultures.", "annotation_id": "32459745", "url": "https://genius.com/32459745", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "Like the eighth man on the Wizards, that’s more of a match for me", "vault_lines": ["Like the eighth man on the Wizards, that's more of a match for me"], "meaning": "Cordae keeps with the themes of humble bragging, likening his income to that of the eighth-most payed player on the Washington Wizards' payroll. In recent history, the Wizards have been looked at as a “mediocre” franchise, clinching their 44th consecutive season with less than 50 wins in 2024. They only adopted the Wizards' name 18 years into the streak of mediocrity.\n\nPoor play aside, the Washington Wizards play at in the NBA, highest level of basketball in the world, and have had many household names suit up for them in their history that received hefty contracts.  At the time of the song’s release, the eighth-most payed player by the Wizards was NBA veteran, Jonas Valančiūnas. Jonas was set to make $9.9M USD for the 2024-25 NBA season.", "annotation_id": "32459307", "url": "https://genius.com/32459307", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "Acting wild like a Black & Mild, we smoked the opponent", "vault_lines": ["Acting wild like a Black & Mild, we smoked the opponent"], "meaning": "Black & Mild is a type of tobacco cigar made by Luca Black Co that is plastic-tipped, rather than wood-tipped. The iconic East Coast cigar reportedly smells like green apples.\n\nThis is relevant because Cordae is referencing his East Coast origins, being from North Carolina himself, and is reflecting about his younger days when he wasn’t a famous rapper.", "annotation_id": "32455628", "url": "https://genius.com/32455628", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "Elvis has left the building, went to Graceland", "vault_lines": ["Elvis has left the building, went to Graceland"], "meaning": "“Elvis has left the building” is a phrase used by announcers after singer Elvis Presley would finish performing to get the audience to either leave the venue or be quiet for the next act.\n\nGraceland is Elvis' mansion located in Memphis, Tennessee.", "annotation_id": "32455169", "url": "https://genius.com/32455169", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "I really got my own sneaker, used to shop at Sears", "vault_lines": ["I rеally got my own sneaker, used to shop at Sears"], "meaning": "Cordae launched his own shoes with Puma, with it being a version of the classic Puma suede. Cordae is amazed at how far he’s come, from shopping at a cheap retail store like Sears, into having his own sneaker.", "annotation_id": "32454514", "url": "https://genius.com/32454514", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "Rap niggas lying through their teeth, though, these are not veneers", "vault_lines": ["Rap niggas lying through their teeth, though, these are not veneers"], "meaning": "Veneers are a thin layer of porcelain put on top of someone’s teeth, meant to cover up any chipped tooth or stain. When Cordae says this, he illustrates how some rappers are “lying through their teeth,“  as veneers are part of your teeth.", "annotation_id": "32454475", "url": "https://genius.com/32454475", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "And if it ain’t a red flag, then it’s a red flag", "vault_lines": ["And if it ain't a red flag, then it's a red flag"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne reps the Bloods, a famous West Coast gang that can be discerned by its red colors. Red flags are commonly used to show respect to the gang.\n\nRed flags could also refer to a visible bad quality about a person, so Lil Wayne is saying that, if someone does not support the Bloods, then they are bad news.", "annotation_id": "32453307", "url": "https://genius.com/32453307", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "Real love, real love, Mary J. playin’", "vault_lines": ["Real love, real love, Mary J. playin'"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is referencing “Real Love”, a 1992 song by Mary J. Blige. She is often labeled as the Queen of Hip-Hop Soul.", "annotation_id": "32453247", "url": "https://genius.com/32453247", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "Skipping through the airport, but soon will hop on Lears", "vault_lines": ["Skipping through the airport, but soon will hop on Lears"], "meaning": "Bombardier Challenger 600, sometimes referred to as LearStar 600 (or shortened to just “Lears”), is a series of business jets. Developed by Canadair, a manufacturer owned by Bombardier Aerospace, LearStar 600’s original design was created by Bill Lear.\n\nLearStar 600 jets are known for flying high priority people, such as the Canadian prime minister.", "annotation_id": "32452914", "url": "https://genius.com/32452914", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "‘Cause where I’m from, they catchin’ bullets like the best receivers", "vault_lines": ["'Cause where I'm from, they catchin' bullets like the best receivers"], "meaning": "A “bullet” is common American football jargon for a fast pass. It typically takes quite a talented player to catch these throws.\n\nCordae is using wordplay here because “catchin' bullets” could also mean to get shot. What he is conveying with this bar is that people in his hometown get shot often.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/aLMHRtD7_rQ", "annotation_id": "32452873", "url": "https://genius.com/32452873", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "Clayton Bigsby, y’all done took a nigga out of the hood", "vault_lines": ["Clayton Bigsby, y'all done took a nigga out of the hood"], "meaning": "In an old sketch from Dave Chappelle’s iconic show “Chappelle’s Show”, Chappelle plays a blind man named Clayton Bigsby who becomes a white supremacist because he doesn’t know that he himself is black.\n\nBigsby becomes a famous white supremacist author and KKK leader who always keeps his face behind a Klan hood. At the end of the skit, he takes off his hood to an audience of his racist fans and unknowingly reveals his true skin color.\n\nHere, Cordae uses wordplay to compare getting out of ‘the hood’, the poor area where he grew up, to Bigsby finally taking off a different kind of hood.", "annotation_id": "32452751", "url": "https://genius.com/32452751", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "Feeling like Rod Wave, I got a lot on my plate And I can really use a stress reliever", "vault_lines": ["Feeling like Rod Wave, I got a lot on my plate"], "meaning": "so fucking funny", "annotation_id": "32452629", "url": "https://genius.com/32452629", "state": "pending", "votes": 131, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "She give me real love with that fake ass It fit me like a glove, OJ hands", "vault_lines": ["She give me real love with that fake ass"], "meaning": "This line references the famous OJ Simpson trial, in which the former American football running back was accused of murdering his ex-wife and her friend.\n\nDuring the trial, OJ Simpson was asked to try on a pair of gloves (one of which was found near the scene of the crime, while the other—covered in blood—was discovered at Simpson’s estate).\n\nLil Wayne is comparing OJ wearing the gloves to sticking his hand up the fake ass of a certain woman, while using the common saying “fitting like a glove”.", "annotation_id": "32452620", "url": "https://genius.com/32452620", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Saturday Mornings", "line": "I love Jim Carrey, but don’t fuck with yes-men I need honest ears when in the studio", "vault_lines": ["I love Jim Carrey, but don't fuck with yes-men"], "meaning": "While Cordae is in the studio recording music, he needs honest feedback from his team rather than “yes-men” who say his music is good no matter what. He references the 2008 comedy movie Yes Man starring Jim Carrey. In the film, he says “Yes” to everything that is asked of him.", "annotation_id": "32451893", "url": "https://genius.com/32451893", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
