{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Guns turn you boys into pussies, sex change", "vault_lines": ["Guns turn you boys into pussies, sex change"], "meaning": "Wayne brings some wordplay here:\n“Guns turn you boys into pussies” means the boys are afraid of the guns or turn into wusses. This is similar to the boys turning into girls (literally), hence “sex change.”", "annotation_id": "39658965", "url": "https://genius.com/39658965", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I hit the strip club, and all them bitches find a pole", "vault_lines": ["I hit the strip club, and all them bitches find a pole"], "meaning": "It seems when he comes to the club the women start dancing to attract him.", "annotation_id": "39658942", "url": "https://genius.com/39658942", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Don’t like my women single, I like my chicks in two’s", "vault_lines": ["Don't like my women single, I like my chicks in two's"], "meaning": "Another example of paraprosdokian: the first part of the line suggests Drake likes taken/married women; however, the second part changes the meaning with him not liking one woman alone (he prefers women in twos).", "annotation_id": "39658927", "url": "https://genius.com/39658927", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "We walk the same path, but got on different shoes Live in the same building, but we got different views", "vault_lines": ["Kane is in the building"], "meaning": "Drake suggests that while he exists in the same reality, he can [presumably] afford better kicks and housing (maybe he’s got the penthouse).", "annotation_id": "39658906", "url": "https://genius.com/39658906", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I got a couple cars, I never get to use", "vault_lines": ["I got a couple cars, I never get to use"], "meaning": "Drake employs paraprosdokian, where the start of the line gives the listener the impression that Drake is bragging about having a couple cars. The end of the line flexes much harder, suggesting that Drake’s got a fleet so large that he has cars he doesn’t even get to use.", "annotation_id": "39658870", "url": "https://genius.com/39658870", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Damn where you stumbled at? From where they make Gumbo at", "vault_lines": ["Damn where you stumbled at? From where they make Gumbo at"], "meaning": "Where did Lil Wayne come from? Well, he is from New Orleans, Louisiana, the official hometown of Gumbo, a popular cajun soup dish.", "annotation_id": "20729872", "url": "https://genius.com/20729872", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Motherfuckin’ Birdman Junior, 11th Grade", "vault_lines": ["Motherfuckin' Birdman Junior, 11th Grade"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne used to frequently go by the nickname Birdman Jr, paying homage to his Cash Money label boss and father figure Birdman.\n\nWhen you’re in 11th grade you are called a “junior”", "annotation_id": "20729855", "url": "https://genius.com/20729855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Skinny pants and some Vans", "vault_lines": ["Skinny pants and some Vans"], "meaning": "This was Wayne’s go-to fit in these days, with this album being released in 2010. During this time he was transitioning his persona from gangster to a more rockstar skateboarder type.\n\nSkinny ass pants, fresh pair of Vans\n\nYou can even see him rocking skinny jeans and Vans sneakers on the cover of this album.", "annotation_id": "20729847", "url": "https://genius.com/20729847", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Yeah, and I ain’t a killer but don’t push me", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and I ain't a killer but don't push me"], "meaning": "Likely a reference to the first line in the first verse of Tupac’s “Hail Mary”, lauded as one of the best lines in hip hop history.", "annotation_id": "18664292", "url": "https://genius.com/18664292", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "We on, it’s Young Money motherfucker", "vault_lines": ["We on, it's Young Money motherfucker"], "meaning": "Young Money Entertainment is an American record label founded by Lil Wayne. As of this song’s release, Wayne and Drake were both signed to the label.", "annotation_id": "13203792", "url": "https://genius.com/13203792", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Uh, wake up and smell the pussy", "vault_lines": ["Uh, wake up and smell the pussy"], "meaning": "A play on the phrase “wake up and smell the coffee” which means to become aware of the realities of a situation, however unpleasant. This ties in to the previous line where he plays on the phrase “Life is a bitch”.\n\nOftentimes, the first thing people smell in the morning is coffee. Since Wayne has sex first thing in the morning, the first thing he smells is a girl’s pussy.", "annotation_id": "12970859", "url": "https://genius.com/12970859", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "[Produced by Kane Beatz] [Intro] Kane is in the building", "vault_lines": ["Kane is in the building"], "meaning": "Watch Kane Beatz recreate the trumpets here:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/0smX2t4fojU?t=150", "annotation_id": "10074798", "url": "https://genius.com/10074798", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Yes I’m in the building, you just on the list of guest names", "vault_lines": ["Yes I'm in the building, you just on the list of guest names"], "meaning": "Like Elvis: known for causing panics when he entered public places (hence the catchphrase “Elvis has left the building!”)\n\n\n\nAlso referring to the producer, Kane Beatz, who’s label is called “the building”, at the beginning of a song done by Kane you’ll hear his tag: “Kane is in the building nigga” (or something of the sort).\n\nThe term “in the building” is slang that means that a person has arrived or has achieved. It came from the older slang term “in the house” and progressed to “in the building”", "annotation_id": "63040", "url": "https://genius.com/63040", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "[Verse 1: Drake]", "vault_lines": ["Kane is in the building"], "meaning": "This verse is recycled from the beginning of his Tim Westwood “freestyle,” with a few changes (and a few mishaps).\n\nIn 2015, most of Drake’s verse was used in the opening theme to the HBO series Ballers.", "annotation_id": "44828", "url": "https://genius.com/44828", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I’m with Marley G’s bro", "vault_lines": ["I'm with Marley G's bro"], "meaning": "He’s using ‘Marley G’s Bro\" as a euphemism for Lil’ Wayne.\n\nMarley G is Lil' Wayne’s right hand man (and/or goon) – he does Weezy’s dirty work, Lil Wayne calms Marley on several songs, saying “Marley don’t shoot ‘em”\n\nDrake is asserting that he can ‘fuck with Hollywood Cole’ since he’s with Wayne and Marley G. Hear that, J. Cole?", "annotation_id": "31182", "url": "https://genius.com/31182", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "And you niggas know I rep my gang like Jessie James", "vault_lines": ["And you niggas know I rep my gang like Jessie James"], "meaning": "The outlaw, not the car-customizer", "annotation_id": "31148", "url": "https://genius.com/31148", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "President Carter, Young Money Democrat", "vault_lines": ["President Carter, Young Money Democrat"], "meaning": "Wayne’s real name is Dwayne Carter and he’s the President/founder of Young Money Records. Wayne stretches this idea out to a whole song with “President Carter”\n\nPolitically-speaking, he’s a Democrat (just like the REAL “President Carter”)\n\n\n\nDisclaimer: It seems that Wayne is no longer actually President but, as Young Money’s CEO, has appointed Mack Maine to fill this position.", "annotation_id": "30512", "url": "https://genius.com/30512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Fake friends write the wrong answers on the mirror for me", "vault_lines": ["Fake friends write the wrong answers on the mirror for me"], "meaning": "In Slumdog Millionaire, the game show host tries to trick the hero by writing an incorrect answer to the question the hero faces on a bathroom mirror\n\nDrake is saying that he lost touch with his real friends and is surrounded by backstabbers who are trying to cause his downfall (just like the game show host)\n\n\n\nDouble meaning:\nWhen you look in the mirror you see you. So his fake friends told him to do the wrong things to himself.", "annotation_id": "29656", "url": "https://genius.com/29656", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Who else is really trying to fuck with Hollywood Cole", "vault_lines": ["Who else is really trying to fuck with Hollywood Cole"], "meaning": "Many people interpreted this as a shot at J. Cole, but it is not. Both Cole and Drake are using this line from “SpottieOttieDopealiscous”.\n\nThe only reason Cole used it is because the pronunciation of Hollywood COURTS by André 3000 sounded like Hollywood Cole.\n\nDrake is known to idolize 3 Stacks; perhaps this is an homage.", "annotation_id": "29648", "url": "https://genius.com/29648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Flying Holly Grove chicks to my Hollywood shows", "vault_lines": ["Flying Holly Grove chicks to my Hollywood shows"], "meaning": "Drake’s rich enough to fly multiple girls from Lil Wayne’s neighborhood all the way out to Hollywood. Lil Wayne would later play on Hollygrove / Hollywood on 2015’s “HollyWeezy”:\n\nToo Hollygrove to go Hollywood\n\nIn the freestyle version, Drake says “treating Birmingham just like my Hollywood shows” instead.", "annotation_id": "29513", "url": "https://genius.com/29513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "This that Slum Dog Millionaire Bollywood flow and uh", "vault_lines": ["This that Slum Dog Millionaire Bollywood flow and uh"], "meaning": "Bollywood is the “Hollywood” of India—they make commercial movies for a mass audience\n\n\n\nThe hit movie Slumdog Millionare, which was set in India, tells the tale of rags-to-riches Indian ballers (similar to Drake’s overnight rise to stardom). Interestingly enough, Slumdog Millionaire is not technically a Bollywood film. It was directed by Danny Boyle, a British director.", "annotation_id": "29134", "url": "https://genius.com/29134", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Limping off tour, ’cause I made more off my second leg", "vault_lines": ["Limping off tour, 'cause I made more off my second leg"], "meaning": "The second “leg” (half) of his concert made more money: if you load more weight on one leg than the other, you develop a limp\n\nWeezy and Drake have taken some tumbles on stage, so there might be a bit of play here as well", "annotation_id": "27681", "url": "https://genius.com/27681", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "Hip-Hop, I’m the heart of that", "vault_lines": ["Hip-Hop, I'm the heart of that"], "meaning": "In homage to Huey Lewis' awful song “The Heart of Rock & Roll”.\n\nWayne personifies hip-hop and claims that he’s the beating heart of the organism; the center of and the leader of hip-hop as a genre and also what’s keeping it alive.\n\n\n\nIf Rap was a human or living being, Wayne would be the heart pumping blood or, for the sake of the personification, music through the veins or minds of the listeners.", "annotation_id": "27664", "url": "https://genius.com/27664", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I can hand it to Drake or do a quarterback draw Wildcat offense, check the paw prints", "vault_lines": ["I can hand it to Drake or do a quarterback draw"], "meaning": "In football’s Wildcat offense, the player who takes the snap normally has a few options: run the ball himself, hand it off, or throw a pass. Either Wayne or Drake can handle the beat and put together some nice rhymes.\n\nDrake has also labeled himself the “Young Money running back,” so this is good continuity from Weezy. He later compared Drake to a famous athlete: remember Drake Griffin?", "annotation_id": "27658", "url": "https://genius.com/27658", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "line": "I been flyin’ so long I fell asleep on the fucking plane", "vault_lines": ["I been flyin' so long I fell asleep on the fucking plane"], "meaning": "To be fly is to have swagger and style. At the same time he uses it to make people see that he’s been “up there” for so long that to “fly” on a plane would be boring, making him fall asleep while other would be greatfull to be “Fly” or be “flying”.\n\nWayne loves to talk about being bored of being the best: on the “Can’t Tell Me Nothing” freestyle he says “I’m so tired of balling excuse me if I yawn”", "annotation_id": "27617", "url": "https://genius.com/27617", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Right Above It", "status": "done", "targets": 25, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-wayne-right-above-it-lyrics", "genius_title": "Right Above It", "genius_id": 896, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ridin’ With the AK", "line": "“Let me use your throwaway, ’cause niggas out here playin’, man”", "vault_lines": ["\"Let me use your throwaway, 'cause niggas out here playin', man\""], "meaning": "By throwaway he means a gun that was probably illegally bought off the streets and they’ll either toss or sell again after using it to commit a crime.", "annotation_id": "40954583", "url": "https://genius.com/40954583", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ridin’ With the AK", "line": "Niggas thought I lost all my blickas in that Katrina flood", "vault_lines": ["Niggas thought I lost all my blickas in that Katrina flood"], "meaning": "Blickas is street slang for handguns. In this line, Currensy is referencing Hurricane Katrina, the 2005 disaster that devastated his hometown of New Orleans and left the city submerged.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7unlPvNxLLA\n\nHe is addressing a common assumption that the floodwaters destroyed his stash of weapons or forced him to start over.", "annotation_id": "39738373", "url": "https://genius.com/39738373", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ridin’ With the AK", "line": "I still got Big U MAC-11 from ’97", "vault_lines": ["I still got Big U MAC-11 from '97"], "meaning": "Vintage Firepower: The Big U Legacy\n\nCurren$y uses Big U to represent the peak street danger of 1997. By linking his  Ingram MAC 11 to that specific year, he’s saying his weapon carries the same lethal reputation as the legendary gang leader did before/during his prison stint and pivot to philanthropy.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zt1uqiXVytw\n\nIt is a flex of longevity, proving he has kept the same “high stakes” threat level since his first day in the game.", "annotation_id": "39738298", "url": "https://genius.com/39738298", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ridin’ With the AK", "line": "I’m the president: My job here is done (Ugh)", "vault_lines": ["I'm the god, just looking for a nun; I'm the president: My job here is done (Ugh)"], "meaning": "The Cash Money President\n\nIn 2005, Birdman officially appointed Lil Wayne as the President of Cash Money Records. This wasn’t just an honorary “street” title; it meant Wayne had a seat at the head of the boardroom table and was tasked with overseeing the label’s operations and scouting new talent (which eventually led to the birth of Young Money).\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yXnmXC1RADY", "annotation_id": "39738198", "url": "https://genius.com/39738198", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ridin’ With the AK", "line": "I’m the Birdman’s son", "vault_lines": ["I'm hot, bitch—word to the Sun; I'm the Birdman's son"], "meaning": "Wayne has (or had) always looked at Birdman like a Father, which explains the tracks “Stuntin Like My Daddy ft. Birdman and Like Father Like Son which is a collab album with Birdman", "annotation_id": "20202312", "url": "https://genius.com/20202312", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ridin’ With the AK", "line": "[Intro: Lil Wayne, Curren$y, & DJ tags] I—I—I—I—I be on it, on it all night—man, I be on it all day Straight up, boy, if you want me (Gangsta Gri-Zillz!) I be riding with that K, K (Yuh), K, K (Huh) K, K, K (Spitta), K (Yuh)", "vault_lines": ["I—I—I—I be on that gangster shit, straight up on that gangster shit"], "meaning": "A direct reference to Big Boi’s song “I’m On It (Kryptonite)”. In Wayne’s version instead of telling you he stays in Atlanta, Wayne let’s you know he rides with an AK.", "annotation_id": "2562710", "url": "https://genius.com/2562710", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ridin’ With the AK", "line": "More workers than Mary Kay; vibing to that Mary J", "vault_lines": ["More workers than Mary Kay; vibing to that Mary J"], "meaning": "Mary Kay is a very large cosmetics company which has a lot of employees, though he claims his crew has more “workers” working for them.\n\n“Mary J” is slang for marijuana, for which Mack, Wayne and Curren$y are big fans\n\n\n\nVibin' to that Mary J. also refers to him listening to Mary J. Blige’s music.", "annotation_id": "2285767", "url": "https://genius.com/2285767", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ridin’ With the AK", "line": "And you don’t need to know your alphabets to recognize a G", "vault_lines": ["And you don't need to know your alphabets to recognize a G"], "meaning": "“G” stands for gangster, which Curren$y claims he is. You don’t need to know your full alphabet from A-Z to recognize that he is a gangster.", "annotation_id": "2285762", "url": "https://genius.com/2285762", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Ridin’ With the AK", "line": "I’m from the Crescent City,", "vault_lines": ["I'm from the Crescent City,"], "meaning": "The Crescent City is a nickname for his and Wayne’s hometown of New Orleans, Louisiana. It got its nickname from the crescent shape of the land along a bend of the Mississippi River", "annotation_id": "2285757", "url": "https://genius.com/2285757", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
