{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "You mad, doggie? You mad, doggie?", "vault_lines": ["You mad, doggie? You mad, doggie?"], "meaning": "This is a phrase coined by rapper Cam'ron when he made his appearance on The O'Reilly Factor in 2003.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zPjUsu2-QMQ\n\nIn the past, Cam'ron and Nicki Minaj have collaborated on I Am Your Leader and So Bad.", "annotation_id": "15105794", "url": "https://genius.com/15105794", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "Rich who? Got bricks, too The rich get richer, that’s my ritual", "vault_lines": ["Rich who? Got bricks, too"], "meaning": "In the first line, “brick” is a slang term for money, particularly a bundled or shrink-wrapped amount of money, usually in amounts of $1,000 or $10,000. The second line plays with the sound of the word “ritual” as equal or similar to “rich rule.”\n\n\n\nNicki states that she messes with rich guys not as a way to climb up to their level, since she’s already there with an estimated net worth of $70 million, but for both of them to increase their cash flow together.", "annotation_id": "14779441", "url": "https://genius.com/14779441", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "Rich crew, link my bitch, too Mack took the Wraith, me and Tune flew", "vault_lines": ["Rich crew, link my bitch, too"], "meaning": "Nicki references Mack Maine, the President of her record label, Young Money Entertainment, originally founded by Lil Wayne. A Wraith is a luxury car made by Rolls Royce which retails for upwards of $320,000.\n\nUnlike Mack, Nicki and Lil Wayne (Tunechi) most likely took a private plane. In 2017, Nicki took to  Instagram to show off her private jet. As seen even in her 2012 song, “Roman Reloaded,” Nicki is all about flying private:\n\nWhen I flew to the set, though, I ain’t fly commercial", "annotation_id": "14774802", "url": "https://genius.com/14774802", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "Go to DR, get that fat transfer", "vault_lines": ["Go to DR, get that fat transfer"], "meaning": "“Fat transfer” refers to liposuction, a surgical procedure in which fat from one part of the body (belly, thighs, etc.) is removed to another part (butt, breasts, etc.). Nicki is insinuating that you’ve got to do what you’ve got to do to bag a rich guy.\n\nWhile “DR” is a small play on doctors' prefix abbreviations Dr., it also refers to the Dominican Republic, where the surgery can be cheaper than in the US, albeit more risky.", "annotation_id": "14773305", "url": "https://genius.com/14773305", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "Queen, where we going again? To the moon, Alice, the goon palace", "vault_lines": ["Queen, where we going again?"], "meaning": "Reference to a catchphrase from the 1955 sitcom The Honeymooners, in which Ralph threatens to send his wife, Alice, to the moon with a punch in the mouth:\n\n\n\nThis might also be a reference to the next collaboration between the two, Lil Wayne’s song “Dark Side of the Moon” (featuring Nicki Minaj) from his highly anticipated album Tha Carter V which came out three months after this song.", "annotation_id": "14771458", "url": "https://genius.com/14771458", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "Facts, all my bitches have no limits Fuck her in a helicopter, now she screamin’, “Sky’s the limit” Fuck her in a drop top, now she screamin’, “Sky’s the limit”", "vault_lines": ["Facts, all my bitches have no limits"], "meaning": "This could be a reference to one of Weezy’s classic tracks, “Sky’s The Limit” (or “Ride For My Niggas”):\n\nDon’t worry about mine, I'mma grind ‘til I get it\nAnd tell all of my niggas that the sky is the limit\n\n“Drop top” refers to a convertible, which is a car with fabric roof that can be folded down. Having sex in either this (where you can see the sky) or a helicopter (where you’re actually in the sky) would literally have her screaming “Sky’s the limit!”\n\nWayne’s women are willing to have sex with him anywhere, they have no limits!", "annotation_id": "14769825", "url": "https://genius.com/14769825", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "Lil mama said she only fuckin’ on a rich dick I cum in her face and tell her, “Now you lookin’ rich, bitch”", "vault_lines": ["Lil mama said she only fuckin' on a rich dick"], "meaning": "Classic Wayne makes a pun on the idiom “put your money where your mouth is” after just referencing his penis as being that of a wealthy man’s, so receiving a blowjob would be metaphorically putting his money where her mouth is like it’s lipstick. Subsequently, the “money” from his ‘rich dick’ would be left on her face, making her look “rich.” Get it?\n\nLipstick is also similar to lip gloss, which is coincidentally the name of rapper Lil Mama’s hit song, someone Wayne may be unintentionally name-dropping in these lines.", "annotation_id": "14766993", "url": "https://genius.com/14766993", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "Who gon’ Rico, Ace me, pay in full my money, Mitch nigga?", "vault_lines": ["Who gon' Rico, Ace me, pay in full my money, Mitch nigga?"], "meaning": "In the 2002 film Paid in Full, the main characters Rico and Ace aren’t paid what they are due by the character Mitch, thus spawning the term “Mitch nigga,” which plays off the common insult “bitch nigga.”\n\nNicki also referenced the film on London On Da Track’s 2017 song “No Flag”:\n\nBitches is finito, I call my car Rico\n‘Cause it’s paid in full, baby, my car don’t get repo’d", "annotation_id": "14766266", "url": "https://genius.com/14766266", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "(Real rich nigga sex)", "vault_lines": ["If you let that broke nigga fuck, we tellin' (Rich sex)"], "meaning": "Ain’t no such thing as an ugly billionaire, I’m cute\n\nThe theme of the song insinuates that only wealthy men are good-looking and deserving of sex, relationships, or both with worthy women, and if a woman has sex with a man who’s not making “bands” (thousands of dollars), she’ll be made fun of. This can go both ways, implying that if you’re wealthy, you can’t be ugly.\n\nNicki has had her history with wealthy partners, including big names like Nas and Meek Mill, both of which have garnered a net worth in the millions.", "annotation_id": "14765925", "url": "https://genius.com/14765925", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "If you know your pussy worth a Benz truck", "vault_lines": ["If you know your pussy worth a Benz truck"], "meaning": "Mercedes-Benz is a very well-known and luxurious car brand. Mercedes has a famous line of expensive SUV’s that can cost over $150k.\n\nIn this line, Nicki states that you can’t be in a relationship with her if you are not ready to put your money on it as having sex with her is expensive, compared to a Mercedes-Benz.\n\nNicki has previously appeared in a Benz commercial and she just previously referenced the car in the first Queen single “Chun-Li”:\n\nPlates say Chun-Li, drop the Benz off", "annotation_id": "14765352", "url": "https://genius.com/14765352", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "Got him callin’ nonstop ’cause he don’t wanna miss this I said, “Don’t panic, keep the faith, nigga, B.I.G.’s bitch”", "vault_lines": ["Got him callin' nonstop 'cause he don't wanna miss this"], "meaning": "This line is reminiscent of one from “Chun-Li,” the album’s lead single:\n\nHe keep on dialin' Nicki like the Prince song\n\nR&B singer Faith Evans, whose 1998 album was named Keep the Faith, is the widow of the late rapper The Notorious B.I.G, AKA Biggie Smalls. Nicki also references B.I.G. on “Ganja Burn,” as well as putting her own spin on his track “Just Playing (Dreams)” with “Barbie Dreams.”\n\nNicki’s potential suitor in this case would be calling her frequently likely to make sure she won’t leave him; to which Nicki reassures that there’s no reason to panic. The prospect of Nicki’s lover missing her is also touched upon on “Majesty.”\n\nNicki took to Twitter to confirm the lyrics:\nhttps://twitter.com/NICKIMINAJ/status/1006208677383061505", "annotation_id": "14765218", "url": "https://genius.com/14765218", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "Full blown Run rich, Brinx Yo (Mula) Yo (Yeah) Ayo", "vault_lines": ["I'ma help him fuck the check up, I'ma run the business"], "meaning": "The intro of the song features the two main artists Nicki and Wayne as well as Brinx Billions, a New York-based rapper who has been signed to Nicki’s currently unnamed label back in 2012.\n\n\n\nBrinx has previously collaborated with Nicki on the tracks “I Endorse These Strippers” and “Mind on My Money.” He teased his involvement in “Rich Sex” days prior to its release on his Instagram account by reposting Nicki’s snippet.", "annotation_id": "14765043", "url": "https://genius.com/14765043", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Rich Sex", "line": "He say, “Baby, every day we ballin’,” I say, “Swish, swish”", "vault_lines": ["He say, \"Baby, every day we ballin',\" I say, \"Swish, swish\""], "meaning": "Nicki references “Swish Swish”, her May 2017 collaboration with Katy Perry for her album Witness. The track’s music video sees the two playing basketball.\n\nNicki continues the play on words for basketball such as “ballin'” and “swish” to represent the song’s theme of richness.\n\nIn 2018, Minaj featured on Post Malone’s “Ball for Me” from the beerbongs & bentleys album.", "annotation_id": "14765025", "url": "https://genius.com/14765025", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
