{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Project Chick [Radio Edit]", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Cash-money-millionaires-project-chick-radio-edit-lyrics", "genius_title": "Project Chick [Radio Edit]", "genius_id": 1592381, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Project Bitch", "line": "To the ladies havin’ babies without no drama To my niggas with figgas say “I love my baby mama”", "vault_lines": ["To the ladies havin' babies without no drama"], "meaning": "In this song Mannie Fresh and the other Cash Money Millionaires summarize the male fantasy of wealth, power, and women who throw themselves at them, while minimizing concerns that their actions or their focus has any negative impact on the women and children that they primarily objectify. The most interesting couplet in the rap due to various interpretations is  “ladies havin’ babies without no drama / To my niggas with figgas say ‘I love my baby mama.’” This and other themes are explored.\n\nMannie Fresh’s first verse, when understood as an oral persuasive essay, uses the technique of first addressing detractors. A listener that may not appreciate the overall tone may conclude, incorrectly in Mannie’s view, that the rappers are exploiting these women. But Mannie Fresh in this rhetorical device makes a case for greater respect for women than the rest of the song suggests. While the women in the song are considered primarily on their sexual promiscuity, Mannie here seeks to let us know that he knows them by name and wants to shout out who they are: “Deshawn, Eshlawn, Lil Mama….” He repeats that he loves them for emphasis: “I love y'all broads.” He loves them so much that he reassures his “dawgs” that he means “no disrespect.” Then he evaluates if there were a coerced relationship then there would be “drama,” but the women here are having babies “without no drama.” For those financially well-off, the Cash Money Millionaires, or similar men, note that they “love my baby mama.” Mannie further explains that he provides for them financially in ways that exceeds what a typical baby’s daddy would provide: paying for tattoos and breast implants. It’s worth noting that Juvenile, a member of the Cash Money Millionaires who raps later in the song, was arrested in 2017 for failing to pay $170,000 in child support.\n\nIn an entire song describing sexual acts and what women will give to him sexually Mannie Fresh notes that the relationship if it were untoward would result in “drama.” At an initial glance, “ladies havin' babies without no drama” seems to express some degree of women’s liberation and that the women have a choice in this matter. Perhaps Mannie supposes that a well-supported, independent woman may choose to have a child and with financial freedom and social supports can expect to raise the child and not worry about her financial well-being for what may have been an unplanned pregnancy. In addition, Mannie Fresh wants us to know that he is not forcing himself sexually on these women—often it’s assumed that if women are in a coerced sexual relationship they would voice their concerns, but this has been shown in multiple analyses to be a flawed interpretation. Mannie Fresh does not consider the relationship any further and is satisfied with his conclusion that if the women did not want to have sex with him, and in some cases, bare his children then they would not do so.\n\nAnalysis of sexuality in late twentieth century New Orleans in this song mirrors nineteenth-century French authors like Balzac, Stendhal, and Flaubert who extensively explored the intersections of sexuality and the French bourgeoisie in their novels. The authors of these novels have been criticized for minimizing the agency and consideration of structures of power that make it hard for women to truly choose. Mannie Fresh makes a similar half-considered conclusion that ladies are “without no drama.”\nThere is a coincidental through-line from Old World France to New World France in New Orleans, Louisiana. The Cash Money Millionaires are a group based out of New Orleans, USA. There has been much analysis of extra-marital sex in French culture particularly when the initial release of Madame Bovary by Gustave Flaubert was blocked by censors and the controversy propelled sales of the book; likewise, 1990s rap in USA resulted in legal and moral concerns that it would corrupt listeners and bring the downfall of traditional family structure. Broader still, in male-dominated societies, and particularly those that value “rugged individualism” it is a nearly universal condition that men have more money and power, while women, when described by men, are subservient and their needs and wants are minimized. History repeats itself.\nWhile the French authors concluded that open sexuality may cause society break down, the Cash Money Millionaires are not concerned that these relationships will cause problems for society. Their tone is positive, playful, and boastful.\nMannie may not share as whole-heartedly with the others in his celebration of a male fantasy that objectifies women. In many cases, a double negative is used to suggest that there is something other than the quality that is being described—Mannie Fresh states “without no drama.” A lack of “drama” does not mean there are no misgivings or concerns about the nature of these sexual and romantic relationships. In the next line, Mannie Fresh may actually be requesting that “niggas with figgas” to say “I love my baby mama.” Perhaps Mannie Fresh is asking others to regard women more fully than the consideration that they are typically afforded. Other analysts of Mannie Fresh note that he tends to focus on positive relationships, so it’s possible that Mannie Fresh is conceding that while the rest of the song will describe sexual acts women provide to the men that the focus should be on love and lasting connection, rather than lust or power.\n\nIt’s worth considering that the entire song is likely self-aware that they are of low socioeconomic status, but similar relationships exist in high socioeconomic status relationships. The rappers describe their relationship with women with enthusiasm and playful regard. Powerful and rich men, some of the wealthiest in the world, also describe their relationship with women in matter-of-fact ways that when regarding the women demonstrates their second-class status. It’s hard not to consider a harem of women serving a plutocrat, or sheik in the same way that the Cash Money Millionaires describe what sounds like a constant flow of mostly unnamed women.\nIs the relationship between the Cash Money Millionaires really that different or more objectifying than men from more “respectable society,” or those that live in France or Saudi Arabia, for example, or who lived 150 to 200 years ago?", "annotation_id": "36526165", "url": "https://genius.com/36526165", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
