{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Problems", "line": "Hello? Hello?", "vault_lines": ["Check your home screen, that's God callin’ (Hello?)"], "meaning": "This is a continuation of the line “every time I thought I was home it was only the matrix.”\nContinuing with not feeling comfortable in what is to be his home, he alluded to the matrix again. In the movie, to get out of the matrix, you had to answer a telephone called by someone from he real world.\nWayne emphasizes the fact that he is not home by his continuous “hello? Hello?”", "annotation_id": "15972037", "url": "https://genius.com/15972037", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Problems", "line": "I should have a tattoo that say, “I am nothin’ like my dad”", "vault_lines": ["I should have a tattoo that say, \"I am nothin' like my dad\""], "meaning": "Lil Wayne chose to “drop the D” from his birth name “Dwayne” as his dad had the same name and he left Weezy’s family when Lil Wayne was very young.", "annotation_id": "15586255", "url": "https://genius.com/15586255", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Problems", "line": "Amen, feeling like Our Father Should I get off my high horse to get on my flyin’ horses?", "vault_lines": ["Amen, feeling like Our Father"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne’s saying he feels like God, then admits he might be on his “high horse”, in other words, he’s feeling superior and overconfident.\nBy “flyin' horses”, Lil Wayne’s referring to Pegasus, a flying horse in Greek mythology.", "annotation_id": "15548615", "url": "https://genius.com/15548615", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Problems", "line": "I live all alone, no house telephone, talk to you later I’m never home, one time I thought I was home and it was only the matrix", "vault_lines": ["I'm never wrong, one time I thought I was wrong and I was only mistaken"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne likely references trust issues with people around them, whether this be people from his label, friends or even women, as a result of being as wealthy and successful as he is. He lives “all alone” and has no place to consider “home” because there’s always the possibility that he’s being used, cheated, taken advantage of, etc.\n\n\n\nTo reinforce this idea, he references the iconic sci-fi movie franchise The Matrix, which revolves around humanity being trapped in a fake reality that is actually a virtual simulation system created by machines.\n\nHowever, by saying “no house telephone, talk to you later,” Weezy probably reasserts control of his own life and career saying that at the end of each day, it’s him who makes the calls and calls the shots.", "annotation_id": "15502358", "url": "https://genius.com/15502358", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Problems", "line": "I’m never wrong, one time I thought I was wrong and I was only mistaken", "vault_lines": ["I'm never wrong, one time I thought I was wrong and I was only mistaken"], "meaning": "Wayne uses the ambiguity of “mistaken” to say he is misunderstood (has been mistaken by others), while making a play on the fact that to be mistaken generally means to be wrong (to have mistaken oneself), making the line ostensibly contradictory. This is somewhat similar to his line on “Blunt Blowin'”:\n\nThey say we learn from mistakes; why, that’s why they mistake me", "annotation_id": "15494292", "url": "https://genius.com/15494292", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Problems", "line": "Blunt thick, thick as Mama June off the diet plan", "vault_lines": ["Blunt thick, thick as Mama June off the diet plan"], "meaning": "“Mama June” is the mother of Honey Boo Boo. Mama June also appeared on “From Not to Hot” in 2017, where she went from 460 to 160 pounds as a result of intense dieting. Her transformation is the inspiration for Lil Wayne’s simile to compare her to a blunt.", "annotation_id": "15488677", "url": "https://genius.com/15488677", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Problems", "line": "Just got them new Cartier, the Rollie was too salty", "vault_lines": ["Just got them new Cartier, the Rollie was too salty"], "meaning": "Cartier is a French jewelry company that sells watches, bracelets, bags, rings and necklaces. The items usually sell from anywhere from $1,000 to $150,000.\nAlso, when he says the Rollie was too salty. He is saying that his Rolex got jealous after he bought the Cartier.", "annotation_id": "15488620", "url": "https://genius.com/15488620", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Problems", "line": "If lovin’ me is wrong, I don’t wanna be right, damn, bitch, you hatin’", "vault_lines": ["If lovin' me is wrong, I don’t wanna be right, damn, bitch, you hatin'"], "meaning": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FvJj7SN9EWI\n\nWeezy plays with the phrase “If loving you is wrong, I don’t want to be right” made famous by Luther Ingram by flipping it to be about himself.", "annotation_id": "15481325", "url": "https://genius.com/15481325", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
