{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Perfect Strangers", "line": "Perfect strangers", "vault_lines": ["I don't know who you are anymore"], "meaning": "This song’s premise is the dissipation of love in a long-term relationship. The couple’s love  fades to the point where they become “strangers” to each other despite  their earlier belief that they were “perfect” together.\n\nThus, they are “perfect strangers.”\n\n\nI don’t know who you are anymore​…\n\nYou don’t even call anymore…\n\nWe don’t even talk anymore…\n\nWe don’t make love anymore…", "annotation_id": "39376427", "url": "https://genius.com/39376427", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Perfect Strangers", "line": "She tell me I’m a cheap thrill I know she don’t mean that She think I feed her lies But I don’t get no feedback", "vault_lines": ["But I'm here, and she here"], "meaning": "She tells me I’m a cheap thrill; I know she don’t mean that.\n\nCalling someone a “cheap thrill” is a derogatory term for a person, often implying interactions with them are brief and lack emotional depth.\n\nShe think I feed her lies, but I don’t get no feedback.\n\nEven though she believes that Wayne is constantly lying to her, their relationship is so damaged that she doesn’t care, so she doesn’t give any feedback to his lies.\n\nUnderlying this is a clever play on the word “feed,” as Wayne “feed[s]” something to her, but she does not “feed” it “back” to him. This further illustrates the disconnect in their relationship.", "annotation_id": "37374850", "url": "https://genius.com/37374850", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Perfect Strangers", "line": "Don’t sweat your alarm", "vault_lines": ["You say \"Don't sweat your alarm"], "meaning": "She says “don’t sweat your alarm” because she does not want him to set an alarm, hoping that he stays with her for as much time as possible.", "annotation_id": "33187336", "url": "https://genius.com/33187336", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Perfect Strangers", "line": "She hate that we can get loose But I can never hang tight", "vault_lines": ["She hate that we can get loose"], "meaning": "Wayne’s girlfriend (“she”) is upset that Wayne seems always willing to “get loose,” but not to “hang tight.” This is susceptible to at least two meanings (depending on which phrase we interpret first).\n\nMeaning 1: To “get loose” means to relax and take things easy. In this context, “hanging tight” is the negative form of “get loose,” and it means doing all of the serious, necessary things to make a relationship work and be healthy.\n\nMeaning 2: To “hang tight” means to wait patiently. In this context, to “get loose” is the negative form of “hang tight,” and it might mean several things, such as igniting arguments, engaging in intercourse, or consuming drugs/alcohol—all of which Wayne struggles to resist in the face of strong temptations.", "annotation_id": "33187317", "url": "https://genius.com/33187317", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
