{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "You fuckin’ with P.A. so you know what it is (What it is)", "vault_lines": ["You fuckin' with P.A. so you know what it is (What it is)"], "meaning": "Bun shouts out Port Arthur, Texas; both he and his late friend Pimp C’s hometown.", "annotation_id": "16781191", "url": "https://genius.com/16781191", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "That nigga that inspired Lyrical tyrants like Kanye West and Em", "vault_lines": ["That nigga that inspired"], "meaning": "Eminem listed Nas as the 8th greatest MC of all time in “‘Till I Collapse”. Em listed himself as 9th.\n\nKanye also referred to Nas as one of the greatest of all time in this interview while discussing Nas and Jay-Z’s beef. https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=RFJePPFb4pk", "annotation_id": "12363252", "url": "https://genius.com/12363252", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "[Produced by Willy Will]", "vault_lines": ["When I step in the spot, motherfuckers say \"Who that?\""], "meaning": "This is the same beat as the intro and interlude, check out the breakdown of the beat here!", "annotation_id": "2787888", "url": "https://genius.com/2787888", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "I gotta make it what they want and wake ’em when I come And shake ’em and bake ’em and take ’em to another place Ain’t no fakin’, ain’t no kind of mistaking how I be breakin’ up everything And be creating a s-s-s-situation when I’m done (Damn!)", "vault_lines": ["I gotta make it what they want and wake 'em when I come"], "meaning": "Busta Rhymes’s talking about how he feels as if he needs to adjust his raps to appeal to the masses, as that would be the only way he thinks he can achieve true success in the industry.\n\nOn the “wake ‘em when I come” bit, he’s referring to the fact that such a feat as for him to create music that actually does appeal to a mass audience would wake the world up to him, the fact that he exists, and the fact that they need to pay more attention to such a rapper as him.\n\n“And shake ‘em and bake 'em” is referring to how shocked that the world would be that he managed to pull off the feat mentioned in the previous paragraph.\n\n“and take ‘em to another place” is referring to that once he has the world in his hands, after appealing to all of them, he’s going to get them going on following him as if they were to be following such artists as Lil Wayne, and he’ll show them just what he has to offer the hip hop community.\n\n“Ain’t no fakin'” is referring to how nobody else in the rap game can come even close to the style he has when it comes to music.\n\nAnd lastly, “Ain’t no kind of mistakin' how I be breakin' up everything and be creating a s-s-s-situation when I’m done (DAMN!)” is referring to how original his music is, and attempting to even match the style he’s created for his music won’t do anything except create an unneccessary, unwinnable situation against you and the Busta himself.\n\nOr, he could simply be rapping lots of verbs together to show off how quickly he can ‘bust a rhyme’.", "annotation_id": "1309242", "url": "https://genius.com/1309242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Call the judges, call the coppers We takin’ over Gotham", "vault_lines": ["Call the judges, call the coppers"], "meaning": "Gotham is a slang term for New York City. So just warn everyone that Shyne and his niggas are taking over the city!", "annotation_id": "614850", "url": "https://genius.com/614850", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "I’m a king in this thing, don’t be dumb", "vault_lines": ["I'm a king in this thing, don't be dumb"], "meaning": "Nas has always been a believer that there ain’t no best rapper, but a lot of great rappers, which is why he says “a king” and not “the king.”", "annotation_id": "413132", "url": "https://genius.com/413132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Chowed down, now my food’s digested", "vault_lines": ["Chowed down, now my food's digested"], "meaning": "His brain has “chowed down” and “digested”,  metaphorically speaking. In other words, he took in all the knowledge he observed and  now it’s resonated with him.\n\nNote the reference back to the lines above referring to digestion.", "annotation_id": "351798", "url": "https://genius.com/351798", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "But ya Nas advocates actors Seem to get typecast in the same role", "vault_lines": ["But ya Nas advocates actors"], "meaning": "Saying rappers don’t show complexity and typically stick to one ‘'persona’‘ or ’‘role’‘.  then wonder why they don’t get taken serious as an artist when they’ve only offered ’‘one layer’‘ to their music.\n\nBeing “typecast” is a common problem for actors who play the same role in each movie. Specifically “comedic actors”.\n\nDon’t believe me? Ask this guy.", "annotation_id": "351740", "url": "https://genius.com/351740", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Gut ’em like a gastric bypass", "vault_lines": ["Gut 'em like a gastric bypass"], "meaning": "Nas ‘'guts’‘ his competition like gastric bypass, a single surgery or a group of similar operations that first divides the stomach into a small upper pouch and a much larger lower “remnant” pouch and then re-arranges the small intestine to allow both pouches to stay connected to it.\n\nDon’t worry Nas, Randy Jackson got the reference.", "annotation_id": "351724", "url": "https://genius.com/351724", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Cash Money is the company And Weezy the boss!", "vault_lines": ["Cash Money is the company"], "meaning": "A reference to Wayne’s line in Stuntin' Like My Daddy:\n\nWhat they doing? Hating on me, but they never cross\nCash Money still the company and bitch I’m the boss", "annotation_id": "350511", "url": "https://genius.com/350511", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "But, yo, my nigga Wayne Let them niggas hate, ’cause like my nigga Drake say “We ain’t got time to respond to niggas”", "vault_lines": ["But, yo, my nigga Wayne"], "meaning": "People have been saying Wayne is falling off, but Nas is telling him to let them think what they want and ignore them. Like Drake once said on an interview, “You can say all you want about me, I will never respond cuz I don’t care.”\n\nDrake has specified in numerous songs that he wont respond to haters:\n\n\n“Diss me, you’ll never hear a reply for it” – Successful\n\n“This one is for all my niggas from my city tryin' to diss, Without a response from me you really fail to exist” – 9AM in Dallas", "annotation_id": "340340", "url": "https://genius.com/340340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "What the fuck you niggas still hangin’ around here for? You apostrophe, bitch?", "vault_lines": ["What the fuck you niggas still hangin' around here for?"], "meaning": "Apostrophes are a grammatical punctuation used in the English language. They tend to hang in the air unlike other punctuation’s like commas, full-stops & question mars. So Busta is question why these fraud rappers are still lingering around in the rap game similar to that of an apostrophe does in mid-air\n\nThere’s more: in classical Greek drama, an “apostrophe” was a speech or poem addressed directly to an absent person; thus Busta’s line here, directed at a lame rapper he is dismissing, is also itself a form of apostrophe.", "annotation_id": "340334", "url": "https://genius.com/340334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Most of you niggas sorry And owe apologies quick", "vault_lines": ["Most of you niggas sorry"], "meaning": "A play on “sorry” as both “pitiful” and “regretful”. The same idea is at play in Malik B.’s “you and your sorry-ass style need an apology” from The Roots' “It Just Don’t Stop”", "annotation_id": "340333", "url": "https://genius.com/340333", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Pow-pow, with my shooters are Techs That’ll bust louder than the noise that I just spit", "vault_lines": ["Pow-pow, with my shooters are Techs"], "meaning": "“Techs” refer to the “TEC-9” submachine gun. He is stating that his TEC-9’s are even louder than his already “loud” rap. (He’s being facetious).\n\n\n\nThis could also be a shout out to Tech N9ne, a fellow rapper who appeared on the interlude of this album", "annotation_id": "340008", "url": "https://genius.com/340008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "We gettin’ to the money like it’s Goldman Sachs", "vault_lines": ["We gettin' to the money like it's Goldman Sachs"], "meaning": "The world’s most successful investment bank; many critics argue that Goldman’s success is predicated as much on its ruthless exploitation of its political connections (ex-CEOs include Jon Corzine and Henry Paulson) as its financial savvy. Indeed, Bun B may be using this example to advertise that he makes his money illegally, since Goldman’s investing practices were subject to investigation by the SEC.\n\nRolling Stone journalist Matt Taibbi famously called the company “a great vampire squid wrapped around the face of humanity.”", "annotation_id": "338145", "url": "https://genius.com/338145", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Tats on my body like an art exhibit", "vault_lines": ["Tats on my body like an art exhibit"], "meaning": "The quantity and caliber of Nas’s tattoos are reminiscent of an art museum, or at least a gallery show.\n\n\n\nNas also referred to his body as an art gallery in the song “Every Ghetto”", "annotation_id": "338141", "url": "https://genius.com/338141", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Reppin’ QB ’til the death of him", "vault_lines": ["Reppin' QB 'til the death of him"], "meaning": "Nas is from Queensbridge, NYC, so he’s repping his hood.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AemsM0dUyCs", "annotation_id": "338139", "url": "https://genius.com/338139", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Flatbush to Bed Do-or-Die From Watts to Larry Hoover, Chi’", "vault_lines": ["Flatbush to Bed Do-or-Die"], "meaning": "Flatbush and Bedford-Stuyvesant (also known as Do Or Die Bed-Stuy) are neighborhoods in Brooklyn, NY.\n\nWatts is a neighborhood in South Central LA, California.\n\nLarry Hoover was the leader of the Chicago Gangster Disciples.\n\n\n\nThe woes of street life are the same all over the US.", "annotation_id": "337495", "url": "https://genius.com/337495", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Been in this shit since ’91", "vault_lines": ["Been in this shit since '91"], "meaning": "A reference to Biggie’s double-time verse on “Notorious Thugs” where he says “Been in this shit since 92”. Nas got his buzz off the Main Source track “Live At The BBQ” which dropped in 91'\n\nhttp://youtu.be/S-zndpG0eS8", "annotation_id": "337347", "url": "https://genius.com/337347", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Chop heads off like King Henry the Eighth Guillotine to your neck, bitch", "vault_lines": ["Chop heads off like King Henry the Eighth"], "meaning": "Henry VIII, King Of England was famous for having six wives, two of whom were beheaded.\n\nHowever neither were beheaded with guillotines, as they didn’t come into use until 1792.", "annotation_id": "336844", "url": "https://genius.com/336844", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Since sixteen, I ain’t grow a day old Yet my brain grow Cocaine white Range Rov’", "vault_lines": ["Since sixteen, I ain't grow a day old"], "meaning": "Nas hasn’t grown up since he started rapping at 16. But his mind has expanded, he’s become more wise and got a bright white Range Rover, a “prestige” vehicle not often seen in the hood.\n\nThis chicken came home to roost when it was revealed that Nas’s teenage daughter had a Mercedes she nicknamed “Cocaine.” Nas expressed displeasure at the revelation.", "annotation_id": "336838", "url": "https://genius.com/336838", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Your fate is sealed, no Heidi Klum", "vault_lines": ["Your fate is sealed, no Heidi Klum"], "meaning": "A play on words. Supermodel Heidi Klum was married to the singer Seal at the time this song was released; their 2012 divorce makes this line even more apt.\n\n\n\nThis pic most certainly gets my ‘seal’ of approval", "annotation_id": "336828", "url": "https://genius.com/336828", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Track record goes back to the Essence", "vault_lines": ["Track record goes back to the Essence"], "meaning": "Aside from the obvious meaning of the phrase “back to the essence”, The Essence is also a 2002 track by AZ featuring Nas. It was nominated for a Grammy for Best Rap Performance By A Duo or Group.", "annotation_id": "336811", "url": "https://genius.com/336811", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "No tricks, man, those is for kids", "vault_lines": ["No tricks, man, those is for kids"], "meaning": "Trix breakfast cereal’s slogan was “Silly Rabbit, Trix are for kids!”", "annotation_id": "336803", "url": "https://genius.com/336803", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Wayne", "song": "Outro", "line": "Live from the state where they chop it and screw that", "vault_lines": ["Live from the state where they chop it and screw that"], "meaning": "Texas (really he’s referring to the greater Houston area), home of “Screw music”, made famous by the legendary DJ Screw.", "annotation_id": "336800", "url": "https://genius.com/336800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
